For more help, type 'help <topic>' for any command, skill, or spell.
Type 'help KEYWORD' to see a list of all of the available topics.
Also help on: DAMAGE DYING EXPERIENCE NEWS STORY TICK WIZLIST GUILDS
If you are new here, we recommend that you read BEGINNERS.

The COMMANDS topic shows only the commands that your current trust level
allows you to use.  Mortals and heroes will see only mortal commands here.
Immortal-only commands are documented under WIZHELP and will not appear to
non-immortals.

See also: COMMANDS, KEYWORDS, BEGINNERS
~

0 MAP MAPPER AUTOMAP AUTOMAPPER MAPPING~
The automatic client-side map is a window in the Mudlet interface (HELP
MUDLET) that follows you as you move.  The
browser client and plain Telnet do not currently show it.  MAPS lists the
maps you hold (HELP MAPS).

  - The map shows what your character knows: every room you have walked
    into, every room of an area whose map you hold, and all of Dresden and
    the starting areas.  The rest stays hidden until you find it.  A room
    you visit is corrected from what the game reports: an exit that was
    removed or points somewhere new is fixed there.
  - Secret exits are not sent until you find them.  Room descriptions,
    EXITS and SEARCH remain the authority.
  - Portals, ropes and the Hyrule cabinet are known too: clicking a room
    beyond one walks you through it.
  - Doors show as open, closed or locked, and a visited room shows what
    it offers:  $ shop  ? questmaster  + healer  P practice  T trainer
                & pet shop  # jail  X death trap (marked once it kills you)
  - It shows one level at a time: the level you are on.  Stairs up and
    down are marks inside a room; take them and the map shows that level.
    TOCGUI LEVELS also shows, faintly, the levels above and below.
  - Each Hyrule dungeon is a map of its own, laid out like its dungeon
    map, with its cellars a level below.
  - Symbols and doors appear only when zoomed in far enough to draw the
    room boxes.  The map shows no people: LOOK does that.
  - The map is kept in your Mudlet profile, not your character.

Click a room to walk there (HELP WALK).

See also: MUDLET, WALK, WALKTO, EXITS, SEARCH, MAPS
~

0 MUDLET TOCGUI~
Mudlet is the recommended desktop client.  Make a profile for
timesofchaos.com, port 9000, and leave "Enable GMCP" and "Allow server to
install script packages" on: the game installs its interface and world map
and offers updates by itself.

Mudlet commands (local aliases; they do nothing in other clients):
  tocgui status          package version, GMCP state and these commands
  tocgui mapcheck        which map and floor you are on, the floors
                         setting, and squares or exits drawn wrong near you
  tocgui levels on|off   draw the floors above and below yours, or not
                         (alone it toggles; status says what it is)
  tocgui atlas           what loading the world map does; it REPLACES this
                         profile's map, so TOCGUI ATLAS BACKUP saves yours
                         first and TOCGUI ATLAS CONFIRM saves it, then loads
  tocgui off | on        hide or restore the interface
  walk ...               walk to a room or place (HELP WALK)

Drawing the floors above and below is Mudlet's own setting, shared by all
your profiles.  The package hides them once, tells you it did and what it
was before, and never changes it again after your own TOCGUI LEVELS.  The
strip over the map names the map and the floor you are on.  An exit button
opens a closed door before stepping through it, as a walk does.

If the interface says GMCP is waiting, enable GMCP and server packages in
the profile and reconnect.  Package files: github.com/jeremydbean/toc2026,
folder mudlet.

See also: 'MUDLET INTERFACE', MAP, WALK, SOUND, GMCP, WEBCLIENT
~

0 'MUDLET INTERFACE'~
What the Mudlet interface shows:

  Gauges    hit points, mana, endurance and experience
  Identity  your name, level, race and class
  Affects   what is on you, soonest to lapse first; five ticks or less
            turns orange
  Target    who you fight, how hurt they are, and your damage per round
            in this fight or the last
  Quest     your quest, its timer and where the target was last seen
  Online    the players you could see
  Map       the world, following you (HELP MAP)
  Exits     a button for each way out
  Tabs      Chat, Tells, Here (the room) and Items; the chat shows only
            what you heard, in your COLOR choices
  Awards    an achievement, shown for a few seconds as you earn it

It also plays short sounds (HELP SOUND), shows your character and area in
Discord when Mudlet's Discord option is on (not while wizinvis), and logs
in by itself with a name and password saved in the profile, checked
exactly as if typed.

See also: MUDLET, MAP, SOUND, GMCP
~

0 SOUND~
Syntax: sound
Syntax: sound on
Syntax: sound off

A client that plays a game's sounds -- Mudlet, with "Allow server to play
media" left on in the profile -- hears a short sound when you gain a level,
finish a quest, earn an achievement, receive a tell, die, or find a secret
in Hyrule.  SOUND alone says whether they are on and whether your client
has said it plays them.  SOUND OFF silences them for this character;
SOUND ON brings them back.  Clients that play no sounds are unaffected.

See also: MUDLET, GMCP
~

0 WALK CLICKTOWALK~
Syntax: walk <vnum>       walk to a room by number
        walk <name>       walk to a destination, such as WALK THALOS
        walk routes       load the published route list
        walk              report on the walk in progress
        walk stop         stop

WALK is a Mudlet alias, not a game command; clicking a room on the map
does the same.  For a walker that works in any client, use WALKTO.

It takes one step at a time and sends the next only once you arrive where
the route expected, so lag, a mobile in the way or a recall stops it.  It
opens a door it knows is shut, and tries OPEN once at a door it did not
expect.  A named walk needs the place on your own map; otherwise it prints
the published route, which starts at the Oak Tree Square.

See also: WALKTO, MUDLET, MAP, RUN
~

0 ROUTES DESTINATIONS TRAVEL~
Almost every area has a published walking route from the Oak Tree Square,
with the commands to follow and how many rooms out it is.  Dresden has
none: the square is in it.

  In game         WALKTO lists the areas and walks you there.
  Mudlet          WALK ROUTES, then WALK <name> (HELP WALK).
  Browser client  the Routes panel.
  Web dashboard   the Directions view.

A route is ordinary walking: it opens doors on the way and passes whatever
lives there, so a distant area is no safe trip for a low level character.
A room that carries you, such as a river or a teleport, counts as several
rooms, since you cannot steer it.  Secret doors are in no route.

See also: WALKTO, WALK, MAP, AREAS, RECALL
~

0 DUMMY TRAINING YARD~
Syntax: dummy                  the menu, and what it is set to
        kill dummy             start a run
        dummy report           stop early and read the numbers
        dummy reset            back to the standard, ranked fight
        dummy leaderboard      the benchmark board
        dummy <level>          what level it pretends to be
        dummy shape|hits|magic|haste|rounds|endless   see DUMMY SETTINGS
        dummy defends yes|no   whether it dodges, parries and blocks
        dummy fights yes|no    whether it hits you back

A training dummy you can hit as hard as you like.  The yard lies under the
Oak Tree Square: from its centre, down, south.  North walks you back out.
In Mudlet, WALK DUMMY takes you there.

  - One fighter at a time; anyone else is told to wait.
  - Nobody dies there: the dummy absorbs everything, and its blows stop
    at one hit point.
  - When a run ends, or you leave mid-run, your hit points, mana and moves
    go back to what they were when it began.  It never heals you.
  - Skills and spells do not improve in the yard.

THE STANDARD FIGHT - THE ONLY ONE THAT IS RANKED
Twenty-five rounds and a bell, against a dummy at your level that defends
itself and hits back.  Change anything, or stop early, and the run is not
ranked; the menu, each change and the bell say so.  When the yard empties
the dummy goes back to standard.

See also: 'DUMMY SETTINGS', 'DUMMY REPORT', LEADERBOARD, COMPARE
~

0 'DUMMY SETTINGS'~
Syntax: dummy shape <name>     soft, armored, evasive, brutal
        dummy hits <type>      slash, pierce, bash, fire, cold, lightning,
                               acid, energy, holy
        dummy magic <kind>     none, force, fire, cold, lightning, acid,
                               light, harm, negative
        dummy haste            an extra attack a round, on or off
        dummy rounds <n>       n rounds, then the bell
        dummy endless          no bell; DUMMY REPORT ends the run
        dummy defends yes|no   whether it dodges, parries and blocks
        dummy fights yes|no    whether it hits you back, spells included

A weapon's damage type tests a resistance; a spell of the same school
tests a saving throw too.  It casts the same spell every time.

DUMMY DEFENDS NO and DUMMY FIGHTS NO measure your damage alone; your own
defences still work.  No setting can change mid-run except DUMMY RESET,
which ends the run.  None of these runs is ranked.

See also: DUMMY, 'DUMMY REPORT', LEADERBOARD
~

0 'DUMMY REPORT'~
Syntax: dummy report

Ends a run early and shows its numbers; the bell shows the same.  The
clock starts at the first blow either way.

  WHAT YOU DID     total, per second, per swing and per round (compare
                   runs on per round), swings landed, best and average
                   hit, what missed, and weapon against spells and skills
  Attack by attack each spell, special attack and your weapon (second and
                   third attack included in it): damage, share, landed,
                   average and best
  WHAT IT DID      its attempts, what you ducked, parried, dodged or
                   blocked, what got through, your worst hit, and how long
                   you would last from full
  After a ranked run: your place on the board and your personal best.

See also: DUMMY, LEADERBOARD, DAMAGENUMBERS
~

0 LEADERBOARD BENCHMARK~
Syntax: dummy leaderboard

The training yard's board: each character's best standard run, best first.
It shows the top ten and where you stand, with class and guild as WHO
shows them (T/W is a thief among the warriors).

ONLY THE STANDARD FIGHT IS RANKED: twenty-five rounds against a dummy at
your level that defends itself and hits back, every setting as DUMMY RESET
leaves it, run to the bell.  Not ranked:

  - a longer, shorter or endless fight (DUMMY ROUNDS, DUMMY ENDLESS)
  - DUMMY DEFENDS NO, DUMMY FIGHTS NO, or any other change
  - stopping early with DUMMY REPORT
  - a run during which somebody changed the dummy
  - an immortal's run (a trusted player is ranked)

A ranked run tells you your place and whether it is a personal best.
Posting the best run of all earns the achievement Top of the Board.  To
run it: DUMMY RESET in the yard, then KILL DUMMY.

See also: DUMMY, ACHIEVEMENTS
~

0 GMCP~
GMCP is the out-of-band protocol modern clients use; it adds nothing to
normal Telnet text, and clients without it work as before.  A client that
turns it on receives:

  Char.Vitals       hit points, mana, movement, experience and level
  Char.Status       name, level, race and class
  Char.Affects      what is on you, with durations
  Char.Quest        your quest, its timer, streak and last sighting
  Char.Target       your foe's health and your damage per round
  Char.Online       who is playing, as far as you could see
  Char.Items        what you carry and wear
  Char.Achievement  an achievement as you earn it
  Char.Login        a login with the name and password the client keeps
  Room.Info         the room, its visible exits and doors, flags, and what
                    it offers (shop, questmaster, healer, trainer ...)
  Room.Chars        who and what is in the room
  Comm.Channel      each line of talk you actually hear
  Client.Map, Client.GUI   where the world map and Mudlet package are
  Client.Media      short sounds (SOUND OFF stops them)
  External.Discord.Status  your character and area, for Discord

Core.Ping is answered.  Secret exits are never sent.

See also: MUDLET, MAP, SOUND
~

0 WEBCLIENT BROWSERCLIENT BROWSER~
The first-party browser client provides ANSI color, masked password entry,
scrollback, command history, reconnect support, aliases, quick actions,
semicolon command chains, and transcript download.  It connects through the
same private web service as the administration dashboard.

On a local installation use:
  http://127.0.0.1:9001/client

On the hosted game use:
  https://timesofchaos.com/client

The browser client does not currently include Mudlet's automatic mapper.

See also: WEB, MUDLET, MAP, ALIAS
~

0 ENTER CROSS~
Syntax: enter <portal>
        cross <portal>

Steps into a portal in the room: a magic gate, a cabinet, a raft, a window
or anything else the room shows as one.  There is no default; name it.

  - You must be standing.  Not every object can be entered.
  - Some portals charge (the Hall of Heroes windows take 500 gold) or
    need an item carried; you are told which.
  - A private room, a guild you may not enter, or Hyrule's order on the
    far side turns you back before anything is paid.

Example: enter portal, or at Hyrule's ledges and rivers, cross gap

See also: CLIMB, CRAWL, JUMP, LOOK
~

0 CASTLE CASTLES~
Syntax: castle
        castle <message>      (also CC or -)

Castle chat reaches every logged-in member of your castle.  CASTLE alone
turns the channel off or on.  CGOS talks to every castle at once.

Castles are guilds of player killers.  A castle head adds a member by
standing in the castle's joining room and typing JOIN <player>; the
player must be there, level 30 or more and a PKILLer.

See also: CGOS, CHANNELS, JOIN, PKILL
~

0 IGNORE~
Syntax: ignore
Syntax: ignore <player>
Syntax: ignore clear

IGNORE stops you hearing one player.  IGNORE <player> adds them to your
list, and the same again takes them off; IGNORE alone shows the list, and
IGNORE CLEAR empties it.  They need not be in the game, and the list is
kept when you quit.  You may ignore up to 20 players.

What an ignored player says, emotes or poses beside you, their tells and
replies, their group tells and everything they put on a channel (gossip,
question, answer, music, shout, yell, castle, hero ...) no longer reach
you, and their lines drop out of your HISTORY.  Their notes leave your
board too, so NOTE LIST numbers only the notes you can read.  Their
tells and replies are answered "<your name> is ignoring you."; nothing
else tells them.

It does not stop what they do: their blows, spells, socials and arrivals
still reach you.  The staff cannot be ignored.  To stop hearing a whole
channel, type its name on its own (GOSSIP ...); DEAF stops every tell.
Harassment breaks the RULES; tell the immortals with a NOTE.

See also: DEAF, QUIET, CHANNELS, HISTORY, RULES
~

0 REPAIR~
Syntax: repair <object>
        repair all
        repair quote <object> | all

A smith mends an item you wear or carry back to full condition.  REPAIR
ALL mends everything that needs it, cheapest first, as far as your purse
goes.  REPAIR QUOTE, anywhere and free, shows what a repair would cost
and restore; QUOTE ALL lists the pieces and how many your purse covers.

  - The price follows the damage and the item's level: a dent is cheap,
    a piece broken past use about four kills' worth at its level.
  - Nothing is destroyed by damage or by repair.  A broken piece must be
    mended before it can be worn again, and always can be.
  - Armour filed down by blows, heat or acid is put back as it was made.
  - Repair never changes what an item sells for; a shop pays a third for
    a broken piece.

Only some shopkeepers repair: look for smiths in guild halls, markets and
weapon or armour shops.

See also: VALUE, SELL
~

0 SECONDARY 'DUAL WIELD' DUAL~
Syntax: secondary <weapon>

Wields a second weapon in your off hand, the slot a shield would use; a
shield there comes off.  You need the DUAL WIELD skill and a main weapon
already wielded, and the second must be a weapon you could wield, light
enough for your strength.  Neither weapon may be two-handed.  A mortal
cannot dual wield an enchanted weapon, or one with a hit or damage bonus
above 5.  REMOVE takes it off again; removing your main weapon leaves it
where it is.

Each round the off hand gets one more chance to strike, your dual wield
skill plus that weapon's skill, divided by five -- at most 40%.  That blow
skips the to-hit roll; the foe's dodge, parry and block still apply.

Usable from level: Thief 17; never Mage, Cleric, Warrior, Monk, Necromancer.
Trainer: Mizry the assassin.  GAINLIST shows who sells it to your class and
guild.

See also: WIELD, REMOVE, SECOND ATTACK
~


0 SHADOWMELD~
Syntax: shadowmeld

Draws the shadows around you.  While melded no creature can see you, and
no player can either unless they have holy sight (staff holylight, or the
Triforce worn).  It does not wear off with time.

It ends when you:
  - leave the room, however it happens (walking, recall, a portal)
  - strike anyone; the blow is struck from the shadows
  - type VISIBLE, or SHADOWMELD again
  - are caught in FAERIE FOG

You may sit, sleep or read while melded.  You cannot start it in a fight,
and a missed roll says so.  Melded, no aggressive creature will attack
you, so it is the safe way to step away from the keyboard; players with
holy sight still see you.

It improves with use.  Nobody teaches it: the fourth remort grants it at
50%, and no later remort takes it away.

See also: STEALTH, HIDE, VISIBLE, REMORT
~

0 HISTORY REPLAY~
Syntax: history                  the last 20 lines, every channel
        history <number>         that many instead
        history <channel> [num]  one channel only
        history tell             your own tells
        history list             what is held, and how much

Reads back what was said on the channels you can hear, oldest first, with
the time of each.  A line '--- while you were away ---' marks the moment
you last left.

  - Channels kept: gossip, shout, yell, question, answer, music, leveling,
    and hero, immtalk and godtalk for those who can hear them.
  - Each channel keeps its last 30 lines; your tells, the last 20.
  - Tells are yours alone; nobody else can read them.
  - SAY is never kept.
  - It is memory only: a reboot clears it.

Example: history gossip 5

See also: CHANNELS, GOSSIP, TELL
~

0 STASH~
Syntax: stash                       what is in it, and the next price
        stash put <item> | all      from anywhere
        stash get <item or number>  at the altar only
        stash buy                   more room, at the altar
        stash link <character>      share with another of yours

Storage for items you do not want to carry around.  It survives reboots
and is kept at the Temple altar, one step west of where RECALL lands you.

  - Items go in from anywhere and come out only at the altar.
  - Room is counted in items, not weight: a bag of gems is one.
  - You start with 50 slots.  Each STASH BUY adds 25, to 500, for 250,
    then 1,000, then 2,250 gold and rising; STASH shows the next price.
  - Anything on a timer, and corpses, are refused.
  - The stash is saved the moment anything goes in or out.

Example: stash put sword

See also: STASHLINK, DONATE, RECALL
~

0 STASHLINK LINK~
Syntax: stash link <character>         offer, or accept an offer
        stash unlink <character>       stop sharing
        stash of <character>           list theirs, from anywhere
        stash take <character> <item>  at the altar only
        stash deny <character>         refuse an offer
        stash block <character>        refuse, and stop them asking
        stash unblock <character>      let them ask again

Two of your characters can share their stashes.  Each must name the other:
STASH LINK from the first, then the same from the second.  Until both
have, nothing is shared, so nobody can reach your stash uninvited.  You
are told at login when someone has named you.

  - The other character need not be logged in.
  - A take saves both files; if either save fails the item goes back.
  - Nothing can be taken while either of you has a death still being
    recorded; both stashes can still be looked at.
  - UNLINK ends your half at once; every item stays where it is.
  - You may keep up to eight links.  STASH BLOCK alone lists who you block.

See also: STASH
~

0 BUFF HERMIE~
Syntax: buff               her menu
        buff <spell|group> ask for one
        heal <spell|group> the same, wherever she stands

Hermie is the free healer: she casts anything on her menu for nothing.
She appears when the staff place her, and by herself after a restart that
put players off the game: then she stands in the Oak Tree Square for four
hours, whenever any player is on.

Ask by command or simply SAY the name to her.  Groups: DEFENSE, DETECTS,
TRAVEL, CURES, RESTORE, COMBAT, or ALL.  HEAL, MANA, REFRESH and VITALS
fill your pools outright.  EMPOWER is her bundle; she no longer gives
TITANIC.  Her buffs last thirty ticks.

Example: buff defense

See also: HEAL, EMPOWER
~

0 ACCOUNT ACCOUNTS LOGIN~
There are no accounts here.  Each character is its own login: its name
and its password.  Make as many characters as you like.

PASSWORD changes the password of the character you are playing; every
letter of a new one counts.  A forgotten password cannot be mailed to
you: ask an immortal, who can reset it.  DELETE removes the character you
are playing, for good.

See also: PASSWORD, DELETE, WHO
~

62 REPORTS~
Syntax: reports
        reports <bugs|typos|ideas> [count]
        reports clear <bugs|typos|ideas>

Reads what players filed with BUG, TYPO and IDEA.  Staff only (trust 62).

REPORTS alone counts each kind and how much is new since you last looked.
Naming a kind shows the newest, twenty unless you give a count, and marks
them read; you are told at login when more arrive.

REPORTS CLEAR sets that file aside under a dated name; nothing is deleted.

Many lines are not reports: BUG typed with the next command on one line
files the command ("wear all").

See also: BUG, NOTE
~

65 MIRROR~
Syntax: mirror <player>
        mirror restore
        mirror clear

Takes off your gear and puts on fresh copies of what a player is wearing,
so you can study their setup from the inside.  Staff only (trust 65);
you must be standing.  Every use is logged.

  - Works online or offline (from their last save); it says which.
  - Copies come from each item's prototype: their enchantments and wear
    are not reproduced.  Alignment and two-hander rules are ignored, but
    an action item is left out, and you are told.
  - Your own gear goes to your inventory, never destroyed.

MIRROR RESTORE packs the copies into "<player>'s gear" and puts your own
gear back on.  MIRROR CLEAR takes everything off and forgets the mirror;
so does logging out.

See also: INVULN, FINGER, STAT
~

62 INVULN INVULNERABLE~
Syntax: invuln
        invuln damage | absorb | off

Staff toggle (trust 62): while it is on nothing takes hit points off you
-- no mobile, player, spell or shield -- and your equipment takes no
damage.  INVULN alone turns it on, and again turns it off.

  DAMAGE   the default: blows read as normal, numbers and all, and you
           lose nothing.  Use it to watch a fight behave.
  ABSORB   blows read as against immunity ("Zog is unaffected by your
           slash!").
  OFF      turns it off.

Nobody else is shown it, SCORE does not list it, and every use is logged.
It protects your own body only, not a mobile you have switched into.

See also: HOLYLIGHT, WIZINVIS, CLOAK
~

0 DIARY~
Syntax: diary
        diary yesterday
        diary <yyyy-mm-dd>
        diary list

Your diary writes down each day you play, by itself: how long you played,
the levels and experience you gained, your kills (bosses and guardians by
name), your deaths and close calls, the quests and dailies you finished
with their points, the achievements you earned, the skills you improved,
the areas you went through and how long you spent in each, the things you
came by, and your coin -- what you earned, where from, and what you spent
it on.

DIARY shows today, DIARY YESTERDAY the day before, and DIARY with a date,
such as DIARY 2026-11-12, any day it still holds.  DIARY LIST shows the
days at a glance, one line each, newest first.  The diary keeps your last
30 days; the oldest is let go as a new one begins.

A day is the game's own calendar day, the one the holidays follow; the
page turns at the game's midnight even while you are playing.  Things taken
out of your own bag, corpse or stash are not counted as gained, and food,
potions, scrolls and coin are left out of what you came by.

Only you can read your diary.  The staff may read it to help you with a
problem.  It never says who else was there, what anybody said, or which
room you stood in.  The staff keep no diary.

See also: SCORE, ACHIEVEMENTS, AQUEST, HISTORY
~

0 CHANGES~
Syntax: changes
        changes all

CHANGES shows what is new in the game, newest first: the changes from the
last two weeks that you have not already been shown three times.  Once you
have seen an entry three times, or it is more than two weeks old, it stops
appearing, so the list stays short.

CHANGES ALL shows every change there has ever been, however often you
have seen it.

Type HELP on any command named in a change for the detail.

See also: NEWS, MOTD
~

0 HITROLL DAMROLL HR DR 'TO HIT'~
Hitroll and damroll are the two numbers SCORE shows beside your armour.

  HITROLL  how often your blows land.  Each point is a little more likely
           to get past your opponent's armour and defences.
  DAMROLL  how hard they land.  Each point is added to every blow that
           connects, so it counts for more the more often you swing.

Both come from your strength, your gear and spells such as BLESS, GIANT
STRENGTH and FRENZY.  COMPARE weighs a piece of gear by what it does to
your damage, which is mostly these two, and the website's Gear Finder
does the same for every slot at once.

See also: SCORE, COMPARE, ATTRIBUTES, AFFECTS
~

0 KEEPSAKE KEEPSAKES TROPHY 'KEEPSAKE TROPHY'~
The keepsake trophy is a quest reward for heroes (level 51 and up): a
silver questmaster's sigil, sold for 600 quest points with AQUEST BUY
TROPHY.  It does nothing in a fight.  It is proof, to anyone who looks at
it, that you served the questmasters well beyond a single contract.

The other end-game rewards on AQUEST LIST are the Legendary Boon (gold and
practices) and the Surprise Cache (a bundle of potions).

See also: AQUEST, HEROLEVELS
~

0 GOD GODS IMMORTAL IMMORTALS STAFF WIZLIST~
The immortals are the people who run Times of Chaos.  WIZLIST names them.
They may be in the game unseen, so WHO does not always show them.

To reach them, write a NOTE to immortal, or file what you found with BUG,
TYPO or IDEA, which they read.  They can reset a forgotten password,
restore something a bug took from you, and answer what the help does not.

See also: NOTE, BUG, RULES, WIZLIST
~

0 'BLACK MARKET' DEALER MARKET VESH SMUGGLER~
Vesh the smuggler sells what cannot be had elsewhere: some of the finest
gear for level 29 and up, holiday treasures out of season, now and then a
training cake or a lucky clover.

  - He is in the world perhaps one hour in ten, and never stays put: he
    moves every minute or so and sometimes vanishes to another place.
  - Nothing finds him from afar.  You see him by walking into his room,
    or with SCAN from the next one.
  - Start buying within a minute and a half or he moves on.
  - LIST shows his five items at most; BUY buys.  He buys nothing, does
    not haggle, and sells one of each to a customer.
  - Prices are steep, and five times that for what nothing else drops.
  - While he serves you nobody else may buy.  When you are done or walk
    away he is gone, and his stock is new next time.

See also: SCAN, LIST, BUY, 'TREASURE GOBLIN'
~

65 GOBLIN~
Syntax: goblin                (where he is, his hit points, the chase)
        goblin spawn          (a new one at a random room, if none)
        goblin here           (a new one in your room, replacing any)
        goblin dismiss        (send him away; another comes soon)

There is always one treasure goblin (mob 29941): a new one appears at a
random room a minute to three after the last is killed.  He wanders a step
every half minute or so and drifts to a random room every twenty to forty
minutes while nobody is after him, and to another area after ten minutes in
one, never into a room he could not be struck in.  Each landed blow spills
four to eight kills' worth of coin at the striker's level and sends him out
a random exit; it takes a thirteenth to an eighteenth of his health by how
hard it hit, so a kill is 12 to 17 flights.  Nothing holds him: stuns,
sleep, bash, trip and maze are shaken off at once, and CONFUSE is refused.
A minute without a blow and he opens a portal and teleports.  His sack holds
about two hundred kills' worth of coin at the killer's level, half the time
the best piece in some slot for the killer's level, and a tenth of the time
a black market rarity.  Appearances come to WIZINFO with the room and to the
log without it (the log is published); kills to both, with the room and the
flights.  Staff see him anywhere; mortals only in his room, or one room away
with SCAN.  BLACKMARKET SUMMON does the same for the black market.

See also: 'TREASURE GOBLIN', BLACKMARKET
~

0 MAPS EXPLORATION 'AREA MAP' 'AREA MAPS'~
Syntax: maps          the areas you hold a map of or have set foot in
        maps all      every area, with how much of it you have walked

Your map (HELP MAP) shows only what your character knows: the rooms you
have walked into, every room of an area whose whole map you hold, and all
of Dresden, Mud School and Limbo, which everybody knows.

Maps of an area come from:
  - a map dropped now and then by a monster slain in that area;
  - the wandering cartographer, who drops the map of wherever he falls
    (HELP 'WANDERING CARTOGRAPHER');
  - in Hyrule, each dungeon's own map, found in the dungeon.

READ a map to learn it for good; the old parchment crumbles as you do.
A map you already know is left whole, to sell or give away.

MAPS shows, area by area, whether you hold its map and how many of the
rooms that count you have walked into.  Walk into every one -- death traps,
guild halls and newbie or hero rooms do not count -- of an area with ten or
more, and the area is explored.  Exploration achievements reward maps held
and areas explored (ACHIEVEMENTS EXPLORATION).

See also: MAP, MUDLET, ACHIEVEMENTS
~

65 CARTOGRAPHER GRANTMAP~
Syntax: cartographer                (where he is and when he leaves or comes)
        cartographer spawn          (bring him now, somewhere at random)
        cartographer here           (bring him to your room)
        cartographer dismiss        (send him away; he comes back in hours)
        grantmap <player> <area|hyrule1..hyrule9|all>
        grantmap <player> <area|hyrule1..hyrule9|all> revoke
        grantmap list               (the words GRANTMAP takes)

The cartographer (mob 29942) visits for twenty to forty minutes every two
to five hours, the first visit half an hour to three hours after a boot, in
an area a map can be made of, at the level of the monsters around him.  He
walks the area a room at a time and never enters Hyrule.  Slain, he drops
the map of the area he fell in.  Appearances come to WIZINFO with the room
and to the log without it (the log is published).  Test worlds have none
(TOC_NO_CARTOGRAPHER) unless called.

GRANTMAP gives a player the whole map of an area -- by file name, as
"camelot", or the start of its name -- of one of Hyrule's dungeons, or of
everything.  It works on a player online or saved, saves their file, is
logged and refused on a player who outranks you.

REVOKE at the end takes the map back instead -- one area's, one dungeon's,
or every map they hold -- under the same rules.  Rooms they have walked
into stay known, a Hyrule dungeon map they found in play is kept, and no
achievement is taken away.  A player online is told, and their Mudlet map
hides the rooms they no longer know at once.

See also: MAPS, 'WANDERING CARTOGRAPHER'
~

0 'WANDERING CARTOGRAPHER' CARTOGRAPHER ODRIC~
Odric Penwhistle, a wandering cartographer, turns up now and then in one
of the world's areas, walks it for half an hour or so and is gone again,
often for hours.  Nothing finds him from afar; you have to come upon him.

He will not part with his charts willingly, and he fights back as hard as
the monsters around him.  Bring him down and his folio holds the whole map
of the area he fell in: READ it to learn it.

See also: MAPS, MAP
~

0 'TREASURE GOBLIN' GOBLIN~
There is always a treasure goblin somewhere: a little thief under a sack
of coin.  Nothing finds him from afar, but SCAN shows him one room away,
and he leaves dropped coin wherever he runs.

  - He never fights back.  Each blow that lands spills coin and sends him
    out a random exit, so a kill is a chase of a dozen rooms or more.
  - Stuns, sleep, bash and confusion do not hold him.
  - Go a minute without landing a blow and he opens a portal and escapes,
    wounds and all.  A blow while it opens shuts it.
  - Killed, his sack bursts: much coin for your level, often a good piece
    of gear for your level, now and then a rarity.  Another soon appears.

See also: SCAN, 'BLACK MARKET'
~

60 QUESTDIAG GEARDIAG HYRULESTATUS ALIGNCHECK~
Syntax: questdiag <player>
        geardiag <player> [item]
        hyrulestatus <player>
        aligncheck <player>

Read-only views of a player in the game; none changes anything, and a
player who outranks you is refused.

QUESTDIAG    quest state, the flags, the gamble, the daily counters, why
             an ordinary or a daily request would be refused now, and how
             many of the target are alive and still suitable.
GEARDIAG     each worn piece (or the one named): level, condition, armour
             beside its prototype, the damage ledger REPAIR restores, the
             repair price, and whether WEAR would take it now.
HYRULESTATUS hearts, caves paid, bombs owned and usable, and dungeon by
             dungeon whether each gate would open.  Spoilers: staff only.
ALIGNCHECK   alignment, tier, perks, worn gear the side rejects, and each
             devotion forecast as DEVOTE PREVIEW shows it.
~

65 FIXES~
Syntax: fixes                (every login fix, oldest first)
        fixes <player>       (which of them an online player has had)

A login fix corrects characters after a bug has been fixed: it runs once
for each character, the next time they log in, tells them what changed,
and reports to WIZINFO and the log.  A character made after a fix was
written is born correct and never runs it.  Fixes are written in the
code (src/fixes.c), never by editing player files, which live on the
game host.

~

60 BLACKMARKET~
Syntax: blackmarket                (where he is, how long, stock, pool)
        blackmarket summon         (bring him into the world now)
        blackmarket here           (bring him, or move him, to your room)
        blackmarket restock        (lay out a new stock)
        blackmarket dismiss        (send him away)

Vesh, the black market (mob 29940), comes for twenty to forty minutes, then
is away for three to six hours; his first visit after a boot is ten minutes
to six hours in.  While he is about he moves every 45-75 seconds, one move
in eight a vanishing to a random room, and after ten minutes in one area he
crosses to another.  A mortal with him has 90 seconds to start buying; the
first purchase gives that buyer alone 60 seconds, and 30 more per purchase,
then he vanishes.  He carries five items at most from a pool of the three
best pieces per slot in every five levels from 29, plus rarities at any
level.  Appearances and purchases come to WIZINFO and the log with the room.
Staff see him anywhere; mortals only in his room, or one room away with
SCAN.

See also: 'BLACK MARKET'
~

0 HOLIDAYS HOLIDAY FESTIVALS 'HOLIDAY FAIR'~
Syntax: holidays               what is on, and this year's dates
        holidays <year>        the dates in another year
        holidays on <date>     what one day is: holidays on 2027-03-28

While a holiday is on, a vendor and a decoration stand in the Oak Tree
Square, creatures now and then carry its treat, and TIME and your login
say so.  Vendors sell and buy nothing.  Dates follow the server's clock.

  Winter Veil         Dec 16 - Jan 6     Pirates' Day       Sep 19
  Lunar Festival      Jan 21 - Feb 4     Brewfest           Sep 20 - Oct 6
  Love is in the Air  Feb 7 - Feb 20     Hallows End        Oct 18 - Nov 1
  St. Patrick's Day   Mar 14 - Mar 17    Day of the Dead    Nov 2 - Nov 3
  Noblegarden         Easter week        Pilgrim's Bounty   Thanksgiving week
  Children's Week     Apr 30 - May 6
  Midsummer Fire      Jun 21 - Jul 5

The decorated towns of Hallows End and Winter Veil change only when the
world restarts, so it restarts itself for them, between 3 and 4 in the
morning New York time: on the first day of each, and the day after each
ends.  Everyone is warned five minutes and one minute before, and saved.
No other holiday restarts anything.

Staff: HOLIDAYS also shows where the date comes from, the fair, and which
towns were loaded at boot -- and, when they disagree with the calendar,
when the restart will be.  HOLIDAYS REFRESH puts the fair up now.
HOLIDAYS RESTART shows the restart, HOLIDAYS RESTART CANCEL (implementor)
calls it off and skips that night, and HOLIDAYS RESTART NOW (REBOOT's
rank) starts the five-minute warning at once.  A game up less than an
hour does not restart, so one restart a night is the most.

See also: 'HOLIDAY TREATS', TIME, 'BLACK MARKET'
~

0 'HOLIDAY TREATS'~
What the holiday treats do:

  Red envelope (Lunar)        OPEN it for lucky money
  Red rose (Love)             GIVE it to another player: it blesses you both
  Clover (St. Patrick's)      EAT it: the next foe you slay spills a pot of
                              gold.  You hold luck for three kills at most;
                              a fourth clover is refused.  Luck is lost when
                              you leave the game.
  Painted egg (Noblegarden)   hidden around Dresden; OPEN it
  Firework (Midsummer)        DROP it and the whole area sees it
  Festival keg (Brewfest)     DRINK from it, free
  Candy (Hallows End)         EAT it for a treat or a trick
  Sugar skull (Day of Dead)   lets you see a little of what the dead see

Clovers, candy hearts and Hallows End candy are a bite, not a meal: you
can eat them when full.

See also: HOLIDAYS
~

0 DISASTERS~
Now and then a disaster strikes an area someone is in.  A warning comes
first -- the ground trembles, the wind rises, smoke is on the air -- and
the first blow a few seconds later, so there is time to take cover.  It
strikes again every few seconds until it passes.

  Flood         sweeps you a few rooms along; flying or a quick dodge
                avoids it; needs rain, never indoors
  Lightning     a bolt from a thunderstorm, outdoors only
  Earthquake    falling rubble, and it knocks you down; not on water or
                in the air
  Tornado       picks you up and drops you somewhere else
  Sand storm    chokes and blinds you, in the desert
  Brush fire    in fields, forests and hills
  Tidal wave    crashes over you on the water

A good saving throw shrugs most of them off.  A disaster never kills: it
leaves you at least one hit point.  It spares the Temple, the altar, safe
rooms, arenas and anyone below level 10.

See also: 'BLACK DRAGON', WEATHER
~

0 'BLACK DRAGON'~
Sometimes the Great Black Dragon swoops down on an area and carries one
person standing outdoors (level 10 or more) to another part of it, and
lands beside them.  It does not attack.

  - KILL DRAGON to fight it: two levels above you, with three times the
    hit points of a usual foe of your level, and it bashes, trips,
    disarms and sweeps with its tail.
  - Its hoard: coin, always a good piece of gear for your level, and now
    and then a rarity.
  - Or walk away: it leaves about three minutes after landing, or soon
    after you go, never during a fight.

See also: DISASTERS
~

60 DISASTER~
Syntax: disaster                    (areas suffering one; where the dragon is)
        disaster <kind>             (strike the area you stand in)
        disaster stop               (end the one in your area)
        disaster dragon <player>    (the dragon takes that player now)

Kinds: flood, lightning, earthquake, tornado, sandstorm, fire, tidal,
dragon.  A disaster you start, like a random one, warns the area and
strikes on the next cycle (every six or seven seconds), then ends half
the time each cycle; fire, tidal and dragon strike once.  Random ones
strike an occupied area two times in ten thousand per cycle, never Mud
School or Limbo; floods need rain and lightning a thunderstorm.  They
never kill and spare staff, characters below level 10, and the Temple,
altar, safe rooms and arenas.  DISASTER DRAGON <player> needs a mortal
of level 10 or more outdoors, out of a fight and not in a no-recall room;
the dragon lands in another outdoor room of their area, scaled to their
level, and leaves three minutes later if nobody fights it.  The log
names who was taken and from which area, never where the dragon waits;
WIZINFO gives the rooms.

See also: DISASTERS
~

0 RESTED 'REST XP' 'RESTED XP'~
Syntax: rested

Quit somewhere a body could rest -- a safe room, a room that heals, the
Temple, the altar where the pit healer stands, or your own recall point --
and you come back rested: each hour away banks 5% of a level of bonus
experience, up to 150%.  While you are rested, every kill earns double
experience until the bonus is spent.  Quitting anywhere else banks
nothing, and neither does coming back within fifteen minutes.

RESTED shows how much you have and whether the room you stand in would
let you rest.  Rest is kept, not spent, at a level where you earn no
experience.

See also: QUIT, RECALL, AQUEST
~

0 USE~
Syntax: use <item> [target]

USE finds the item and does the obvious thing with it: quaffs a potion,
recites a scroll, eats food or a pill, drinks from a flask or a fountain,
zaps a held wand at the target, brandishes a held staff, plays an
instrument, wears or wields gear, looks inside a container and enters a
portal.  USE BOMB <target> sets a bomb against a cracked wall or a foe,
and USE CANDLE <target> burns with a candle.  A key needs no using:
UNLOCK the door or chest it fits.

See also: QUAFF, RECITE, ZAP, BRANDISH, EAT, DRINK, WEAR, PLAY, BOMB
~

0 AFK~
Syntax: afk
        afk <message>

Marks you away from the keyboard: [*AFK*] shows beside your name in WHO
and before your prompt.  Anyone who sends you a TELL or REPLY is told you
are AFK, with your message if you gave one.  Typing AFK again, or sending
a TELL or REPLY yourself, clears it.  It lasts only for this session.

AFK does not protect you from anything; see SHADOWMELD.

Example: afk back in ten minutes

See also: TELL, REPLY, WHO
~

0 CRAWL~
Syntax: crawl <thing>

Crawls through something in the room -- a hole, a tunnel, a gap under a
wall -- and out the other side.  Not every hole leads anywhere; one that
does not answers "Nothing seems to happen."  These ways through are not
listed among a room's exits, so read the room description and LOOK at
what it mentions.

Example: crawl hole

See also: CLIMB, JUMP, ENTER, LOOK
~

0 JUMP~
Syntax: jump <thing>

Jumps up or down something in the room -- a ledge, a pit, a chasm -- to
wherever it leads.  As with CRAWL and CLIMB, these ways through are part
of the room's scenery rather than its exits, so read the description.

Example: jump pit

See also: CRAWL, CLIMB, ENTER, LOOK
~

0 DAMAGENUMBERS~
Syntax: damagenumbers

Turns on or off the exact damage figure after each blow you deal or take:
"You slash the goblin hard [-47]".  Others in the room see only the
words.  With it on you are also told each small shift in your alignment.
It is off for new characters, and saved.

See also: SETTINGS, ALIGNMENT, DUMMY
~

0 NEW SETTINGS CUSTOMIZE OPTIONS~
The settings you can change, all saved with your character.  HELP any of
them for detail; AUTO lists how they stand.

Display
  COLOR           colour on or off, and which colours
  PROMPT          the status line; PROMPT DEFAULT puts it back
  BRIEF           short room descriptions
  COMPACT         no blank line between messages
  SCROLL          lines per page
  DAMAGENUMBERS   exact damage figures in combat (off by default)
  AUTOEXIT        list the exits as you enter a room

What happens for you
  AUTOLOOT        take everything from corpses you kill
  AUTOGOLD        take the coins from corpses you kill
  AUTOSAC         sacrifice the empty corpse afterwards
  AUTOSPLIT       share looted coin with your group
  AUTOASSIST      join a fight your group is in
  WIMPY           flee by yourself below a number of hit points

Others and you
  CHANNELS        the channels you hear
  NOSUMMON        stop others summoning you (on by default)
  NOFOLLOW        stop others following you
  NOLOOT          stop others looting your corpse

See also: AUTO, PROMPT, COLOR, CHANNELS
~

0 PRACTICELIST~
Syntax: practicelist

Lists every guildmaster your class and guild allow you to use, and for each
one the skills you already know that they can practise you in -- which is
exactly what PRACTICE will accept from them. Your current percentage is
shown beside each, and your remaining practice sessions at the end.

A skill you have just gained sits at 1% until somebody raises it, and
nothing else in the game tells you who can.

See also: PRACTICE, GAINLIST, GAIN, GROUPS
~

0 GAINLIST ABILITIES~
Syntax: gainlist                          (or ABILITIES)
        gainlist trainers
        gainlist preview <class> [guild]

GAINLIST lists every skill and spell your class and guild can learn:

  X    not learned, usable at your level -- go and GAIN it
  -    not learned, usable at a later level (you may GAIN it now)
  +    learned, with how well you know it

Each thing still to learn shows what to GAIN for it (a group brings
several spells), the cost in trains, the trainer, and the WALKTO that
takes you there.  The Oracle is given the same list.

GAINLIST TRAINERS lists each trainer you may use and everything they sell:
'have' if you have it, 'n/a' if your class cannot gain it.

GAINLIST PREVIEW shows another build before you remort into it: its kit by
level, starred where it must be GAINed, the trains it costs, blows a round
at levels 10, 25, 40 and 50, and what a guild adds or gives up.  Monks and
necromancers have no guild: GAINLIST PREVIEW MONK.

See also: GAIN, PRACTICELIST, TEACHLIST, TRAIN, WALKTO
~

0 PROMPT~
Syntax: prompt                    what you use now
        prompt codes              every placeholder
        prompt <format>           set your own
        prompt default            the standard one
        prompt on | off           show or hide it
        prompt room on | off      add the room vnum (staff)

Sets the status line shown after each command.  Codes: %h %H hit points
now and maximum, %m %M mana, %v %V movement, %e experience to level,
%X total experience, %g gold, %s silver, %r room name, %c %C your normal
and highlight colours, %% a percent sign.  %R, the room vnum, shows for
staff only.  Anything else prints as typed.

  prompt %C<%hhp %mm %vmv>%c      the default
  prompt <%h/%H hp %m/%M mana>    current and maximum together

See also: COLOR, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 QUESTION~
Syntax: question
Syntax: question <message>

Toggles or uses the Q/A channel.  With no message, the command toggles your
subscription to the Q/A channel.  With a message, it broadcasts your question
to everyone listening on that channel so other players can help.

See also: GOSSIP, TELL, CHANNELS
~

0 BANK INTEREST~
There is no BANK command.  In a bank room you have four:

  BALANCE                       what you have on deposit
  DEPOSIT <amount> [coin type]  pay in (platinum unless you say)
  WITHDRAW <amount> [coin type] take out, if you can carry it
  CONVERT                       change your coins into the largest
                                denominations they make

  - Interest: 0.25% a real day on a balance of 1 platinum or more, paid
    while you play and caught up for up to 7 days away.
  - No fees.  Each deposit and withdrawal saves you at once.
  - Your balance is kept through deaths and remorts.
  - Where a banker stands, player killers are refused.

See also: BALANCE, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, WORTH
~

0 BALANCE~
Syntax: balance

Shows your current bank account balance in all denominations: platinum,
gold, silver, and copper.  You must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, WORTH
~

0 DEPOSIT~
Syntax: deposit <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Moves coins from your inventory into your bank account.  An optional coin
type may be specified; if omitted, platinum is assumed.  You must be inside
a bank room.

Examples:
  deposit 100 gold
  deposit 500 platinum

See also: BANK, WITHDRAW, BALANCE, CONVERT
~

0 WITHDRAW~
Syntax: withdraw <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Retrieves coins from your bank account into your inventory.  Same syntax
as DEPOSIT.  You must have room in your inventory to carry the coins and
must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, BALANCE
~

0 CONVERT~
Syntax: convert

Exchanges all your carried coins upward to the highest denomination
possible (copper -> silver -> gold -> platinum).  Handy before depositing
to keep your inventory tidy.  Must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, BALANCE
~

0 CASINO LUCKY DRAGON GAMBLING~
The Lucky Dragon Casino is located in Dresden's Market Plaza -- head south
from the plaza to find the gilded entrance doors.

Games available:
  SLOTS     -- Three-reel slot machine (cost: 1 gold per pull)
  BET       -- Hi/Lo dice game (1-100,000 gold per bet)
  ROULETTE  -- European single-zero roulette wheel (1-100,000 gold per bet)
  POKER     -- Video poker, dealt once, paid on hand rank (5-200 gold)

Type the game's name alone for full payout tables and instructions.
See also: SLOTS, BET, ROULETTE, POKER, BANK
~

0 SLOTS~
Syntax: slots

Play the three-reel slot machine in the casino.  Costs 1 gold per pull.

  Symbols: SEVEN, BAR, CHERRY, LEMON, ORANGE, GRAPE
  Payouts:
    Three BARs   -- Win 50 gold
    Three SEVENS -- Win 10 gold
    Three of any -- Win 10 gold
    Two matching -- Get your 1 gold back
    No match     -- Lose 1 gold

  The machine keeps about seven coins in every hundred.

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, BET, ROULETTE, POKER
~

0 BET~
Syntax: bet <amount> hi
Syntax: bet <amount> lo

Hi/Lo dice game at the casino.  Place a wager of 1 to 100,000 gold on the
outcome of two dice.  Bets above 500 gold require confirmation.

  HI: wins if the two-dice total is MORE than 7  (pays 1:1)
  LO: wins if the two-dice total is LESS than 7  (pays 1:1)
  Exactly 7: the house always wins.

Examples:
  bet 50 hi
  bet 200 lo

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, ROULETTE, POKER
~

0 ROULETTE~
Syntax: roulette <amount> <bet>

European single-zero roulette at the casino.  Place a bet of 1 to 100,000
gold.  Bets above 500 gold require confirmation.

  EVEN-MONEY bets (1:1): red  black  even  odd  low(1-18)  high(19-36)
  DOZEN bets      (2:1): first(1-12)  second(13-24)  third(25-36)
  STRAIGHT-UP    (35:1): any single number from 0 to 36

  Zero (0) is green and loses all even-money and dozen bets.

Examples:
  roulette 100 red
  roulette 50 17
  roulette 200 first
  roulette 25 odd

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, BET, POKER
~

0 POKER~
Syntax: poker <amount>

Video poker at the casino.  A hand of five cards is dealt from a standard
52-card deck.  The hand is evaluated immediately; there is no hold mechanic.
Minimum bet: 5 gold.  Maximum bet: 200 gold.

  Payout table (net multiple of wager):
    Royal Flush     (A-K-Q-J-10 same suit) -- 100x
    Straight Flush  (5 consecutive, flush)  --  20x
    Four of a Kind                          --  10x
    Full House      (trips + pair)          --   7x
    Flush           (5 same suit)           --   5x
    Straight        (5 consecutive)         --   3x
    Three of a Kind                         --   2x
    Two Pair                                --   1x
    Jacks or Better (pair J/Q/K/A)         --  push (bet returned)
    Anything else                           --  lose

Cards are shown as rank + suit letter, e.g. "Ac Kc Qc Jc 10c" = royal flush.
Suits: c=clubs  d=diamonds  h=hearts  s=spades

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, BET, ROULETTE
~

0 BREW CONCOCT 'HERBAL BREWING'~
Syntax: brew <herb>
        concoct <component> <component>
        brew preview <herb>
        concoct preview <component> <component>

BREW makes an herbal tea from one herb you carry; CONCOCT makes a potion
from two spell components, in either order.

  - Ingredients that make nothing are refused and kept.
  - An attempt takes a few seconds.  A failure uses the ingredients up,
    and a botched potion burns you; the burn can kill.
  - PREVIEW is free: what would come out, its spells, its level, your
    chance and what failure costs.  It shows only what you could make
    from what you carry.
  - What you make is your level, and nobody below it can use it.
  - Herbs and components turn up all over the world, outside Hyrule, and
    lie where they fell until somebody picks them up; they never wither.
    Every recipe is in the Magic Book, in Hyrule's Level 8.
  - The rarest concoctions hold TITANIC or EMPOWER.  They last half your
    level in ticks, at most thirty.

BREW: Monk 12, from the Master of Movement.  CONCOCT: Mage 17, Cleric 17,
Necromancer 19, from Flame the fire mage, Tempora the high priestess and
Marilith.  GAINLIST shows who sells them to you.

See also: SCRIBE, QUAFF, LORE, ACHIEVEMENTS
~

0 SCRIBE~
Syntax: scribe <component> <component> <component>
        scribe preview <component> <component> <component>

Writes a scroll from three spell components you carry.
Component order does not matter, and no blank scroll or quill is needed.
Components that make nothing are refused and kept; a failed attempt uses
them up.

  - The scroll is cast at your level and needs that level to read.  One
    whose spell works on an object is read at one you carry: RECITE
    <scroll> <object>.  Reading is a roll on your SCROLLS skill.
  - PREVIEW is free and shows what you would make and your chances.
  - Some inks write TITANIC or EMPOWER.  The Magic Book in Hyrule's
    Level 8 lists every ink.

Usable from level: Mage 20, Cleric 25, Necromancer 30.  Trainers: Dawn the
wand maker, Drixt the golden dragon, the Undead Spirit.

See also: BREW, RECITE, ACHIEVEMENTS
~

0 SHOOT ARCHERY BOW~
Syntax: shoot <target>

Fires a wielded bow at a visible mobile through an open adjacent exit,
in the next room; anyone in your own room is out of range.

  - Needs the ARCHERY skill.  Not while fighting, and never at a player.
  - A hit deals 2d4 piercing damage.  No arrows are needed.
  - It costs three seconds, hit or miss.  A wounded creature may come
    after you; one that cannot still makes you pay a few hit points.

ARCHERY is also the skill for a bow swung in melee.  Usable from level:
Mage 35, Cleric 30, Thief 25, Warrior 15, Monk 20; never Necromancer.
Trainer: Rakar the weaponsmaster.

In Hyrule its own bow and boomerangs SHOOT an enemy in the same room, with
no ARCHERY needed (HYRULE ITEMS).  Only a character of level 54 or more
wielding the Silver Arrow can finish Ganon: SHOOT GANON once he flashes
red.

See also: WIELD, SCAN, 'HYRULE ITEMS', GANON
~

0 HYRULE DUNGEONS~
Hyrule lies inside the arcade cabinet in the Games Room on the university
campus: ENTER CABINET.  RECALL works on its open land, not in the nine
numbered dungeons.

The dungeons are done in order, and each works the same way:
  - Entering needs the previous dungeon's Triforce piece.
  - The guardian's chamber needs a tool found in an earlier dungeon.
  - The guardian gives a key to every player it credits: the killer,
    their group in the room, and anyone in the room who struck it.
  - The key opens the door behind the guardian and the chest there.
    Taking the piece spends it; the chest keeps a piece for the rest of
    the party.  Keys are lost at QUIT.
  - The dungeon's map, compass and small keys crumble when you leave
    it; see 'HYRULE ITEMS'.
  - Pieces and tools cannot be dropped, given or sold.  Most tools lie in
    a cellar down a block stair (PUSH BLOCK, then DOWN), and each player
    who takes one gets a copy of their own.
  - COMBINE TRIFORCE joins all nine.

Once you hold the Recorder, PLAY RECORDER on the open land carries you to
a dungeon's door.  The Oracle knows every path: PRAY and ask her.

See also: 'HYRULE ORDER', 'HYRULE GUARDIANS', 'HYRULE ITEMS', GANON
~

0 'HYRULE ORDER' 'HYRULE LEVELS'~
  Level  For levels  To enter      Before the guardian  Tools found inside
  1      2-8         --            --                   boomerang, bow
  2      8-14        piece 1       a boomerang          Magical Boomerang
  3      14-20       piece 2       Magical Boomerang    raft
  4      20-27       piece 3+raft  the raft             stepladder
  5      27-33       piece 4       the stepladder       Recorder
  6      33-40       piece 5       the bow              Magical Rod
  7      40-46       piece 6       the Recorder         Red Candle
  8      46-52       piece 7       the Red Candle       Magic Book,
                                                        Magical Key
  9      53-59       pieces 1-8    the Magical Key      Silver Arrow,
                                                        Red Ring

From The First Quest Begins, where the cabinet sets you down; each
entrance is DOWN from its screen:
  1  north up the I column to the River Landing, stepping stones west
  2  east to Moblin Hollow, north, west to the Eastern Dock, then north
  3  west through the woods to the Bramble Wood, south, then east
  4  Shore Below the Snake, plank bridge north, ENTER RAFT
  5  north-east: the Lost Hills, west of Lone Armos Ridge, north 4 times
  6  far north-west, past the Lost Woods (north, west, south, west), the
     graveyard and Ghost Hill
  7  the Still Pond in the west: PLAY RECORDER to drain it
  8  the Lion's Thicket in the east: BURN BUSH
  9  Spectacle Rock: up the Mountain Stair, west twice, BOMB CRACKED

Leaving a dungeon you have won, an owl tells you where the next one is.

See also: HYRULE, 'HYRULE GUARDIANS'
~

0 'HYRULE GUARDIANS'~
Nothing in Hyrule attacks first, and the land around each dungeon is
graded for it.  Each guardian is a hard fight for its band: an ordinary
character at the top of the band loses alone.  With Hermie's buffs (BUFF
EMPOWER, BUFF DEFENSE, BUFF COMBAT) one can win alone, narrowly; a group
does better.

  - The ashen Gleeok of Level 8 is sized for two players, Ganon for
    three.  One may try, and should expect to lose.
  - The guardians of Levels 7 to 9 heal when left alone.
  - Gohma's eye closes against every finishing blow but an arrow: carry
    the bow.
  - A digdogger, Level 5's guardian among them, cannot be hurt until the
    Recorder is played at it.
  - The first eight guardians carry a weapon, a Heart Guard and a Heart
    Container.  Every guardian also drops three pieces from its own table
    of ten or eleven, each the best a character of its band can find.

See also: HYRULE, GANON, BUFF, HEARTS
~

0 GANON RELICS 'MASTER SWORD' TRIFORCE 'RED RING'~
Ganon, at the end of Death Mountain, is a fight for three; fewer may try,
but should expect to lose.  He blinks about the dark hurling fireballs no
parry stops, and heals if left alone.  At one hit point he flashes red and
is stunned, and only a level 54 or higher character wielding the Silver
Arrow can finish him: SHOOT GANON.  The Silver Arrow lies in one of Death
Mountain's cellars; a group does best with two.

  - Each player his death credits gets the Golden Key.  It opens Zelda's
    chamber and the great chest there: the last Triforce piece and the
    Master Sword (level 58), the finest blade a mortal can carry, which
    hastens its wielder.
  - He carries his crown and trident, and leaves three pieces of his drop
    table and one relic:
      Hero's Tunic (54)    heals you when you kill a creature yourself
      Pegasus Boots (55)   walking costs a quarter fewer moves
      Mirror Shield (56)   15% less damage from anything but blows
  - The Red Ring (level 58, Death Mountain's other cellar) gives sanctuary
    and takes a fifth off all damage, the Blue Ring (level 45, from the
    ring shop) a tenth.  One of each may be worn: 28% together.
  - COMBINE TRIFORCE, with all nine pieces, makes The Triforce (level 58).
    Worn as your light, it gives the sight of a level 59 HOLYLIGHT,
    counts as every piece at every door, and is the best light there is:
    +5 hitroll and damroll, +50 hit points, +100 mana, -10 armour and -5
    to your save against spells.

EXAMINE a relic for its exact limits; COMPARE understands them.

See also: 'HYRULE GUARDIANS', SHOOT, COMBINE, COMPARE
~

0 JUNK~
Syntax: junk <object>

JUNK is another name for SACRIFICE.  It works only on an object lying on
the ground here, never on one you carry: DROP it first.  The gods pay a
few copper coins for it.

See also: SACRIFICE, DROP, DONATE
~

0 "HEROIC GRIP" HEROICGRIP HEROIC GRIP~
Syntax: (passive; nothing to type)

Each time someone tries to disarm you, or anything knocks your weapon
loose, heroic grip is a chance you keep hold of it; fully practised, you
cannot be disarmed.  It covers both hands.  It does nothing to the weapon:
REMOVE and WIELD work as ever, unlike BEWITCH WEAPON.

Warriors of the warriors' guild only, from level 35; every one already
holds it.  A guildmaster raises it quickly: Vladamir the veteran teaches
it, and Dolonar the blacksmith and Breark the troll practise it.  Past
that it is earned in fights: each grab at your weapon can improve it.

  You wrench at Karg's weapon, but he grips it like iron!

See also: DISARM, BEWITCH WEAPON, PRACTICE
~

0 DOORBASH 'DOOR BASH'~
Syntax: doorbash <door>

A warrior's shoulder to a closed door: success opens it, and unlocks it,
on both sides.  It needs no key and works on locks no pick can open, but
not on a magical lock or one of Hyrule's seals.  You cannot bash in a
fight.  It costs three seconds; a failure leaves you on the ground,
resting.

Usable from level: Warrior 10; never Mage, Cleric, Thief, Monk, Necromancer.
Trainer: Vladamir the veteran, to warriors of the warriors' guild.  GAINLIST
shows who sells it to your class and guild.

See also: PICK, UNLOCK, BASH
~



0 HIT~
Syntax: hit <character>

HIT is KILL under another name: it starts a fight with a creature.  Both
refuse players; use MURDER for those.

See also: KILL, MURDER, FLEE
~

0 MURDER MURDE~
Syntax: murder <character>

Attacks a player; KILL refuses players.  Your victim yells for help, and
every MURDER is logged.  It works on creatures too.  Read PKILL and RULES
before you use it.

MURDE, or MUR, only tells you to type MURDER in full.

See also: KILL, PKILL, RULES
~

0 DEATHTRAP DEATHTRAPS 'DEATH TRAP' 'DEATH TRAPS' DT~
A death trap is a room that kills whoever stays in it: a pit, a spiked
floor, a fall with no bottom.  There are a handful in the world, and the
room description is usually the only warning you get.

Step into one and you have about two seconds.  The first moment knocks you
down and takes half your hit points ("Your fate has been sealed!"); the
next one kills you.  Get out the way you came at once, if you still can.
A death there is an ordinary death: your corpse and your gear lie in the
trap's room, which is why they are so hard to recover.

Teleport and gate never set you down in one, WALKTO never walks into one,
and a recall point cannot be set in one.  Immortals are not harmed.

See also: DEATH, CORPSE, WALKTO, RECALL
~

0 AQUEST QUEST QUESTING QUESTPOINT QUESTPOINTS QUESTOR THEWS~
Syntax: aquest info | points | time | gamble <yes|no>    anywhere
        aquest request | complete | list | buy | abort    at a questmaster
        aquest daily ...                                  see DAILY

The questmaster sends you to kill a creature or recover an item.  Each
subcommand may be cut to its first letter; AQUEST alone lists them.

  - Targets are creatures no higher than your level, in places you may
    go; never in Hyrule or the training yard.  Your group in the room at
    the kill shares the credit, and a pet's kill counts.
  - A recovery item is bound to you and vanishes when the quest ends.
  - Completed quests may be turned in to any questmaster for coin and a
    pending quest-point reward.  AQUEST GAMBLE YES then risks it on an
    even chance of double; NO keeps it.  Settle it before your next quest.
    AQUEST GAMBLE NEVER always keeps it, with no offer; ASK brings it back.
  - Most quests allow 10 to 30 minutes.  About one in five is a RUSH (5
    to 8 minutes, double points), one in twenty an EMERGENCY (5 minutes,
    five times the points); never both.
  - Quests in a row build a streak bonus, up to 300% more points.
    Aborting, timing out or quitting on a quest ends it -- except an
    EMERGENCY that runs out of time, which keeps your streak.
  - The wait between quests runs while you are logged out too.

AQUEST INFO shows your quest or wait, points, gamble and streak, and what
ABORT would cost.  AQUEST LIST and BUY are the questmaster's shop.

See also: DAILY, TOPTEN, RESTED, 'SCROLL OF FARSLAY'
~

0 DAILY DAILIES~
Syntax: aquest daily                   your dailies, streak and points
        aquest daily request           at a questmaster
        aquest daily buy train [n]     160 daily points a train
        aquest daily buy practice [n]  16 daily points a practice

Three daily quests a day: the same kind of target, thirty minutes each,
never a rush or emergency.  Each pays coin and 1 daily point, and all
three in one day pay 1 more.  Days in a row with all three done are your
daily streak; a full day then pays 1 extra from 7 days, 2 from 14, 3 from
30 and 4 from 100.  A month of every daily buys about one train.

  - Daily points are not quest points.
  - A daily never touches your quest streak or the wait between quests.
  - Giving one up, by abandoning it or leaving the game, means ten
    minutes before the next.
  - The day turns over at midnight UTC.

See also: AQUEST
~

0 TOPTEN~
Syntax: topten

Lists the leading player killers: how many times each was killed by a
player, how many players each killed, and the difference, which orders
the list.

See also: PKILL, MURDER
~


0 SHOVE~
Syntax: shove <character> <direction>

Pushes someone out of the room through the exit you name, to split one
creature from a pack.  Any start of the direction works: SHOVE GUARD S.

  - Your strength, size, level and skill against theirs decide it.
  - Not someone fighting, an aggressive creature, a servant, or any of
    Hyrule's creatures; not through a closed door, into the air or water
    they cannot cross, or into a room they may not enter.
  - It takes a moment.  A failed shove now and then provokes an attack,
    more often from a foe above your level.

Usable from level: Mage 16, Cleric 15, Thief 13, Warrior 10, Monk 11,
Necromancer 16.  Trains: 1 for warriors and monks, 2 for others.
Trainers: Rakar and Jazair.

See also: BASH, TRIP, KICK
~

0 ROLL DICE~
Syntax: roll <number>d<sides>

Rolls dice where everyone in the room can see the result, for settling a
bet or sharing out loot: ROLL 2D6, ROLL 1D100.  From 1 to 20 dice of 2 to
1000 sides.

See also: SAY, EMOTE
~


0 QUIT QUI~
Syntax: quit

Saves your character and leaves the game.  You cannot quit in a fight, and
your things, purse, bank and stash are all kept.  Where you quit matters
for rest: see RESTED.

QUIT has to be typed in full.  QUI only reminds you to spell it out, and
shorter words run other commands: Q and QU are QUESTION.

See also: SAVE, RESTED, DELETE
~

60 BEEP~
Syntax: beep
        beep <player>

BEEP <player> sends the named player, wherever they are, a terminal bell and
the line "<you> beeps you."  It works on any player, not only staff, and is
refused when they have turned beeps off ("They are not accepting beeps.").

BEEP with no argument turns your own beeps off, or back on.

See also: IMMTALK, WIZINFO
~

0 ALIAS ALIASES UNALIAS~
Syntax: alias                     list your aliases
        alias <word>              show one
        alias <word> <commands>   set it
        alias delete <word>       remove it
        unalias <alias>           the same

An alias is a word that types commands for you.  You may keep 20, each of
up to 10 commands separated by semicolons.  They run one at a time as if
typed, so lag from one delays the next, and anything typed after the
alias goes on the end of its last command.

  alias mm cast 'magic missile'
  alias kb kill;bash
  kb orc                       kill, then bash orc

Any line may chain commands with semicolons: n;kill orc.  Write \; for a
semicolon inside a command.  A line that sets an alias is taken whole.
An alias never runs another alias, and ! repeats the alias, not its last
command.

See also: COMMANDS
~

0 'HUNTING GROUNDS' 'WHERE TO HUNT' GROUNDS~
Where ordinary foes gather in numbers, for a first life alone; a group, a
remort or good gear moves the bands.  WALKTO <area> walks you there.
Slaying the good darkens your alignment, slaying the evil brightens it.

  Levels  Area                 Ordinary foes          Keep clear of
  8-14    Dwarven Catacombs    templar pages,         templars and their
                               spelunkers, mist       officers, and the
                                                      skeletons, zombies
                                                      and wraiths of the
                                                      graves, attack on
                                                      sight
  13-16   Town of Solace       townguards (good)      one guard post is
                                                      aggressive
  15-20   Arachnos             young, drone and wolf  the Bird Spider,
                               spiders                the guardians, Yevaud
                                                      and Arachnos attack
                                                      on sight
  17-22   Mushroom Grove       drow females (good)
  24-32   Korzath's Fortress   security guards
                               (neutral)
  28-34   Coven                witches (evil): one    the warlock, the
                               in the inn's rooms,    coven leader and the
                               through the under-     beast in the fire;
                               brush east of the      the villagers are
                               logging trail          no quarry
  30-36   the Upper Argo       Orgeem imps (evil)     the dragoneers and
                                                      the trolls are far
                                                      harder
  33-50   Pirate Ship          midshipmen, drunk      the drunks attack on
                               pirates, swashbucklers sight
                               (evil)

A named creature in its own lair is a boss, meant for a group.  CONSIDER
tells you how a foe measures up.

See also: WALKTO, AREAS, ALIGNMENT, CONSIDER
~

0 AREAS WORLD CITIES~
Syntax: areas

AREAS shows you a list of areas in the game.  Area names are shown with
recommended levels.  However, those recommendations are just a rough range
and it is a good idea to read road signs to determine if you are entering
somewhere that may be particularly dangerous.

AREAS lists everywhere that exists.  WALKTO lists everywhere there is a
published walking route to, with the distance to each.

See also: WALKTO, ROUTES, WHERE, RECALL
~

0 ARRIVE~
Syntax: arrive <string>

Sets the message that others see when you enter a room.  The message must
include $n (your name) and $T (the direction you arrived from).

  $T (arrive) = the north, the south, the east, the west, above, below

Example:  arrive $n strides in from $T.

See also: DEPART
~

0 DEPART~
Syntax: depart <string>

Sets the message that others see when you leave a room.  The message must
include $n (your name) and $t (the direction you are heading).

  $t (depart) = north, south, east, west, up, down

Example:  depart $n slips away to the $t.

See also: ARRIVE
~

0 AUTO AUTOLIST~
Syntax: auto
Syntax: autolist

AUTO (or AUTOLIST) displays the current status of all automatic feature
flags: AUTOLOOT, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC, AUTOEXIT, AUTOASSIST, AUTOSPLIT, as
well as PROMPT, COMBINE, NOFOLLOW, NOSUMMON, and NOLOOT.  When you create
a new character all automatic actions are on by default.

See also: AUTOLOOT, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC, AUTOEXIT, AUTOASSIST, AUTOSPLIT
~

0 AUTOLOOT~
Syntax: autoloot

Toggle automatic looting.  When on, you automatically try to pick up all loot
from mobiles you kill.  Anything you cannot carry remains in the corpse, and
AUTOSAC will not sacrifice a corpse while it still contains loot.

See also: AUTO, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC
~

0 AUTOGOLD~
Syntax: autogold

Turns automatic coin taking on or off.  With it on and AUTOLOOT off, you
take the coins from creatures you kill and leave the rest in the corpse.

See also: AUTO, AUTOLOOT, AUTOSPLIT
~

0 AUTOSAC~
Syntax: autosac

Toggle automatic corpse sacrifice.  When on, dead monster corpses are
automatically sacrificed after automatic looting.  Only empty corpses are
sacrificed; a corpse containing money, equipment, or anything you could not
carry is left in the room and you are told why.

See also: AUTO, AUTOLOOT, SACRIFICE
~

0 AUTOEXIT~
Syntax: autoexit

Toggle automatic exit display.  When on, visible room exits are shown
every time you enter a room, alongside the room description.

See also: AUTO, EXITS, BRIEF
~

0 AUTOASSIST~
Syntax: autoassist

Toggle automatic group assist.  When on, you automatically join combat
if another member of your group is attacked.

See also: AUTO, GROUP, RESCUE
~

0 AUTOSPLIT~
Syntax: autosplit

Toggle automatic gold splitting.  When on, gold looted from kills is
automatically split among all group members in the same room.

See also: AUTO, SPLIT, AUTOGOLD
~

0 BRANDISH~
Syntax: brandish

BRANDISH activates a magical staff you are holding.  You must HOLD the
staff first.  Staves have multiple charges; each brandish uses one.  When
all charges are exhausted the staff is consumed.  May require the STAVES
skill.

See also: ZAP, HOLD, RECITE, QUAFF
~

0 QUAFF~
Syntax: quaff <potion>

QUAFF drinks a magical potion, triggering its effects immediately.  This
is distinct from DRINK, which consumes mundane beverages.  Potions have a
single charge and are consumed on use.  No skill is required to quaff.

See also: BRANDISH, RECITE, ZAP, DRINK
~

0 RECITE~
Syntax: recite <scroll> [target]

RECITE reads from a magical scroll, casting the inscribed spell.  The
target argument is optional and depends on the nature of the scroll.
Scrolls have a single charge and are consumed on use.  May require the
SCROLLS skill.

A scroll whose spell works on an object must be read at something you are
carrying, and never at itself; either is refused before you start reading.
Crafted scrolls, teas and potions answer to their colour, to what they are
and to the spell they are known for: RECITE GLOBE reads an ironbound scroll.

See also: BRANDISH, QUAFF, ZAP
~

0 ZAP~
Syntax: zap <target>
Syntax: zap

ZAP fires a magical wand at a target.  You must HOLD the wand first.  If
no target is given and you are in combat, the current opponent is targeted
automatically.  Wands have multiple charges; each zap uses one.  When all
charges are exhausted the wand is consumed.  May require the WANDS skill.

See also: BRANDISH, RECITE, HOLD
~

5 TEACHLIST~
Syntax: teachlist

Lists each guildmaster your class and guild may use, and the skills and
spells you already know that each can practise you in.  PRACTICELIST is
the same with your percentages; GAINLIST shows what you can still learn,
who sells it and where they stand.

See also: PRACTICELIST, GAINLIST, PRACTICE, GAIN
~

69 BUILDING OLC~
Building rooms without leaving the game.

The easy way in: BUILD.  BUILD AREA, BUILD ROOM, BUILD MOB and BUILD OBJ
ask what you want a question at a time and do the rest.  See HELP BUILD.
Everything below is what it does for you, for when you would rather type
it yourself.

GOTO <vnum> to a number no room uses makes one on the spot.  SET ROOM
gives it a name, a description, flags and a sector.  RLINK connects it to
its neighbours, with doors, locks and secret ways.  RSAVE writes the lot
to disk, and until you type it nothing you built survives a reboot.

  goto 29200                       a room forms out of nothing
  set room 29200 name The Long Gallery
  set room 29200 desc A hall of cracked mirrors runs north.
  set room 29200 flags +indoors +no_mob
  set room 29200 sector inside
  rshow                            read the room back in those words
  rlink north 29201                dig on, and the way back comes free
  rsave confirm                    keep it

Rooms you make this way belong to the builder area, which is written to
area/custom.are.  Anyone from level 69 up may build there freely, and
saving takes no more than RSAVE CONFIRM.

An area of your own.  ANEW <low> <high> <name> claims a block of vnums and
makes a new area with its own file, so your work is not mixed in with
everyone else's in custom.are.  A room, and later a mobile or object, whose
vnum falls in the block belongs to that area and is yours to edit and save.
ALIST shows every area with its range and contents; ASTAT reads one in
detail.  See HELP ANEW.

  anew 29200 29249 The Sunken Grotto
  goto 29200                       the room is born into the Grotto
  ...build and rsave as above...

Mobiles and objects.  Inside an area of your own, MCREATE and OCREATE make
a new mobile or object at a free vnum in its range -- blank, or a copy of
any mobile or object in the game, which is the quickest start: copy the
nearest thing to what you want and change it.  ASAVE then writes the whole
area, mobiles, objects, rooms, resets and shops together.

  mcreate 29210 4444               a copy of mobile 4444
  ocreate 29220                    a blank object
  load mob 29210                   see it
  asave                            keep the lot

Rooms that came out of a shipped area file are a different matter.  Only
an implementor may change one at all, and saving over that area's file
takes a confirmation naming the file, so a stray RSAVE cannot rewrite
Hyrule because you happened to be standing in it.  The previous file is
kept beside it as a dated backup either way.

What will not save yet: river or teleport rooms, and rooms carrying a room
affect.  Those hold data outside the room
itself, and rather than write a file that has quietly lost it, RSAVE stops
and names the room.  Move your building elsewhere, or ask for the format
to be finished.

See also: RLINK, RSAVE, ASAVE, MCREATE, OCREATE, SET, GOTO, RSTAT
~

69 RLINK DIG~
Syntax: rlink <dir> <vnum>      dig an exit, and the way back
        rlink <dir> none        take the exit out again
        rlink <dir> open        a plain doorway, no door
        rlink <dir> door        a door that can be closed
        rlink <dir> pick        a door that cannot be picked
        rlink <dir> secret      a door that does not show in EXITS
        rlink <dir> key <vnum>  the object that unlocks it
        rlink <dir> name <word> what to call it: 'door gate'
        rlink <dir> desc <text> what you see looking that way

Directions: north east south west up down, and the four diagonals.

RLINK works on the room you are standing in.  Digging to a vnum nothing
uses makes that room as well, so you can lay out a run of rooms without
stopping.  The matching exit back is dug for you, unless the far room
already has one that way, or it came out of an area file and you are not
an implementor -- then the link is one way and RLINK says so.

A secret exit is one the loader recognises by its keyword, so RLINK SECRET
sets the keyword to 'secret' as well as the lock.  Add more words with
RLINK <dir> NAME SECRET <word>, keeping 'secret' first; drop it and the
exit stops being hidden the next time the area loads.

OPEN, DOOR, PICK and KEY set the way back as well, when it leads here and
is a room you may edit: a door is a door from both sides.  SECRET is left
one-sided, since a way hidden from one side only is often the point.

Nothing is written to disk until RSAVE.

See also: BUILDING, RSAVE, SET, RSTAT
~

69 RSAVE~
Syntax: rsave
        rsave confirm
        rsave confirm <filename>

RSAVE writes the rooms of the area you are standing in back to that area's
file.  Without it, everything built in game is gone at the next reboot.

RSAVE on its own writes nothing.  It reports the area, the file, and how
many rooms would be written, and tells you how to confirm.

In the builder area -- the rooms you made yourself, held in custom.are --
RSAVE CONFIRM is enough.

In an area that shipped with the game, you must be an implementor and you
must name the file: RSAVE CONFIRM HYRULE.ARE.  That is deliberate.  A room
you are standing in for some unrelated reason should not be savable by
reflex.

What gets written is the rooms as they are loaded right now, including
changes another immortal made this boot.  Mobiles, objects, resets, shops
and specials in the file are left exactly as they are.  The old file is
kept beside the new one with a timestamp, so a bad save can be undone from
the shell.

An area made with ANEW is different: it saves whole, exactly as ASAVE
does, because writing only its rooms would leave the mobiles and objects
built beside them out of the file.

See also: BUILDING, ASAVE, RLINK, SET
~

69 ANEW~
Syntax: anew <low vnum> <high vnum> <name>

ANEW makes a new buildable area that owns a block of vnums all its own,
instead of building loose in the shared workshop (custom.are).

  anew 29200 29249 The Sunken Grotto

The range must be empty -- no room, mobile or object may already use a
vnum in it -- and must not overlap another area.  Pick a free block with
RLIST, VNUM, or ALIST.  Keep an area under 5000 vnums; vnums themselves
run from 1 to 1000000.

ANEW writes a stub file, area/built/<name>.are, carrying an #AREADATA
header with the range and your name as its builder, and lists it in
built/built.lst so it loads at the next reboot, after the shipped world.
It is linked live at once, so you can GOTO a vnum in the range
straightaway: the room is born into the new area, not the workshop.  Build
as usual and ASAVE to write it.

Anyone named in the area's Builders line may build there; an implementor
may build anywhere.  ASTAT shows who.

What you save is kept.  Every few minutes the server copies the built
areas into the game's repository along with the player files, and a code
update leaves them where they are.  What you have not saved is not.

See also: BUILDING, ALIST, ASTAT, RLINK, RSAVE
~

68 ALIST~
Syntax: alist

ALIST lists every area the game has loaded: its name, its file, its vnum
range, and how many rooms, mobiles and objects it holds.  A '*' marks an
area you can build in and save with RSAVE -- the workshop and any area made
with ANEW.  An area that shipped with the game shows no range and is
editable only by an implementor.

See also: BUILDING, ANEW, ASTAT
~

68 ASTAT~
Syntax: astat
        astat <area name>

ASTAT reads one area in detail: its name, file, vnum range, the builders
allowed to work in it, its room/mobile/object counts, and whether you may
build in it.  With no argument it reads the area you are standing in.

See also: BUILDING, ANEW, ALIST, ASAVE
~

69 ASAVE~
Syntax: asave
        asave <area name>

ASAVE writes everything in an area made with ANEW to its file: the
mobiles and objects in its vnum range, its rooms, its resets, its shops and
its specials.  With no argument it saves the area you are standing in.

  Saved The Sunken Grotto: 12 rooms, 4 mobiles, 7 objects, 9 resets.

The previous file is kept beside the new one with a timestamp.  Nothing is
written if anything in the area cannot be saved faithfully -- a river or
teleport room, a room affect, a value the file format cannot hold -- and
ASAVE says which room or vnum is in the way.

A reset that names something gone (a mobile never saved, a door taken out
since) would stop the game at the next boot, so it is left out of the file
and ASAVE tells you how many it dropped.

Only an area with a vnum range of its own can be saved whole: the range is
how it knows which mobiles and objects are its.  The workshop and the
areas that shipped with the game save their rooms with RSAVE.

A room with no exits at all is made NO_MOB when the game boots, as it
always has been, so a lone room comes back with that flag set.

See also: BUILDING, ANEW, MCREATE, OCREATE, RSAVE
~

69 MCREATE~
Syntax: mcreate <vnum>
        mcreate <vnum> <vnum to copy>

MCREATE makes a new mobile.  The vnum must be free and inside the range of
an area made with ANEW that you may build in.

With one number you get a blank: a level 1 human called "a new mobile".
With two you get a copy of any mobile in the game -- its descriptions,
race, level, dice, flags, actions, special and shop, if it keeps one --
which is usually the quicker start.

  mcreate 29210 4444

The copy is its own mobile from then on; changing it changes nothing
else.  LOAD MOB <vnum> puts one in front of you.  ASAVE keeps it; until
then it is gone at the next reboot.

See also: OCREATE, ASAVE, ANEW, LOAD
~

69 OCREATE~
Syntax: ocreate <vnum>
        ocreate <vnum> <vnum to copy>

OCREATE makes a new object.  The vnum must be free and inside the range of
an area made with ANEW that you may build in.

With one number you get a blank: a piece of trash you can pick up, called
"a new object".  With two you get a copy of any object in the game --
type, flags, values, spells, affects, extra descriptions and wear actions.

  ocreate 29220 3021

LOAD OBJ <vnum> makes one; OSTAT <vnum> reads it.  ASAVE keeps it.

See also: MCREATE, ASAVE, ANEW, LOAD
~

68 MSHOW MEDIT 'SET MOB'~
Syntax: mshow <vnum>
        set mob <vnum> <field> <value>

MSHOW reads a mobile prototype in words, and SET MOB with a vnum changes
one, taking the same words MSHOW prints.  Only a mobile in an area made
with ANEW can be changed: that is what ASAVE writes, so a change anywhere
else could never be kept.  Copy a shipped mobile in with MCREATE first.

What it says
  keywords <words>        what players type to mean it: dwarf smith
  short <text>            in a sentence: a burly dwarf smith
  long <text>             in the room: A burly dwarf smith works here.
  desc <text>             what LOOK at it shows; wrapped for you
  desc + <text>           add to the description
  desc clear              empty it

What it is
  race <name>    human elf dwarf giant dragon wolf ...  A race brings its
                 natural flags with it, and they cannot be taken away:
                 every boot puts them back, so a dwarf always sees in
                 the dark.  Change the race to lose them.
  sex male | female | neutral        size tiny small medium large huge giant
  material <name>                    alignment good | neutral | evil | -1000..1000

How it fights
  level <n>      Setting a level raises the hitroll and damage bonus, and
                 lowers the armour, to the least a mobile of that level
                 gets -- the game does this at every boot anyway, so it
                 is done now and you are told.
  hitroll <n>
  hp <dice>      hit points: 4d10+80 is four ten-sided dice plus 80
  mana <dice>    damage <dice>
  attack <name>  slash pierce bite claw punch flaming_bite ...
  ac <n>         all four armour classes; lower is better
  ac pierce | bash | slash | exotic <n>

How it behaves
  position <pos> how it is found: standing sitting resting sleeping
  default <pos>  what it returns to after a fight
  wealth <n>     its coin; 0 means what a mobile of its level carries
  special <name> a special behaviour (cast_mage, breath_fire...), or none

Flags, by name.  +name adds, -name takes away, a bare name adds, NONE
clears.  Give a field alone to list its names.
  act         aggressive sentinel stay_area wimpy scavenger pet ...
  affect      sanctuary invisible detect_invis flying haste sneak ...
  offense     dodge parry bash kick trip disarm assist_all ...
  immune / resist / vulnerable    fire cold lightning poison magic ...
  form / parts                    what kind of body it has

  set mob 21003 act +aggressive -wimpy
  set mob 21003 resist +fire +cold

A shop
  shop                    makes it a shopkeeper (markup 120%, pays 80%)
  shop markup <percent>   what buyers pay, of an item's value
  shop pays <percent>     what it pays for what it buys
  shop buys <types>       up to five item types: weapon armor potion ...
  shop hours <open> <close>    0 to 23
  shop none               closes the shop
  It sells what it carries, and never runs out: PLACE OBJ <vnum> ON
  <its vnum> stocks it.

A mobile already in the world keeps the form it was made with; PURGE it
and LOAD MOB <vnum> to see the change.  ASAVE keeps the prototype.

See also: MCREATE, ASAVE, BUILDING, OSHOW
~

68 OSHOW OEDIT 'SET OBJ'~
Syntax: oshow <vnum>
        set obj <vnum> <field> <value>

OSHOW reads an object prototype in words, and SET OBJ with a vnum changes
one, taking the same words OSHOW prints.  Only an object in an area made
with ANEW can be changed; copy a shipped one in with OCREATE first.

What it says
  keywords <words>     what players type: sword runed
  short <text>         in a sentence: a runed sword
  long <text>          the line on the ground: A runed sword lies here.
  desc <text>          what LOOK at it shows
  detail <word> <text> what LOOK <word> shows: detail runes They read...
                       detail <word> + <text> adds, detail <word> none removes;
                       quote several words: detail 'runes markings' ...

What it is
  type <type>   weapon armor clothing light container drink food potion
                pill scroll wand staff portal money key treasure ...
                Changing the type clears the values: they meant something
                else to the old one.
  level <n>   weight <n>   material <name>
  cost <price>  5g 20s, 1p, 3 gold, or a number of copper
  condition     perfect good average worn damaged broken ruined
  wear          +take (can be picked up) +wield +hold +finger +neck +torso
                +head +legs +feet +hands +arms +shield +about +waist +wrist
  flags         +glow +hum +magic +bless +nodrop +noremove +invis +metal
                +anti_good +anti_evil +anti_neutral +rot_death +no_locate
                +humans_only +elves_only +dwarves_only +no_steal ...

What it does, by type
  weapon      class sword|dagger|axe|mace|spear|flail|whip|polearm|bow|exotic
              dice 3d6   attack slash   weapon +sharp +flaming +frost
              +vampiric +vorpal +two_handed
  armor       ac <n>, or ac pierce|bash|slash|exotic <n>  (higher is better)
  light       hours <n> | infinite
  container   capacity <weight>   container +closeable +closed +locked
              +pickproof   key <object vnum>
  drink       capacity <n>   amount <n> | full   liquid water|beer|wine...
              poisoned yes|no
  food        hours <n>   poisoned yes|no
  money       coins <n>   coin copper|silver|gold|platinum
  potion, pill, scroll   spell level <n>   spells 'cure light' armor
  wand, staff            spell level <n>   charges <n>   spell <spell>
  portal      portal plain|random|crystal_ball|keyed   destination <room>
              key <object vnum>  (for keyed)
  any type    v0 .. v4 <number>, the raw values, for anything else

What it gives the wearer
  affect +hitroll 2        a stat: strength dexterity intelligence wisdom
  affect ac -10            constitution hp mana moves ac hitroll damroll
  affect -hitroll          saves ...  One per stat; setting it again
  affect none              replaces it.
  grants +haste            a power while worn: haste sanctuary flying
                           invisible detect_invis infrared pass_door ...

Affects and powers cannot change while anybody is wearing one of these:
they come off the way they went on, so have it taken off first.  Objects
already in the world keep their old values; LOAD a fresh one to see a
change.  ASAVE keeps the prototype.

See also: OCREATE, ASAVE, BUILDING, MSHOW
~

68 RSHOW~
Syntax: rshow
        rshow <room vnum>

RSHOW reads a room in the words SET ROOM and RLINK take: its name and
area, its kind of ground, its flags by name, its description, each exit
with where it goes and what kind of way it is (open way, door, door that
cannot be picked, secret door), its key and keywords, its details, and how
many resets it has.  With no vnum it reads the room you are in.

See also: SET, RLINK, RESETS, MSHOW, OSHOW, BUILDING
~

69 BUILD WIZARD~
Syntax: build area
        build room
        build mob
        build obj

BUILD is the guided way to build.  It asks what you want, one question at
a time, and makes it with the same commands you could type yourself --
ANEW, RLINK, MCREATE, SET MOB, SET OBJ, PLACE, ASAVE -- so it can build
anything they can and nothing they would refuse.

  build area   asks how many rooms and what to call it, finds a free block
               of vnums, makes the area and takes you to its first room
  build room   names and describes the room you stand in, its kind of
               ground, then offers to dig a new room in any direction and
               walks you into it to build that one too
  build mob    a mobile for the area you are in: names, description, race,
               sex, level, how it fights, any other field you like, and
               whether to place it in this room
  build obj    an object: names, description, its type and what that type
               needs (a weapon's class and dice, a drink's liquid...), where
               it is worn, level, weight, price, and where to place it

Every question says what it wants and gives an example.

  Enter        keep what is there and go on
  .            ends a description (they are typed a line at a time)
  cancel       stop; what was made so far stays, ASAVE keeps it
  /<command>   run a command without leaving the build: /look, /say hi

A bad answer is refused in the command's own words and asked again.  A
description can hold any punctuation, semicolons included.

BUILD ROOM leaves you in the last room you dug, and BUILD MOB and BUILD
OBJ place what they make in the room you are standing in.

See also: BUILDING, ANEW, MSHOW, OSHOW, PLACE
~

68 PLACE RESETS UNPLACE~
Syntax: place mob <vnum> [<how many>]
        place obj <vnum>
        place obj <vnum> in <container vnum>
        place obj <vnum> on <mobile vnum>
        place obj <vnum> worn <mobile vnum>
        place door <direction> open | closed | locked
        resets
        unplace <number>

A reset is what makes something come back.  Every few minutes the game
walks an area's resets and puts each mobile, object and door back the way
they say.  PLACE adds one for the room you are standing in, and does it
once straight away so you can see it.  Only an area made with ANEW holds
resets, because ASAVE is what keeps them.

  place mob 21003           the smith stands here, and comes back if killed
  place mob 21004 3         three rats
  place obj 21010           a chest on the floor
  place obj 21011 in 21010  a ring inside that chest
  place obj 21012 worn 21003   the smith wields his hammer
  place obj 21013 on 21003     and carries a key
  place door north locked   the door north is locked again at every reset

An item for a mobile, or for a container, needs that mobile or container
placed in this room first: the reset that gives or fills follows the one
that makes it.  WORN picks the slot from the item's wear slots, the second
finger or wrist if the first is taken.  A door is set from both sides when
the far room is one you may build in.

Objects on the floor and in containers come back only when a reset finds
the area empty of players, as they always have; mobiles and doors come
back whenever the area resets.

RESETS (trust 68) lists what is placed in this room, numbered.  PLACE and
UNPLACE need trust 69.  UNPLACE <number>
takes one out -- and with a mobile its items, with a container what is
in it.  What is in the room now stays until you PURGE it.

See also: ASAVE, MCREATE, OCREATE, BUILDING
~

70 DNS~
Syntax: dns
        dns on | off
        dns list
        dns guard
        dns allow <address>
        dns <address or host name>

Host-name lookups and the connection guard.  Trust 70.

DNS alone says whether lookups are on and counts the resolver's work since
boot (asked, named, nameless, queue full, dropped, average and slowest).

DNS ON | OFF turns lookups on or off.  They run off the game loop, only for
a connection that sends a line; when a name arrives the bans are asked
again.  With lookups off, a ban on a host name never matches; a ban on an
address always does (HELP BAN).

DNS LIST shows each connection SOCKETS would show you, with its host name,
its address and its client.  A name is only shown: the address decides.

DNS GUARD and DNS ALLOW: HELP 'DNS GUARD'.

DNS <address or name> resolves one now, and blocks the whole game until
the answer comes: never point it at something that may hang.

See also: 'DNS GUARD', BAN, SOCKETS
~

70 'DNS GUARD' 'DNS ALLOW'~
Syntax: dns guard
        dns allow <address>

DNS GUARD lists the connection guard's pauses, floods and wrong-password
lockouts.  Pauses and floods live in memory: a reboot lifts them.

  - An address no player has logged in from in 90 days, nor plays from
    now, is paused for six hours after six connections in six hours that
    reach no password prompt, or twenty in a minute: doubling each time
    in a week, to seven days.  A pause refuses new characters only; a
    player's login lifts it.
  - Sixty connections in a minute is a flood: refused for an hour.
  - At most eight connections from one address may wait at the login
    prompts; while two hundred wait game-wide, untrusted addresses are
    refused.  An IPv6 address counts with its /64.
  - Never a strike: a login prompt that timed out, an MSSP query, the web
    client.  A connection that types nothing for ten minutes is closed.
  - Five wrong passwords in fifteen minutes lock an address out for 15
    minutes, then 1 hour, 6 hours, 1 day, 7 days within a week (a
    player's own address an hour at most).  Lockouts survive reboots.

DNS ALLOW <address> lifts a pause and a lockout, forgets the address's
history, and trusts it (with its /64) as a player's for 90 days.

Staff hear each pause, flood, lockout and lifted pause on WIZINFO.  Player
addresses are kept in log/player_addresses.tsv.  A shared address is an
observation, not proof that two characters are one person.  BAN is a
separate list.

See also: DNS, BAN, WIZINFO
~

70 GRANTPSI~
Syntax: grantpsi <player> [now|level] [skill1,skill2,...]

GRANTPSI gives a player psionics.  It never tells the player anything:
they find out when their powers awaken, in the same words as anybody
whose mind awakens on its own.

  grantpsi <player>          at their awakening check -- the first level
                             they gain from 18 to 21.  Past the band, it
                             waits until they reach it again (a remort).
  grantpsi <player> level    on the next level they gain, whatever it is.
  grantpsi <player> now      at once.

A comma-separated list of powers after any of these names the ones to
award; without one the usual sets are used.  Valid picks include:
psionic armor, psychic shield, mindbar, torment, ego whip, enervate,
mindblast, clairvoyance, astral walk, shift, project, telekinesis,
transfusion, nightmare, mind leech, pyrotechnics, and confuse.

The target does not have to be online: the saved character is loaded,
granted or flagged, and written back.  When the powers are actually
given, by this or by a remort, WIZINFO names them.

See also: PSIONICS, ADVANCE
~

67 FSAVE~
Syntax: fsave

This command will force all the players to save, but doesn't send them a
message when you do it.  This command is also invoked on reboots and
shutdowns.

See also: SOCKETS, PRESTORE

~

0 BRIEF~
Syntax: brief

If brief is on, you will see only short descriptions of rooms when you
enter them and when you type LOOK.  Typing brief once, turns brief on; typing
it again, turns it off, allowing you to see full descriptions.

The default is for brief to be off.

See also: COMPACT, COMBINE, PROMPT
~

0 BUY~
Syntax: buy <object>
Syntax: buy <n>.<object>
Syntax: buy <count> <object>

BUY purchases an object from a shopkeeper.  When multiple items of the
same name are available, prepend the position index: 'buy 2.sword' buys
the second sword in the list.

With a count, BUY buys that many, up to 50, one at a time: 'buy 10 fly'
buys ten potions of fly.  It stops at the first one you cannot afford or
carry, or when the shopkeeper runs out, and says how many you got.

See also: LIST, SELL, VALUE
~

0 LIST~
Syntax: list
Syntax: list <name>

LIST shows the items a shopkeeper will sell you.  With a name argument,
only items matching that name are displayed.

See also: BUY, SELL, VALUE
~

0 SELL~
Syntax: sell <object>

Sells an item to the shopkeeper here.  The sale happens at once, with no
confirmation: ask VALUE first to hear the price.  Keepers refuse what they
do not trade in and anything on a timer, and pay less for a broken piece.
The HAGGLE skill can raise the price.

See also: VALUE, BUY, LIST, REPAIR
~

0 VALUE~
Syntax: value <object>

VALUE asks a shopkeeper how much they will pay for an item.  Use this
before SELL to confirm the item is wanted and check the offered price.

See also: SELL, BUY, LIST
~

0 CAST~
Syntax: cast <spell> [target]

Casts a spell you know, for its mana.  The better you know it, the more
often it works.  Many spells pick a sensible target, such as your foe in
a fight.  A victim of an offensive spell usually gets a saving throw,
which weakens or stops it.

  - No quotes needed: the longest run of words that names a spell is the
    spell, and the rest is the target.  CAST CURE LIGHT DWARF heals the
    dwarf; CAST 'CURE LIGHT' DWARF works too.
  - Names may be shortened a word at a time (CAST CU LI), and a short
    name finds a spell you know first.
  - A slip in a name you started right is read as that spell, and a name
    the game cannot place gets the nearest of your spells suggested.

See also: SPELLS, GROUPS, GAIN, PRACTICE
~

0 BOMB BURN FEED PLAY BLOW~
Syntax: bomb <cracked surface | creature>
        burn <bush | creature>
        feed <goriya> | feed bait
        play <instrument> [level]     (or BLOW)

Hyrule's hidden ways open only to the right act with the right tool in
hand; OPEN, PICK, DOORBASH and UNLOCK will not shift them.

  BOMB  uses one bomb you carry (not in your stash; the arrow shops sell
        four for 20 gold).  It opens a cracked wall or rock; a surface
        that will not open costs nothing.  At a creature, anywhere, it is
        a blast of fire with three seconds of lag; Dodongo swallows one
        and loses a quarter of his health; the fourth finishes him.
  BURN  needs a lit candle as your light and does not use it up.  A bush
        burns away to show what it hid; a creature takes a lick of flame.
        The Blue Candle burns a creature once a room, the Red as often as
        you like and hotter.
  PLAY (or BLOW) needs the Recorder.  It drains Level 7's lake and
        shrinks a digdogger.  Elsewhere on the open land it calls a
        whirlwind to the door of any dungeon whose piece you carry, or the
        next you need: PLAY RECORDER <level> picks one.
  FEED  gives bait to the hungry Goriya.  FEED BAIT anywhere else sets it
        down: the room's enemies turn to the smell for a while, and you
        keep the bait.  Guardians are not fooled.

PUSH moves an Armos, a gravestone or a block; the warp stones also need
the Power Bracelet.

See also: 'HYRULE ITEMS', 'HYRULE SECRETS', PUSH, SHOOT
~

0 GAMBLE~
Syntax: gamble left|middle|right

GAMBLE plays the old man's money-making game in one of Hyrule's five
gambling caves, as the NES played it.  You need 40 gold.  Three coin
signs lie face down: one wins 20 or 50 gold, one loses 10, and one loses
10 or 40, laid out at random.  Pick one and all three are turned.

Over many games it pays nothing either way, a game wins at most 50 and
loses at most 40, and each costs a round of lag.  The NES asked for 10
coins, but it sent you out of the cave between games; here you play on,
so you need the worst sign's 40 and every loss is paid in full.  It is
not the casino and does not count toward the casino's achievements.

See also: HYRULE SECRETS, BET, SLOTS
~

0 HEARTS~
Syntax: hearts

Shows how many hearts you have, counted the NES way: 3 to start, one for
each guardian of Hyrule's Levels 1 to 8 you have defeated, and one for each
Heart Container you have taken from a "take any one you want" cave or the
dock east of Lake Hylia -- 16 at most.

The old man at the top of the waterfall gives the White Sword to a hero of
5 hearts, and the old man beneath the graveyard's loose gravestone gives the
Magical Sword to one of 12.

See also: HYRULE SECRETS, HYRULE ITEMS
~

0 'HYRULE SECRETS' 'TAKE ANY' 'MONEY CAVE' 'DOOR REPAIR'~
Hyrule's caves keep the old game's rules, and what pays once pays each
character once, for good:

  Money caves (14)    "It's a secret to everybody": the moblin pays 10, 30
                      or 100 gold the first time you walk in.
  Door repairs (9)    "Pay me for the door repair charge": 20 gold, once.
  Money games (5)     GAMBLE LEFT, MIDDLE or RIGHT.  See GAMBLE.
  Take any one (4)    A Heart Container or a red potion: take one, and the
                      other is refused.
  The dock heart      A Heart Container on a ledge off the eastern coast:
                      carrying the stepladder, ENTER GAP.
  White Sword         5 hearts.  Magical Sword: 12 hearts.  See HEARTS.
  Hint caves          Two old women sell hints: GIVE them gold coins (one
                      takes 10, 30 or 50, the other 5, 10 or 20).  Two old
                      men give theirs free.
  Bomb bags           The old men of Levels 5 and 7 each sell room for four
                      more bombs, 100 gold: BUY BAG.
  Potion shops        The old woman sells only to someone carrying Princess
                      Zelda's Letter, from the old man in the north-east.
  Fountains           The great fairies restore anyone not fighting.

LIST in any cave says what it offers and how to have it.

The swords, the Letter and the Power Bracelet are given one at a time:
lose yours and the cave gives you another.

See also: HEARTS, GAMBLE, 'HYRULE ITEMS', HYRULE
~

0 'HYRULE ITEMS' 'DUNGEON MAP' COMPASS 'BLUE RING' 'MAGICAL SHIELD'~
What Hyrule's things do:

  Dungeon map     LOOK or READ it: the floor plan, @ where you stand, the
                  guardian's chamber, the Triforce chest, and what the
                  guardian's door needs.
  Compass         In its own dungeon: the first step toward the Triforce
                  chest, and how many rooms away it and the guardian are.
  Bombs           BOMB a cracked wall or an enemy; each uses one.
  Bow + arrows    Wield the bow and carry a quiver: SHOOT an enemy in the
                  room.  Each arrow costs a gold coin.  An arrow kills a
                  pols voice outright, and only an arrow finishes Gohma.
  Boomerangs      Wield one and SHOOT: it stuns an enemy until struck
                  again, and kills a keese or a gel outright.
  Candles         A light; BURN a bush or an enemy.
  Bait            FEED a hungry Goriya, or FEED BAIT to calm a room.
  Recorder        PLAY it: every digdogger in the room shrinks, once.
  Magical Rod     ZAP it; it never runs dry, and with the Magic Book
                  carried its blast also burns.
  Potions         The red 2nd Potion heals twice and turns blue; the blue
                  Life Potion heals twice.
  Small keys      Each opens one locked door, and keeps through a quit.
                  The Magical Key opens them all and is never used up.
  Blue Ring       Takes a tenth off every blow (level 45).
  Magical Shield  Takes a tenth off fire and magic, but not with the
                  Mirror Shield, which does more.
  Power Bracelet  Strength while worn, and it moves the warp stones.
  Raft            ENTER RAFT to the Isle of the Snake, and from the Eastern
                  Dock of Lake Hylia to the Lonely Isle.
  Stepladder      The coast's Heart Container ledge (ENTER GAP), and Level
                  5's moat.

A dungeon's own things -- its map, its compass and the small keys found
in it -- crumble to dust when you leave it, however you leave: walking
out, recall, a portal, a death.  They are found again on your next visit.
What the map showed you stays on your map (MAPS, and the Mudlet map).
The small keys the shops sell are kept, and so is a guardian's key until
its chest has given you its prize.  Quitting inside a dungeon keeps them.

Guardians' drops: HELP 'HYRULE GUARDIANS'.

See also: BOMB, SHOOT, HEARTS, 'HYRULE SECRETS', HYRULE
~

0 CLIMB~
Syntax: climb <object>

In order to get between certain rooms, you must CLIMB a rope, ladder or
other object.  Read room descriptions carefully to find the object you need
to climb.

See also: JUMP, CRAWL, EXITS
~

0 OPEN CLOSE~
Syntax: open  <object|direction>
Syntax: close <object|direction>

OPEN opens a door or container.  CLOSE shuts it again.  Both commands
accept either a direction (to act on that exit's door) or an object name
(to open a container in the room or your inventory).  In a room with more
than one door, name the door and its direction together -- OPEN DOOR EAST
-- or just the direction.

A direction may be shortened to any start of its name, as the movement
commands take one: OP WES opens the west door, OPEN SO the south one.
Where a start fits more than one direction the plain one wins (NO is
north, SO is south); a diagonal is NE, NW, SE or SW, or long enough to
tell apart (NORTHE).  A direction with a door is that door, even when
something you carry also begins with the same letters.

See also: LOCK, UNLOCK, PICK, DOORBASH
~

0 LOCK UNLOCK~
Syntax: lock   <object|direction>
Syntax: unlock <object|direction>

LOCK secures a closed door or container.  UNLOCK removes the lock.  Both
require the appropriate key in your inventory.  Without a key, characters
with the PICK LOCK skill can bypass the lock with the PICK command.
A direction may be shortened as for OPEN: UNLOCK WE is the west door.

See also: OPEN, CLOSE, PICK, DOORBASH
~

0 COLOR~
Syntax:  color
         color on
         color off
         color list
         color default
         color <group> <color>

COLOR alone shows whether color is on and the color of each group of
messages: regular text, gossips, shouts, tells, says, socials, room
names, damage and the rest.

COLOR ON and COLOR OFF turn color on and off; your choice is kept when
you log out. COLOR DEFAULT puts every group back to its usual color.

COLOR LIST shows every color you can choose, each in its own color.
There are forty: the sixteen every client shows, and twenty-four more
-- orange, gold, purple, pink, teal, crimson and others -- for clients
with 256 colors, which nearly all are today, Mudlet and the web client
included. Pick one by name, or the start of its name:

  color gossips orange
  color tell sky
  color room lav

PLAIN shows a group in your client's ordinary text color.

See also: PROMPT, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 COMBINE~
Syntax: combine
Syntax: combine <item>

If combine is on, you will see like items in your inventory combined on
a single line.  The number of items of that type you have will be shown in
parentheses in front of the item.

For example, if, with combine off, you see:
a big pot pie
a big pot pie
a big pot pie

with combine on you would see:
(3) a big pot pie

If combine is on, typing combine again turns it off, showing you the long
inventory again.  The default is for combine to be on.

COMBINE <item> makes something whole from its parts instead.  The one
recipe is COMBINE TRIFORCE, which joins the nine Hyrule Triforce pieces,
carried in hand or loose in your inventory, into The Triforce.
See HYRULE.

See also: INVENTORY, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 ACHIEVEMENT ACHIEVEMENTS~
Syntax: achievements                    your summary and latest five
        achievements <category>         one category, with progress
        achievements earned | incomplete | all
        achievements <word or phrase>   search
        achievements <view> <part>      a later part of a long view

Achievements are lasting marks of what your character has done.  They
earn points, which are also marks to spend with REDEEM (spending never
lowers your points).  They give no combat power.

Categories: CHARACTER, COMBAT, ENCOUNTERS, QUESTS, EXPLORATION,
COLLECTION, CRAFTING, MISADVENTURE, ECONOMY, and HYRULE.

  - A hidden achievement shows nothing, and no search finds it, until
    you earn it.
  - Level, remort, play time, bank and money milestones count what you
    already had.  Lifetime kills, quests and deaths count from when the
    game began recording them.
  - A boss's credit is shared with your group in its room.
  - SCORE shows your total.

Example: achievements combat

See also: REDEEM, SCORE
~

0 REDEEM MARKS~
Syntax: redeem
Syntax: redeem recall
Syntax: redeem trophy <achievement>
Syntax: redeem stash
Syntax: redeem restring <weapon> <new name>

Every achievement point you earn is also a mark. REDEEM spends marks on
things that change how your character looks or save you time -- never on
stats, coin, trains or the power of your gear. Spending marks never lowers
your achievement points.

  RECALL     10 marks   move your recall point again now, without waiting
  TROPHY     25 marks   a keepsake trophy naming an achievement you earned
  STASH      40 marks   25 more stash slots, up to the usual 500
  RESTRING  100 marks   a new name for a weapon you carry

A new weapon name runs from 4 to 45 plain characters, takes no colour, and
must keep one of the weapon's old keywords so you can still GET and WIELD it.

See also: ACHIEVEMENTS, STASH, RECALL
~

0 COMMANDS~
Syntax: commands

COMMANDS shows you all the commands in the game.

See also: KEYWORDS, HELP, SOCIALS
~

0 COMPACT~
Syntax: compact

When COMPACT mode is on, your actions and the game responses are shown to
you without spaces between the lines.  Typing compact once turns compact
mode on; typing it again, removes compact mode, showing blank spaces between
lines again.

The default is for compact to be off.

See also: BRIEF, COLOR, COMBINE
~

0 COMPARE~
Syntax: compare <item>              against what you wear in its slot
        compare <item> <item>       two items against each other
        compare upgrades [slot]     the best you could wear today
        compare profile             how your damage is made
        compare defense ...         rank by toughness instead

COMPARE tells you which gear makes you hit harder, worked out for your
level, skills, stats, race and the rest of what you wear.

  - Damage comes first: weapon damage a round for fighters, mana to cast
    with for casters, a blend for hybrids.  Toughness counts a quarter as
    much.
  - For rings, amulets and bracers it compares against the weaker of the
    pair you wear.  It says when you cannot use an item yet, and why.
  - UPGRADES draws on gear creatures carry or sell, chests hold and
    Hyrule's guardians drop (with how often); with a slot, the top five
    and where to get them.

The website's Gear Finder ranks the same way by class and level.  To
settle it by hitting something, see DUMMY.

See also: DUMMY, LORE, EQUIPMENT, SCORE
~

0 CONSIDER~
Syntax: consider <character>

Sizes up a fight before you start it: their level against yours, their
hit points against yours, about how many rounds each of you would need to
bring the other down, and a verdict from "You should win easily" to "You
would almost certainly die".  It also names sanctuary, unnatural speed, a
foe that attacks on sight, one already fighting, and a player killer.

The rounds count weapons and armour only, not spells, dodging or fleeing,
so treat them as a guide.  Shopkeepers, trainers and anyone nobody may
fight are not rated.  It is never an attack, so it works anywhere.

See also: SCORE, WIMPY, FLEE, COMPARE
~

0 COUNT~
Syntax: count

Shows how many players you can see in the game, and the most there have
been today if that is more.

See also: WHO, WHERE
~

0 CREDITS~
Syntax: credits

This command shows the list of the original Diku Mud implementors.

See also: DIKU, MERC, ROM
~

0 DELETE DELET~
Syntax: delete
        delete <password>

Deletes your character for good.  Type DELETE alone first: it warns you
and asks for your password.  Then DELETE <password> deletes you and ends
your session.  A wrong password, or anything typed after DELETE before it
has asked, cancels.  DELET on its own does nothing but tell you to type the
whole word.  Nobody can force you to delete.

The name becomes free for others.  The game keeps a copy of the character
as it stood, so staff can bring back a deletion that was a mistake -- ask
them quickly, before somebody else takes the name.  If that copy cannot be
written safely first, nothing is deleted: you are told, and you can try
again in a moment.  You cannot delete yourself while you lie dying.

See also: QUIT, PASSWORD, GODS
~

0 DESCRIPTION DESC~
Syntax: description <string>
Syntax: description + <string>

Sets your long description to the given string.  If the description string
starts with a '+', the part after the '+' is appended to your current
description, so that you can make multi-line descriptions.

See also: TITLE, WHO, SCORE
~

0 DONATE~
Syntax: donate <object>

If you have items in your inventory that you no longer need, you may
wish to donate them for the use of other adventurers.  The donate command
allows you to do this even if you are far away from the Temple of Devota.
The donated item will be placed randomly in one of the donation pits -
either in the Temple of Devota or in one of the guild halls in Dresden.

See also: DROP, SACRIFICE, JUNK
~

0 DRINK~
Syntax: drink <object>

DRINK consumes a liquid from a drink container to quench your thirst.
Characters who become dehydrated suffer regeneration penalties, and left
parched they lose hit points each tick and cannot sleep (see HUNGER).  Use
FILL to refill empty drink containers from a fountain or pool.

See also: EAT, FILL, QUAFF, HUNGER
~

0 EAT~
Syntax: eat <object>

EAT consumes a food item to satisfy your hunger.  Characters who become
hungry suffer regeneration penalties, and left starving they lose hit
points each tick and cannot sleep (see HUNGER).  Some foods apply
temporary magical or status effects when eaten.

Staff can eat anything they carry, which destroys it on the spot, and
are never too full to.  An endless meal is still not used up.

See also: DRINK, FILL, HUNGER
~

0 HUNGER THIRST STARVING PARCHED~
Hunger and thirst

You grow hungry and thirsty as the ticks pass, and EAT and DRINK put that
right.  While you are hungry or thirsty you heal more slowly.

Left long enough, they begin to hurt.  Every tick you spend starving takes
a share of your maximum hit points -- 2% the first tick, a point more each
tick in a row, up to 8% -- and every tick parched takes 3%, rising to 10%.
The game tells you which each tick.  It never takes you below one hit
point: hunger kills nobody, but it leaves you easy prey.  Eating or
drinking starts the count over.

You are too hungry or too thirsty to SLEEP while starving or parched, and
hunger or thirst wakes a sleeper.  A magical sleep holds.

Nothing is taken while you have lost your link, are idling in the void or
stand in the Oracle's sanctum.  Heroes, characters who have remorted and
staff never hunger or thirst.

See also: EAT, DRINK, FILL, SLEEP, REMORT GIFTS
~

0 FILL~
Syntax: fill <object>

FILL refills a drink container with fresh water from a nearby fountain
or pool.  You must be in a room with an accessible water source.

See also: DRINK, EAT
~

0 DROP~
Syntax: drop <object>
        drop all
        drop all.<keyword>
        drop <amount> <copper|silver|gold|platinum>

Puts things on the ground here.  Coins need their kind named in full.

Example: drop 20 gold

See also: GET, PUT, GIVE, DONATE
~

0 GET TAKE~
Syntax: get <object>
Syntax: get <object> <container>
Syntax: take <object>
Syntax: get all
Syntax: get all.<keyword>
Syntax: get <number>.<keyword> [container]

GET (or TAKE) picks up an object from the ground, from a container, or
from a corpse.  'get all' picks up everything available; 'get all.sword'
takes all items named "sword".  With several alike, a number picks one:
'get potion bag' takes the first potion listed, 'get 2.potion bag' the
second, and so on.

See also: DROP, PUT, INVENTORY
~

0 GIVE~
Syntax: give <object> <character>
        give <amount> <copper|silver|gold|platinum> <character>

Hands an object or coins to someone in the room who can carry it.  Coins
need their kind named in full.

Example: give 5 silver guard

See also: DROP, SPLIT, DONATE
~

0 PUT~
Syntax: put <object> <container>

PUT places an object into a container.  The container must be in your
inventory or the room.  The object must fit within the container's weight
and size limits.

See also: GET, DROP, INVENTORY
~

0 EMOTE ,~
Syntax: emote <action>
Syntax: , <action>

EMOTE broadcasts a custom action to everyone in your current room.  The
comma (,) is a shortcut for EMOTE.  Unlike fixed socials, EMOTE lets you
type any free-form action.

Example:  emote grins wickedly.    or    ,grins wickedly.

See also: POSE, SOCIAL, SAY
~

0 POSE~
Syntax: pose

POSE displays a random, class-appropriate emote selected automatically by
the game.  Each class has its own set of poses.  Use POSE for quick flavor
text without typing a full EMOTE message.

See also: EMOTE, SOCIAL
~

0 SOCIAL SOCIALS~
Syntax: socials
        <social>
        <social> <character>

SOCIALS lists every social: a ready-made action such as SMILE, WAVE or
BOW that the room sees.  Type its name alone, or with someone's name to
aim it at them.  EMOTE writes your own.

Example: wave guard

See also: EMOTE, POSE
~

0 EQUIPMENT~
Syntax: equipment

Lists all items you currently have equipped: worn armor, wielded weapons,
held items, and any light source.  Each item is shown at its wear location.
Use INVENTORY to see items you are carrying but not yet equipped.

See also: INVENTORY, WEAR, WIELD, HOLD, REMOVE
~

0 INVENTORY~
Syntax: inventory

Lists all items you are carrying in your pack but not currently equipped.
Use COMBINE to view stacked identical items as a single line.  Use
EQUIPMENT to see what you have worn or wielded.

See also: EQUIPMENT, COMBINE, COMPARE, LORE
~

0 LOOK READ~
Syntax: look                        the room
        look room                   the full room, even with BRIEF on
        look <character|object>
        look <direction>
        look <keyword>              something the room mentions
        look in <container|corpse>
        read <object>

LOOK alone describes the room; looking at a character shows their
description and what they wear.  READ is LOOK, except for things that
can be read: a dungeon map shows its floor plan, and a dungeon compass,
inside its own dungeon, points toward the Triforce chest and says how many
rooms away it and the guardian's chamber are.  With several, name one:
READ EAGLE COMPASS.

See also: EXAMINE, SCAN, 'HYRULE ITEMS'
~

0 EXAMINE~
Syntax: examine <container>
Syntax: examine <corpse>

EXAMINE is shorthand for 'LOOK <container>' followed by 'LOOK IN
<container>'.  It shows the container itself and then lists all of its
contents in one step.  Particularly useful for inspecting corpses and packs.

See also: LOOK, INVENTORY, GET
~

0 EXCHANGE~
Syntax: exchange

If you are a hero, you may exchange experience for more practices.  The cost
is 5000 experience points.  This means that if you would rather get more
practices rather than another level, exchange the experience before leveling.
Otherwise you'll have to get 5000 more experience up and above your new level.
Exchanging experience for practices gives you 5-7 more; the exact number of
practices you get is random.  You may exchange only at the guru in Hero Hall.

See also: PRACTICE, GAIN, TRAIN, WORTH
~

0 EXITS~
Syntax: exits

Tells you the visible exits of the room you are in.  Closed doors are shown
in parentheses.  Not all exits are visible;  room descriptions will generally
provide some clues as to the existence of hidden exits and their names.

See also: AUTOEXIT, SEARCH, LOOK
~

0 FLEE~
Syntax: flee

Tries to escape the fight you are in through a random exit; it can fail.
A successful flight costs 10 experience.  RECALL from a fight costs 25 and
is a roll on your recall skill, and a friend can RESCUE you instead.  WIMPY
makes you flee by yourself when your hit points fall low.

If you lose your link in a fight, you fight on and now and then try to
recall.

See also: WIMPY, RESCUE, RECALL
~

0 FLIP MOVE PULL PUSH TURN~
Syntax: flip <object>
        move <object>
        pull <object>
        push <object>
        turn <object>

Some rooms contain objects which need to be manipulated by flipping, moving,
pushing, pulling or turning them.  Manipulating these objects can have
various effects.  For example, one might push a lever or flip a switch to
open a secret door.  Read room descriptions carefully in order to find
objects that you can manipulate.

See also: LOOK, SEARCH, OPEN, CLIMB
~

0 FOLLOW GROUP ~
Syntax: follow <character>
        follow self
        group
        group <character>

FOLLOW makes you go where someone goes; FOLLOW SELF stops.  NOFOLLOW
stops others following you.

GROUP <character> takes someone who is following you into your group,
or out of it again; GROUP alone shows your group.  Members must be
within five levels of you.  They share experience from kills and may use
GTELL and SPLIT; AUTOASSIST makes you join a fight one of them is in.

See also: GTELL, SPLIT, NOFOLLOW, AUTOASSIST
~

0 GAIN~
Syntax: gain list
        gain <skill or group>
        gain convert

The gain command is used to learn new skills, once the proper trainer has
been found.  The following options can be used with gain:

gain list:    list all groups and skills that can be learned
gain <name>:  add a skill or skill group, at the listed cost
gain convert: turns 10 practices into one training session

Gain uses training sessions, not practices. (see 'help train').

Read the helps on the various spellgroups and skills to determine what
you want to gain.  The helps will show you the minimum level you need to be
to use a skill or the lowest level spell in a spellgroup.

See also: TRAIN, PRACTICE, GROUPS, TEACHLIST, GAINLIST
~

0 GROUPS~
Syntax: groups

The GROUPS command shows which spell groups you have (both default and
groups that you have gained).

See also: GAIN, SKILLS, SPELLS
~

0 STOP~
Syntax: stop
        stop walk

Ends a walk WALKTO is making for you, as WALKTO STOP does.  A walk your
client makes, typing the steps itself, is the client's to stop: the Times
of Chaos Mudlet package stops its own walk on STOP, STOP WALK or WALK
STOP.

See also: WALKTO, MUDLET
~

0 HEAL~
Syntax: heal
        heal <spell>

At a healer, such as the one by the Temple's pit, HEAL alone lists what is
offered and the price, and HEAL <spell> buys it.  HEAL HEAL, HEAL MANA
and HEAL REFRESH buy as many castings as you need to fill that pool, at
one casting's price each, as far as your purse goes; a full pool is sold
nothing.  Where Hermie stands, HEAL asks her instead, free (HELP BUFF).

The cleric spell is HEALSPELL.

See also: BUFF, SCORE, AFFECT
~

0 HELP~
Syntax: help
Syntax: help <keyword>

HELP without any arguments shows a one-page command summary.

HELP <keyword> shows a page of help on that keyword.  The keywords include
all the commands, spells, and skills listed in the game.  Type HELP KEYWORDS
for an alphabetical listing of all keywords.  A word with no help names
any topic within a letter or two of it, so a typo finds its way.

See also: KEYWORDS, COMMANDS, SOCIALS
~

0 WEAR~
Syntax: wear <object>
Syntax: wear all

WEAR equips a piece of armor or clothing at its appropriate body slot.
You may not be able to wear an item if its alignment does not match yours,
it is too heavy for you, or you are not experienced enough.  WEAR ALL
attempts to equip every suitable item in your inventory at once.

See also: WIELD, HOLD, REMOVE, EQUIPMENT
~

0 WIELD~
Syntax: wield <object>

WIELD equips a weapon in your main hand.  Characters with the SECONDARY
skill can equip a second weapon in the off hand using the SECONDARY
command.

See also: WEAR, HOLD, REMOVE, SECONDARY, DUAL WIELD
~

0 HOLD~
Syntax: hold <object>

Holds an item in your hand: a wand, a staff, or anything else made to be
held.  HOLD on a light puts it in your light slot instead.

See also: WEAR, WIELD, ZAP, BRANDISH, REMOVE
~

0 REMOVE~
Syntax: remove <object>
Syntax: remove all

REMOVE takes an equipped item and puts it back into your inventory.
REMOVE ALL strips all your gear except for cursed items, which cannot
be removed until the curse is lifted.

See also: WEAR, WIELD, HOLD, EQUIPMENT
~

0 JOIN~
Syntax: join
        join <mage|cleric|thief|warrior>

At Melancholy the guild clerk (WALKTO MELANCHOLY), joins a guild.  JOIN
alone tells you the fee: one gold coin.

  - From level 3 until you reach level 6, and only if you have never been
    in a guild: the choice is made once.
  - A mage, cleric, thief or warrior still in no guild on reaching level 6
    is put in their own class's guild, free, with its starting group.
    From then on the choice is over until a REMORT.
  - Monks and necromancers have their own guild and join no other.
  - Before you choose: HELP GUILDS, and GAINLIST PREVIEW <class> <guild>
    to see a build (GAINLIST PREVIEW WARRIOR THIEF).

A castle head adds castle members with JOIN <player>: see CASTLE.

See also: GUILDS, GAINLIST, CASTLE
~

0 KILL~
Syntax: kill <name of mob>

The KILL command starts a fight with a mobile (non-player character).  You
need to type kill only once per fight.  Attacking player characters
requires MURDER instead.

See also: MURDER, HIT, FLEE, WIMPY
~

0 PKILL~
Syntax: pkill <password>

Makes you a player killer, for good.  From level 15; your password
confirms it.  Player killers may MURDER one another; anyone else fights
players only in the arena.

  - CONSIDER shows ***PKILLER*** on one; SCORE shows your own.
  - Attacking a player who is not WANTED, outside the arena, makes you
    WANTED, and a WANTED corpse is anybody's to loot.  PKILL alone never
    makes yours lootable.
  - You cannot undo it.  Staff can, and a remort clears it.
  - Castles take only player killers.

See also: MURDER, TOPTEN, CASTLE, ARENA
~

0 NOFOLLOW  NOFOL~
Syntax: nofollow       (or NOFOL)

Turns on or off whether others may follow you.  Turning it on sends away
everyone following you, your pet and raised undead included, and ends
your group.  To be grouped you must accept followers.  It is off for new
characters; AUTO shows how it stands.

See also: FOLLOW, GROUP, AUTO
~

0 NOLOOT~
Syntax: noloot

Turns on or off whether others may take things from your corpse, or pick
it up.  It is on for new characters, so nobody can loot you; turn it off
if you want a friend to bring your things back.  AUTO shows how it
stands.

Every death turns it back on, so give leave after the death you want
help with.  Leave counts only while you are in the game: logged out or
link-dead, your corpse is yours and the staff's alone, and no monster or
follower ever takes from it.  HELP DEATH has the whole rule.

See also: AUTO, NOSUMMON, NOFOLLOW, DEATH
~

0 NOSUMMON~
Syntax: nosummon

Turns on or off your protection from other players' SUMMON, GATE and
CANCEL.  It is on by default, and while it is on REMOVE CURSE cannot lift
a curse from your gear either, whoever casts it.  Turn it off only for
friends you trust.  AUTO shows how it stands.

See also: AUTO, NOLOOT, NOFOLLOW
~

0 NOTE NOTES~
Syntax: note                         your notes (N marks unread)
        note unread                  only the unread ones
        note read [number]           one note; alone, the next unread
        note search <word>           by sender, subject or text
        note catchup                 mark them all read
        note remove <number>         one you wrote or that is to you
        note reply <number>          start a reply, subject Re: theirs
        note forward <number> <player>

Writing:  note to <names | immortal | all | castle name>
          note subject <text>
          note + <line>        add a line
          note -               take off the last line
          note show | clear    see or discard what you have
          note post            send it (also NOTE SEND)

A note's number is its place in your full list, in UNREAD and SEARCH
too, so the number you see is the one to read.  At login you are told how
many are unread.

See also: BUG, IDEA, TYPO, IGNORE
~

0 ORDER~
Syntax: order <character> <command>
Syntax: order all <command>

ORDER orders one or all of your charmed followers (including pets) to
perform any command.  The command may have arguments.  A follower that
somebody else is controlling answers only to them.  You are responsible
for the actions of your followers, and others who attack your followers
will incur the same penalty as if they attacked you directly.

Most charmed creatures lose their aggressive nature (while charmed).  However,
they will become aggressive again when the charm wears off.  For that reason,
charmed aggressive mobs should not be brought outside the areas they are
normally found in.

See also: GROUP, FOLLOW, CHARM PERSON
~

0 OUTFIT ISSUE SUBISSUE REISSUE 'SUB ISSUE'~
Syntax: outfit

The outfit command, usable by levels 5 and below, equips your character with
sub issue gear (banner, vest, shield and your class's weapon), courtesy of
the Mayor's warehouses.  Only empty equipment slots are affected, and a
matching piece already in your pack is put on rather than a new one made,
so running it again gives you nothing twice.  Monks get no weapon.

ISSUE, REISSUE and SUBISSUE do the same.

See also: EQUIPMENT, WEAR
~

0 PASSWORD~
Syntax: password <old password> <new password>

Changes your password; it is never logged.  A new password is at least
five characters long, and every character counts.

  - A password set before November 2026 still accepts only its first
    eight letters, as it always did, until you choose a new one.
  - Five wrong passwords at login within fifteen minutes lock your
    address out for fifteen minutes; each further lockout within a week
    is longer: an hour, six hours, a day, a week.  An address you play
    from is never locked out for more than an hour.
  - At login you are told of failed attempts on your character since you
    were last on.  If they were not yours, change your password.

A forgotten password cannot be mailed to you: an immortal can reset it.

See also: ACCOUNT, DELETE
~

0 PRACTICE~
Syntax: practice
        practice <skill|spell> [count|max]

PRACTICE alone shows how well you know each skill and spell; it works
anywhere.  At a guildmaster, PRACTICE <name> spends a session raising it,
by more the higher your intelligence.  A count spends several at once;
MAX spends what it takes to reach your adept level.  Above 1% a skill also
improves with use.  Wisdom gives more sessions a level, and unspent ones
are kept.

Example: practice dodge 5

See also: PRACTICELIST, TRAIN, GAIN, SKILLS
~

0 SAVE~
Syntax: save

Saves your character now.  The game also saves you about every five
minutes and when you quit, so you seldom need it; it costs a few seconds
of lag.

Some things are never saved, and are gone at your next login:
  - keys, except Hyrule's small keys (a dungeon's own crumble when you
    leave it: HELP 'HYRULE ITEMS')
  - a blank map
  - an active quest's recovery item

See also: QUIT, STASH
~

0 RENT~
Syntax: (not applicable)

There is no RENT in this game.  Simply SAVE and QUIT when you want to
leave; your character and equipment are stored automatically.  You will
return to the same room next time you log in.

See also: QUIT, SAVE
~

0 RECALL /~
Syntax: recall            (or /)
        recall set        make this room your recall point
        recall default    put it back at the Temple
        recall where      where it is, and when you may move it

Prays to be carried to your recall point: the Temple of Devota in Dresden
until you move it.

  - It is a roll on your recall skill, which every class holds at 100%
    from level 1; now and then it goes unanswered, and about one time in
    twenty-five it lands you somewhere else.  From the third remort it
    always works and always goes home.
  - It halves your movement, except out of a fight from level 58.  From a
    fight it also costs 25 experience.
  - Not from a no-recall room, a jail, or while you are cursed.
  - Your pet comes with you; a mount is left behind.
  - RECALL SET works once every 30 minutes, not in or just after a fight,
    and not in a no-recall room, jail or death trap.  RECALL WHERE says
    when you may move it next.  RECALL DEFAULT works at any time.

WORD OF RECALL, its scrolls and potions, and the Recall Ring always go to
the Temple, and work while you are cursed.

See also: 'WORD OF RECALL', REDEEM, RESTED
~

0 REMORT~
Syntax: remort <password> <class> <guild> <race>

Starts your character over at level 3 in a new class, keeping the name.
Each remort lets you reach one level higher, to 59 after the fifth.

  - When: at level 54 for the first remort, 55 for the second, up to 58
    for the fifth and last.  Not from a jail cell, nor during an
    automatic quest.
  - You become level 3 in the Temple with every stat at 13, and lose your
    spells, skills, groups and affects; your player-killer flag is
    cleared.
  - You keep your coin, bank, quest points, items and stash.  Gear level
    3 cannot wear goes into a bag in your stash; NODROP and timed items
    stay in your pack.  Empty light, body, shield and weapon slots get
    the newcomer's outfit.
  - Your pet comes too; other charmed followers leave, and raised undead
    crumble.  A title you chose is kept; a class title is replaced.

Example: remort mypassword thief warrior elf

See also: 'REMORT GIFTS', 'REMORT CLASSES', HEROLEVELS, SHADOWMELD
~

0 'REMORT GIFTS'~
Each remort keeps the gifts of those before it:

  1st  Hunger and thirst no longer touch you.
  2nd  Psionic powers: one from each of the four disciplines.
  3rd  A second power from each discipline; twice the carrying room;
       twice as long before the idle void takes you; RECALL never fails
       (from a fight it still costs experience).
  4th  A third power from each discipline, and SHADOWMELD at 50%.
  5th  Every psionic power, no carrying limit, and a free choice of class.

Psionic powers are never lost: each life hands them back at 1%, to
practise again.  Each opens at its own level, from 17 to 28; until then
you hold it but cannot use or practise it.

  Remort  At level  New max  Start HP/Mana/Move  +Pracs  +Trains  Per level
    1st       54       55          200 each         17      10       +1
    2nd       55       56          400 each         19      12       +2
    3rd       56       57          600 each         21      14       +3
    4th       57       58          800 each         23      16       +4
    5th       58       59         1000 each         25      18       +5

Unspent practices and trains are kept, and the new life's are added.  "Per
level" is added to hit points, mana and movement at each level of that
life.

See also: REMORT, PSIONICS, SHADOWMELD
~

0 'REMORT CLASSES'~
Every life must be a class you have not lived before, until the fifth
remort, which lets you choose any; a refused class is answered with the
list still open to you.  The guild is free: any, as often as you like, or
none.

  Classes  mage, cleric, thief, warrior, monk, necro
  Guilds   mage, cleric, thief, warrior, none
           (monks and necros have no guild and must give none)
  Races    human, elf, dwarf, hobbit, saurian
           (monks may be dwarf or human; necros elf or human)

Class and guild may be shortened to two letters or more.  A mage, cleric,
thief or warrior who picks none is put in their class's guild, free, at
level 6.

See also: REMORT, CLASS, GUILDS, GAINLIST
~

0 AFFECT AFFECTS~
Syntax: affect

Shows all magical and psionic effects currently active on your character.
For each effect, the entry shows the spell or skill name, the modifier it
applies, the stat or attribute affected, and how many ticks remain.  A
duration of "permanent" means the effect does not expire.

Some effects change no statistic at all - a psionic ward such as MINDBAR
is a named protection and a duration and nothing else.  Those show the
name and how long is left.

Gear that grants a spell while worn is listed too, naming the item: the
Red Ring's sanctuary, the Master Sword's haste.  Wearing the Triforce
lists 'holylight' with the level it sees at.

See also: SCORE, IDENTIFY, ATTRIBUTE
~

0 ATTRIBUTE ATTRIBUTES STATS STRENGTH INTELLIGENCE WISDOM DEXTERITY CONSTITUTION DEX~
Syntax: attribute

Displays a quick summary of your five core attributes: Strength,
Intelligence, Wisdom, Dexterity, and Constitution.  Values are shown as
base(current) to separate your trained score from bonuses applied by
equipment or active spells.

See also: SCORE, AFFECT, TRAIN
~

0 REPORT~
Syntax: report

Tells you and the room your hit points, mana and endurance, current and
maximum, and your experience:

  You say 'I have 120/150 hp 80/100 mana 90/90 end 5230 xp.'

See also: SCORE, WORTH, GROUP
~

0 SCORE~
Syntax: score

Displays your full character sheet: name, level, class, race, all
attributes, hit points, mana, movement, armor class, hitroll, damroll,
experience, gold, remort count, and more.  Attribute scores are shown as
trained(current) so you can see exactly how much bonus gear and spells
are contributing.  Only you can see your own SCORE output.

See also: ATTRIBUTE, AFFECT, WORTH, REPORT
~

0 WORTH~
Syntax: worth

Shows the coins you carry, platinum to copper, your experience and how
much more you need for the next level.  Your bank balance is not in it:
BALANCE shows that in a bank.  SCORE shows the whole sheet.

See also: SCORE, BALANCE, EXPERIENCE
~

0 SLEEP~
Syntax: sleep

SLEEP puts your character into a deep slumber.  While asleep you
regenerate hit points, mana, and movement at the fastest possible rate,
but you are more vulnerable to attack and will not hear most events in
the room.  Use WAKE or STAND to get up.

You cannot sleep while you are starving or parched: eat and drink first.
Hunger or thirst reaching nothing wakes you (see HUNGER).

For information on the sleep spell, see SLEEPSPELL.

See also: REST, STAND, WAKE, WIMPY, HUNGER
~

0 REST STAND WAKE SIT~
Syntax: rest
Syntax: stand
Syntax: wake
Syntax: sit

These commands change your position.  When you REST or SIT, you regenerate
hit points, mana, and movement faster than while standing, while remaining
aware of your surroundings.

STAND or WAKE returns you to a standing position.  You can also use WAKE
to rouse a sleeping character.

See also: SLEEP, FLEE, WIMPY
~

0 RUN~
Syntax: run <direction> [distance]

RUN moves repeatedly in one direction.  Distance is optional; it defaults to
30 and is capped at 30.  Movement can stop early at a wall, closed exit, trap,
combat, or any other condition that prevents normal movement.  All ten normal
directions and their abbreviations are accepted.

See also: SPEEDWALK, RECALL, AUTOEXIT, EXITS
~

0 SPEEDWALK~
Syntax: speedwalk <route>

SPEEDWALK follows compact lowercase direction tokens.  Put an optional count
before each direction; each count defaults to 1 and is capped at 30.

Examples: speedwalk 3n2e1s
          speedwalk 2ne4w1d

Valid tokens are n, e, s, w, u, d, ne, nw, se, and sw.  Movement still uses
normal exits and costs, and stops if combat begins or you cannot continue.

See also: RUN, EXITS, AUTOEXIT
~

0 SACRIFICE~
Syntax: sacrifice <object>      (also JUNK)

Offers an object lying on the ground here to the gods, who destroy it and
pay you twice its level in copper: no more than it is worth, except for a
corpse.  With AUTOSPLIT on, the coin is shared with your group.  A
player's corpse that still holds anything is refused.

See also: JUNK, AUTOSAC, DONATE
~

0 SCAN~
Syntax: scan

The SCAN command tells you what mobs are in surrounding rooms.  Only visible
mobs will be shown, unless you are affected by the appropriate detection
spells.  You cannot scan through a closed door.

See also: SEARCH, TRACK, EXITS
~

0 SCROLL~
Syntax: scroll
Syntax: scroll <number>

This command changes the number of lines the mud sends you in a page (the
default is 24 lines).  Change this to a higher number for larger screen
sizes, or to 0 to disable paging.

See also: BRIEF, COMPACT, PROMPT
~

70 FARSLAY~
Syntax: farslay <player>

A god's Farslayer: it never misses, never backfires on the caster and
reaches anywhere.  It works on anyone except the divinely warded, who turn
the blade back on whoever casts it.  For the scroll mortals buy, HELP
SCROLL OF FARSLAY.

See also: SLAY, ORACLEADMIN
~

0 'SCROLL OF FARSLAY' FARSLAY VENGEANCE VENGENCE~
Syntax: recite farslay <character>
        recite scroll <character>

The Scroll of Farslay is bought from the quest master for 600 quest points
(AQUEST BUY FARSLAY); a rare scribed scroll carries the same rite, called
vengeance.  It seeks one named character anywhere in the world.

It refuses before costing anything when you name yourself, somebody under
divine protection (the blade turns on you), or a creature nobody may harm.
Otherwise Farslayer has an even chance to kill the target or turn back and
kill the reader -- and either way a mortal reader pays: left stunned at one
hit, mana and movement point; maximum hit points, mana and movement each
reduced for good, by twice your level or 25, whichever is more; and one
attribute reduced by 3 for good.  Striking the target makes you WANTED.

The scroll is consumed even if the recitation fails, but naming nobody, or
somebody it cannot find, does not consume it.  Farslay cannot finish Ganon
or Gohma; it lands one hard blow on them instead, and Ganon still needs the
Silver Arrow.

See also: AQUEST, RECITE, WANTED, POTION OF POWER
~

0 'POTION OF POWER' 'POWER POTION'~
The Potion of Power is bought from the quest master for 450 quest points
(AQUEST BUY POWER, or AQUEST BUY EMPOWER).  It is made at your level, and
holds three ordinary spells cast at level 60: sanctuary, haste and fly,
each lasting as those spells do.  It is not EMPOWER, though it names it:
for the crafted bundle see HELP EMPOWER.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF THE GIANT, SCROLL OF FARSLAY, EMPOWER
~

0 'POTION OF THE GIANT' 'GIANT POTION'~
The Potion of the Giant is bought from the quest master for 450 quest
points (AQUEST BUY GIANT).  It is made at your level, and holds three
ordinary spells cast at level 60: giant strength, stone skin and pass
door, each lasting as those spells do.  Stone skin never takes on a monk or
a necromancer, nor on somebody inside a major globe.  It is not TITANIC:
for the crafted bundle see HELP TITANIC.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF POWER, SCROLL OF FARSLAY, TITANIC
~

0 SKILLS SPELLS~
Syntax: skills
Syntax: spells

The skills and spells commands are used to display your character's list
of available skills (or spells, as the case may be).  They are listed in
order of level, with mana cost (for spells) or percentage (for skills)
listed where applicable.

See also: GAIN, GROUPS, PRACTICE
~

0 SPLIT~
Syntax: split <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Shares coins equally between you and your group members in the room;
gold if you name no kind, and the kind may be shortened.  AUTOSPLIT does
it for coin you loot.

Example: split 30 silver

See also: AUTOSPLIT, GROUP, GIVE
~

0 TIME~
Syntax: time

Displays the current in-game time of day, the date since the mud was last
started, and the real-world local time on the host server.  Game time
progresses faster than real time; day and night cycles affect some spells,
monster behavior, and room lighting.

See also: WEATHER, TICK
~

0 WEATHER~
Syntax: weather

Shows the sky, if you are outdoors: cloudless, cloudy, rainy or lit by
lightning, and whether a warm or a cold wind blows.  Indoors you cannot see
it.  The weather decides some spells -- call
lightning needs bad weather -- and some disasters.

For the weather spell group, HELP GROUP WEATHER or HELP WEATHERSPELLS.

See also: TIME, CONTROL WEATHER, DISASTERS
~

0 TITLE~
Syntax: title <string>
        title default
        title

Sets your title to a given string.  TITLE is available from level 3;
before that the game answers Huh?.  A title is cut at 45 characters.

The game supplies your class's title when your character is created, and
moves it along each time you advance a level.  Once you set a title of
your own it stays: levelling and remorting leave it alone.

TITLE DEFAULT gives you back your class's title for your level, and from
then on it changes as you level again.  TITLE on its own shows your
current title and whether it is yours or your class's.

See also: DESCRIPTION, SCORE, WHO
~

0 TRAIN~
Syntax: train <str|int|wis|dex|con|hp|mana> [count]

At a trainer, spends training sessions: each raises an attribute by one,
or your hit points or mana by 10.  A count trains several at once, up to
100: TRAIN HP 10 adds 100 hit points.  It stops when your sessions run
out or the attribute reaches its maximum, set by race and class.

You gain a session each level.  GAIN spends them on skills, and GAIN
CONVERT turns 10 practices into one.  Trainers stand in Mud School, in
the guilds and about town.  Wisdom and constitution are the usual first
choices: more practices and more hit points each level.

See also: PRACTICE, GAIN, ATTRIBUTE
~

0 TUTORIAL~
Syntax: tutorial            (or TUTORIAL STATUS)
        tutorial on
        tutorial off
        tutorial next

The tutorial walks you through Mud School room by room to its end, where
the educated goblin holds the Mud School diploma: held, it adds 10 to
your intelligence, wisdom and constitution, and every level you gain with
it in hand gives more hit points, mana and practices.  After that it
gives short hints as you play, each tied to something that just happened:
your first steps, picking up and
wearing things, sizing up and fighting monsters, your first kill and
death, a new level, finding your way with WALKTO and RECALL, closed
doors, food and drink, your stash, the questmaster, the Oracle, choosing
a guild at level 3, and the optional player-killer choice at level 15.

  - New characters start with it on; older ones start with it off and
    may turn it on.  It remembers what you have seen, across logins,
    lost links and remorts, and never repeats a hint.
  - One hint at a time, never in the middle of a fight, a WALKTO or a
    page of text.  There is no quest to follow and nothing is required.
  - TUTORIAL OFF stops the hints and keeps your progress; TUTORIAL ON
    picks up where you left off.
  - TUTORIAL NEXT shows the next hint now.  TUTORIAL STATUS lists the
    hints and which you have seen.
  - It will ask you to try the Oracle (PRAY), who answers questions about
    the game; asking her earns Seeker of Answers.  Seeing it through --
    Mud School to the diploma, a first fight, kill and level, your gear,
    WALKTO and the Oracle -- earns Schooled, once.  Dying and PKILL are
    never part of it.
  - TUTORIAL ON looks at where you stand: a guild still to choose, a
    diploma not yet held, a new level, PKILL at 15.  A hint you show you
    know -- a CONSIDER that found someone, a WALKTO that set off, gear
    put on -- is not shown again; a failed try teaches nothing.

See also: BEGINNERS, 'NEWBIE INFO', MUDSCHOOL, GUILDS
~

0 VISIBLE~
Syntax: visible

VISIBLE cancels your hiding and sneaking, as well as any invisibility,
making you visible again.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH
~

0 WHERE~
Syntax: where
        where <character>

WHERE alone lists the players you can see in your area, and where each
is.  WHERE <name> finds one character with that name in your area,
creatures included.

See also: WHO, SCAN, AREAS
~

0 WHO WHOIS~
Syntax: who
        who <level> [level]
        who <class or race> [level] [level]
        who <castle>
        whois <character>

WHO lists the players in the game you can see; some stay unseen.  WHOIS
shows one player.  Classes and races may be shortened.

  who 10            level 10 and up
  who 15 20         levels 15 to 20
  who elf cleric    elven clerics
  who immortal      immortals

See also: WHERE, SCORE, AFK
~

0 WIMPY~
Syntax: wimpy
        wimpy <number>

When a blow leaves you below your wimpy hit points, you try to FLEE by
yourself, unless you are still lagged from a command of your own or from
a bash or trip.  WIMPY alone sets it to a fifth of your maximum hit
points; a number may be anything from 0 to half your maximum.

Some creatures are wimpy too.

See also: FLEE, RECALL, SCORE
~

0 !~
Syntax: !

! repeats the last command you typed.

See also: COMMANDS
~

0 NORTH SOUTH EAST WEST UP DOWN NORTHEAST NORTHWEST SOUTHEAST SOUTHWEST NE NW SE SW DIRECTIONS~
Syntax: north | south | east | west | up | down        (N S E W U D)
        northeast | northwest | southeast | southwest  (NE NW SE SW)

Walks you one room that way.  Any start of a direction's name works too
(NO, WES, SOU); where it fits more than one direction the plain one wins:
NO is north, SO is south, NORTHE is northeast.  OPEN, CLOSE, LOCK,
UNLOCK, PICK, LOOK, RUN, SHOVE and the other commands that take a
direction read it the same way.

See also: EXITS, RUN, SPEEDWALK, WALKTO
~

0 BUG IDEA TYPO~
Syntax: bug <message>
Syntax: idea <message>
Syntax: typo <message>

These record a bug, an idea or a typo for the staff, and they are read.
Type them where the trouble is: the room you are in, your level and your
last few commands are kept with the report, so "bug the fountain will not
fill my waterskin" right after trying is all it takes.  Longer reports can
go on the bug and idea note boards (see NOTE).

What you type and what the game answers is recorded to help find bugs.
The words of tells, replies, group tells and notes are left out, and
passwords always are -- though a character staff flag with LOG has
everything but passwords recorded.

See also: NOTE
~

0 GOSSIP CHAT .~
Syntax: gossip <message>       (or . <message>)
        gossip

Talks to every player with the channel on.  GOSSIP alone turns the channel
off or back on; speaking on it turns it on.

See also: CHANNELS, HISTORY, SHOUT, TELL, QUIET
~

0 SHOUT~
Syntax: shout <message>
        shout

Talks to every player in the world, sleeping or awake, except those with
shouts off or in QUIET mode.  Each shout costs three seconds.  SHOUT alone
turns hearing shouts off or back on; with them off you cannot shout
either.  From level 3.

See also: GOSSIP, YELL, QUIET, CHANNELS
~

0 YELL~
Syntax: yell <message>

Talks to everyone in your area, sleeping or awake.  Narrower than SHOUT,
wider than SAY.

See also: SHOUT, SAY, CHANNELS
~

0 INFO~
Syntax: info

INFO is a broadcast channel that displays automatic notifications when a
player attains a new level.  Typing INFO alone toggles the channel off or
on; you cannot send messages on the info channel directly.

See also: LEVELING, CHANNELS
~

0 LEVELING~
Syntax: leveling <message>
Syntax: leveling

The LEVELING channel is for congratulatory messages to players who just
leveled up.  INFO displays the automatic level notification; LEVELING is
where other players respond with congratulations.  Typing LEVELING alone
toggles the channel.

See also: INFO, GOSSIP, CHANNELS
~

0 MUSIC~
Syntax: music <message>
Syntax: music

The MUSIC channel is used for singing and sharing music-related content
with all players.  Send a message or type MUSIC alone to toggle it off or
on.

See also: GOSSIP, CHANNELS
~

0 DEAF~
Syntax: deaf

Turns tells off or back on.  While deaf you hear no TELL or REPLY, anyone
who tries is told you are not receiving tells, and you cannot send them
yourself.  Staff can still reach you.  Channels are not affected: SHOUT
alone turns shouts off, and QUIET silences everything.

See also: QUIET, SHOUT, TELL, IGNORE
~

0 QUIET~
Syntax: quiet

Turns quiet mode on or off.  While quiet you hear only says and emotes in
your room: no channels, shouts or tells, and you cannot use the channels
or send tells yourself.  Your channel settings are as you left them when
it ends.  QUIE is the shortest you can type it: QUI is kept for QUIT's
reminder.

See also: DEAF, CHANNELS, IGNORE
~

0 TELL~
Syntax: tell <character> <message>

TELL sends a private message to one awake player anywhere in the world.
Only the target sees your message.  Use REPLY to respond to the last tell
you received.

See also: REPLY, SAY, GTELL, CHANNELS
~

0 REPLY~
Syntax: reply <message>

REPLY sends a message to the last player who sent you a TELL.  REPLY
works even if you cannot see the player and without revealing their
identity.  This is handy for talking to invisible or switched immortal
players.

See also: TELL, SAY, GTELL
~

10 CGOS CASTLECHAT CC -~
Syntax: cgos <message>
Syntax: cc <message>

CGOS allows you to send a message to members of all castles.

CC is used to send  message only to members of your castle.  The dash, '-'
is a synonym for CC.

Type a channel name without a message to turn that channel off.

See also: CHANNELS, GOSSIP, TELL
~

51 HERO =~
Syntax: hero <message>

A channel is provided for heroes to communicate with one another.  This is
similar to the other public channels, like gossip, etc. The equals sign,
'=' is a synonym for hero.

Immortals may also use the hero channel.

See also: GOSSIP, CHANNELS, HEROQUEST, HEROLEVELS
~

0 CHANNELS~
Syntax: channels

Shows which channels you hear.  Type a channel's name alone (GOSSIP,
MUSIC ...) to turn it off or on; QUIET turns them all off, tells too.

What is said on a channel is kept for HISTORY and written to the
channel log, which is published with the game's logs; you only ever see
the channels you may hear.  SAY is logged but not kept for HISTORY.  Tells
are private: never in the shared history or the channel log, and the
command log leaves out their words -- unless staff have flagged that
character with LOG <name>, which records everything but passwords.

See also: GOSSIP, SHOUT, TELL, SAY, HISTORY, DEAF, QUIET
~

63 AREAVNUMS~
Revised 7/19/96 - Soul.

00  limbo     20  catacomb     40  moria        60  haon       80  mega1
01  smurf     21  hood         41  moria        61  haon       81  -
