See also: VNUM, MEMORY
~


63 CLONE~
Syntax:
  clone obj <object>
  clone mob <mobile>
  clone <name>

The clone command duplicates an object or mobile.  The level of item or mobile
that may be cloned depends on your level.  The target object or mob must be in
the same room as you, or in your possession.  Strung and set information is
preserved by clone, as well as any spell effects and container contents or
mobile inventory, except for items which are too high for you to clone.
Strung extended descriptions on objects are not kept, however.

See also: LOAD, VNUM, STRING
~

66 ADVANCE~
Syntax: advance <character> <level>

ADVANCE sets the level of a player standing in the room with you, from 1 to
70 and never above your own trust.  Below trust 69 it works only on
yourself ("You can only advance yourself at your current level.").  It is
refused on a mobile and on anyone who outranks you.

Raising a level runs every level gained in between, with its hit points,
mana and practices, exactly as earning them would.  Lowering it -- or
setting the level a character already has -- first resets them to level 1
with 20 hit points, 100 mana and moves and no practices or trains, then
raises them again to the level asked for: a demotion is not undone by
advancing them back.  A snapshot is taken before the level changes
(PRESTORE <player> ... lists it as an advance), which is the way back from
a mistake.

ADVANCE is how a player is made an immortal in game.

See also: TRUST, PRESTORE, WIZLIST
~

72 CREATOR~
Syntax: creator
        creator <player> on|off

The creator's rank stands above every level: trust 72, whatever the
character's own level.  A creator outranks every implementor and anyone
trusted to 71, holds the commands nobody else does (SHUTDOWN, REBOOT,
LOG, SNOOP, BAN, ALLOW, WIZLOCK, NEWLOCK, DENY, UNDENY), always logs in
wizinvis at 72 unless they left at a level of their choosing, and cannot
die: blows stop at one hit point, and SLAY and FARSLAY strike down
whoever throws them.

CREATOR alone lists the creators online.  With a name, the founding
creator grants or takes the rank -- online, or in the save file of a
player who is offline.  Nobody else may, and nobody may take it from the
founder, who outranks the creators he makes.  Taking it away brings the
player's WIZINVIS level down to the trust they keep.

See also: TRUST, WIZINVIS
~

70 TRUST~
Syntax: trust <character> <level>

TRUST sets a character's trust to the specified level.  This is similar to
advancing the character for accessing immortal commands, but has no effect
on combat strength, et cetera.  You can give more trust to mortal (or
immortal) characters to give them access to more power without messing up
the character.  You can also restrict a player's trust to less than their
natural level.

Trust may be set from 1 to 71, never above your own, and never on
someone who outranks you; 0 resets it.  Trust 71 outranks every
implementor.

A trust of 0 means to use the character's natural level again.

See also: ADVANCE, WIZLIST
~

67 ALLOW BAN~
Syntax: allow   <site>
        ban     <site>
        ban

BAN <site> prohibits players from that site from logging in.  BAN with
no arguments lists the banned sites.  ALLOW <site> removes a site from
the ban list.  The list is saved and kept across reboots.  Creator only
(trust 72).

A site is an address or a host name, and the two are matched differently:

  An address (IPv4 such as 203.0.113.9, or IPv6 in any spelling, such as
  2001:db8::42 or 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0042) is kept in one
  canonical form and matched against the connection's own address, exactly
  -- whether or not its host name has been looked up, whether the lookup
  failed, and whether DNS is on.  A web client player is matched by the
  address the web bridge names.

  Anything else is a host name, matched whole (not by suffix) against the
  name the resolver found.  It cannot match until a name comes back, so a
  host-name ban closes a connection a moment after it arrives; with DNS
  OFF, or for an address with no name, it never matches.  Ban the address
  when it must hold.

A ban is checked when a name is typed, again when the password is
accepted, and again when a host name arrives.

See also: BAN, DNS
~

65 GATHER~
Syntax: GATHER <object name>
        gather <object name> <character>
        gather <object vnum>

***WARNING***
This command is very powerful.  Upmost discretion must be taken
in its use.

The command will gather all items in the game that match the object
fields name. Example:

gather sword

This will gather all items that have the word 'sword' in the
'name' field of the object.

This command should only be used to gather quest items that are
no longer in use, or objects that are suspected of being a bug
in the game.

The second form of the gather command, with a character's name as the
argument, will gather all of the objects that match the name from that
specific character.

The third version will gather all objects that have a corresponding
vnum.

See also: SPLIT, GIVE, GET
~

69 REBOOT SHUTDOWN~
Syntax: reboot
        shutdown

Both save every player (as FSAVE does, then you), close every connection
and stop the game.  Creator only: trust 72, the creator's rank, which no
level or ordinary TRUST (at most 71) reaches.

  reboot     the game exits and comes back by itself: startup.sh and
             the host's service start it again after five seconds.
  shutdown   writes area/shutdown.txt, which tells startup.sh and the
             host's service not to start it again.  If the file cannot
             be written the game stays up and says so, since it would
             only come back as after a REBOOT.

Unless you are wizinvis, everyone sees "Reboot by <name>." or "Shutdown
by <name>.".  Wizinvis, a REBOOT says nothing and a SHUTDOWN says "The
game is shutting down."; either does just the same.  Warn players with
ANNOUNCE first.

See also: ANNOUNCE, WIZLOCK, CREATOR, TRUST
~

66 WIZLOCK~
Syntax: wizlock

WIZLOCK toggles a lock on logins: a character below level 51 (hero) is
refused with "The game is wizlocked."  A link-dead character may still
reconnect, and nobody already playing is put out.  The lock is not saved:
a reboot lifts it.  Creator only (trust 72).

Use it sparingly -- critical maintenance or an exploit emergency -- and
ANNOUNCE it first.  Type WIZLOCK again to unlock.

See also: NEWLOCK, SHUTDOWN, REBOOT, DENY
~

62 NEWLOCK~
Syntax: newlock

NEWLOCK toggles a lock on character creation: existing characters log in
as normal, but a new name is refused with "The game is newlocked."  Use
it against somebody making alternate characters to evade a ban.  The lock
is not saved: a reboot lifts it.  Creator only (trust 72).

Type NEWLOCK again to allow new characters.

See also: WIZLOCK, DENY
~

67 DENY~
Syntax: deny <name>

DENY permanently locks a named character out of the game.  The player will
be unable to log in with that character; the game simply disconnects them
during the login sequence.  This is a hard ban on a character file.

DENY should be reserved for serious, repeated violations where a FREEZE or
timeout is insufficient.  A denied character stays locked out until the
creator uses UNDENY.  Creator only (trust 72), as is SET CHAR ... DENY.

See also: UNDENY, FREEZE, BAN, WIZLOCK
~

67 UNDENY~
Syntax: undeny <name>

UNDENY removes a previously applied DENY flag from a character, restoring
their ability to log in.  Only supply the character name - they do not need
to be online for UNDENY to take effect.  Creator only (trust 72).

See also: DENY
~

67 DUMP~
Syntax: dump <name>

DUMP (formerly DISCONNECT) breaks a player's network link, returning them to
their telnet prompt or their client's connection screen.  The character
remains in the game briefly in a link-dead state and saves normally.

Use DUMP as a non-lethal first response to spammers or players who need to
be cleanly logged off.  If the player reconnects and continues misbehaving,
consider FREEZE, DENY, or escalate to BAN.

See also: FREEZE, DENY, BAN, SNOOP
~

64 FREEZE~
Syntax: freeze <name>

FREEZE <character> is a toggle which prevents a character from issuing any
commands at all. It can be removed from within the game, so freezing a
troublemaker is usually a better option than the deny command.

See also: DENY, PETRIFY, STASIS, MUTE
~

63 FORCE~
Syntax: force <character> <command>
Syntax: force all <command>

FORCE forces one character to execute a command, except of course delete.

Below level 67 force can only be used on mobiles, and force all is
unavailable.  You also cannot 'force orc murder hugo', for example.

FORCE 'all' forces all player characters to execute a command.
This is typically used for 'force all save'.

See also: TRANSFER, AT, SOCKETS
~

63 LASTLOG~
Syntax: lastlog
        lastlog <count>
        lastlog <count> <name>
        lastlog <name>

LASTLOG lists the most recent logins, newest first, with the date and time,
the character, whether the session was a new character, an ordinary connect,
or a reconnect, how long the session lasted, and the host the player came
from.

A session has no length until it ends, so the Played column shows a dash for
a player who is still connected, and for the login half of a finished
session.  The closing row carries the time.  Sessions that ended in a hard
crash were never closed and keep the dash.

The Event column says how the session ended as well as how it began:
quit is a deliberate exit, linkdead a dropped connection, and shutdown a
server restart.

SOCKETS only sees a descriptor while it is still attached, so it cannot tell
you who was here an hour ago.  LASTLOG reads a journal the server appends at
every login, so the history survives both disconnects and server restarts.

Without arguments the last 20 logins are shown.  Supply a count to widen or
narrow that, and a name to restrict the list to one character.  The server
keeps the most recent 200 entries.

Records contain player IP addresses.  Treat the output as private operational
data and do not paste it into public channels.

See also: SOCKETS, FINGER, WHOIS
~

63 SOCKETS~
Syntax: sockets
        sockets <name>
        sockets ip <ip address>

SOCKETS reports all of the visible users connected to the server, including
users in the process of logging in.  The two numbers inside brackets are the
channel number and the 'connected' state.  The 'connected' states are listed in
merc.h:

PLAYING                      0
GET_NAME                     1
GET_OLD_PASSWORD             2
CONFIRM_NEW_NAME             3
GET_NEW_PASSWORD             4
CONFIRM_NEW_PASSWORD         5
GET_NEW_RACE                 6
GET_NEW_SEX                  7
GET_NEW_CLASS                8
GET_ALIGNMENT                9
DEFAULT_CHOICE              10
GEN_GROUPS                  11
PICK_WEAPON                 12
READ_IMOTD                  13
READ_MOTD                   14
BREAK_CONNECT               15
RETRY_PASSWORD              16
COPYOVER_RECOVER            17

The optional name argument shows the site of a player, or optional can be used
to identify switched gods (i.e. socket orc shows the name of anyone switched
into an orc).

You can also use the IP argument to list only the players from a certain site.
Example:  socket ip nowhere.com

See also: TRANSFER, STAT, MEMORY

~

65 LOAD~
Syntax: load mob <vnum>
        load obj <vnum> <level>

The load command is used to load new objects or mobiles (use clone to
duplicate strung items and mobs).  The vnums can be found with the vnum
command, or by stat'ing an existing mob or object.

Load puts objects in inventory if they can be carried.  Otherwise they are
put in the room.  Mobiles are always put into the same room as the god. Old
format objects must be given a level argument to determine their power, new
format objects have a preset level that cannot be changed without set.

See also: CLONE, VNUM, STAT
~

67 SLOOKUP~
This command will show the slot number, and skill number used by the
system. It is useful for finding out numbers to use when 'setting'
a potion or scroll, and various other items.

See also: LOAD, BREW, SCRIBE
~

66 SET~
Syntax: set char  <player or mobile> <field> <value>
        set obj   <object> <field> <value>
        set room  <vnum> <field> <value>
        set skill <character> <skill or spell | all> <percent>

Changes a live value on a character, an object or a room; it takes effect
at once and a player is saved with it.  Trust 66.  Any form without a
field lists what it accepts.  PLAYER and MOB are other names for CHAR.

  - Never on anyone who outranks you.  A player gets a snapshot first
    (PRESTORE lists it as a staff change).
  - SET MOB or SET OBJ with a vnum edits a prototype you are building:
    HELP MSHOW, HELP OSHOW.
  - A changed mobile or object lasts until it resets or the game reboots;
    a room until you RSAVE its area.

Details: HELP 'SET CHAR', HELP 'SET OBJECT', HELP 'SET ROOM'.

See also: STRING, STAT, RSAVE, BUILDING
~

66 'SET CHAR' 'SET SKILL'~
Syntax: set char <player or mobile> <field> <value>
        set skill <character> <skill or spell> <percent>
        set skill <character> all <percent>      (players only)

The fields of SET CHAR (also SET PLAYER, SET MOB with a name):

  Stats       str int wis dex con
  Identity    sex class race level align guild
  Resources   hp mana end move maxmove practice train exp stash
  Combat      hitroll damroll armor hunt annoy
  Currency    platinum gold silver copper bank qp
  Conditions  thirst hunger drunk timer
  Estates     castle castlehead mountable were
  Flags       pkiller excon wanted freeze deny   (each on | off)
  Records     dcount pkills

  - ARMOR sets all four armour classes at once.
  - QP adds to the points; below trust 70 at most 100 at a time.
  - DENY is the creator's alone.  STASH sets a player's stash size and
    never below what it already holds.
  - Carry capacity has no field: raise strength, dexterity or level.

SET SKILL ALL skips abilities no class can learn.

See also: SET, PARDON, ADVANCE, PRESTORE
~

66 'SET OBJECT'~
Syntax: set obj <object> <field> <value>

  Numbers     level weight cost timer type condition material
  Values      value0 value1 value2 value3 value4  (v0 to v4)
  Flags       extra wear
  Text        name short long   (the rest of the line)

COST is in copper: a silver is 100, a gold 10000 (set obj sword cost
50000 is five gold).  value0-value4 mean different things for each type;
STAT OBJ shows them.  A portal or manipulation item needs trust 70.

Flags take the letters STAT and area files use.  Lead with + to add or -
to remove; without either the whole field is replaced.

    set obj sword extra +H        add nodrop, keep the rest

  EXTRA flags:
    A glow            B hum             C dark            D lock
    E evil            F invis           G magic           H nodrop
    I bless           J anti-good       K anti-evil       L anti-neutral
    M noremove        N inventory       O nopurge         P rot on death
    Q vanish on death R metal           S bounce          T teleport
    U no identify     V no locate       W race restricted X adds affect
    Y embalmed

  WEAR flags (A is needed for anything a player may pick up):
    A take            B finger          C neck            D body
    E head            F legs            G feet            H hands
    I arms            J shield          K about body      L waist
    M wrist           N wield           O hold            P two handed

  TYPE numbers:
     1 light      2 scroll     3 wand       4 staff      5 weapon
     8 treasure   9 armor     10 potion    11 clothing  12 furniture
    13 trash     15 container 17 drink can 18 key       19 food
    20 money     22 boat      23 npc corpse 24 pc corpse 25 fountain
    26 pill      27 protect   28 map       29 scuba gear 30 portal
    31 manipulation           32 castle relic           33 saddle
    34 herb      35 spell component        36 soul container
    37 action

See also: SET, STRING, STAT
~

66 'SET ROOM'~
Syntax: set room <vnum> <field> <value>

  Words       flags sector
  Text        name desc detail

    set room 21001 flags +indoors -dark     +adds, -takes away
    set room 21001 flags none               clears them
    set room 21001 sector forest
    set room 21001 desc A long hall.        replaces, wrapped for you
    set room 21001 desc + More of it.       adds a line
    set room 21001 detail altar A stone altar, cracked.

Sectors: inside city field forest hills mountain water_swim water_noswim
underwater air desert underground.  Flag names: dark jail no_mob indoors
cult_entrance death_trap private safe solitary pet_shop no_recall
imp_only gods_only heroes_only newbies_only law hp_regen mana_regen arena
castle_join silent no_teleport always_lit bank.  River, teleport and
room-affect flags cannot be set by hand.  The old flag letters and sector
numbers still work.  Write \; for a semicolon in a text.

A room you made with GOTO is yours to change at any staff rank; one from
an area file needs an implementor.  RSAVE makes a change permanent.

See also: SET, RSHOW, RSAVE, BUILDING
~

63 STRING~
Syntax: string obj <field> <string>
        string mob <field> <string>

The string command changes the various strings associated with an object or
mobile.  A full list of fields can be viewed by typing string obj or string
mob.  Sorry, multi-line strings are not yet supported.

For the action_to_room and action_to_char strings.  Use $p for the object
name and $n for the player name if you want those in the message.

DO NOT USE ANY OTHER VARIABLES, DOING SO WILL CAUSE THE MUD TO CRASH.

See also: STAT, LOAD, CLONE
~

66 PARDON~
Syntax: pardon <character> <flag>
  Flags: wanted excon pkiller warned freeze deny all

PARDON removes a punishment or status flag from a player.  The flag names
are:

  wanted   -- clears the WANTED flag
  excon    -- clears the EXCON (ex-convict / con-death) flag
  pkiller  -- clears active PKiller status
  warned   -- clears the WARNED flag (and the LOG the warning turned on)
  freeze   -- removes the FREEZE (total lockout) flag
  deny     -- restores login access denied by the DENY flag
  all      -- clears WANTED, EXCON, PKILLER, and WARNED in one step

The old keywords "killer" and "thief" are accepted as aliases for "wanted"
and "excon" respectively.  Don't pardon a player unless you are sure they
deserve it.  Characters are saved immediately after any flag change.

See also: SET, FREEZE, DENY
~

66 PURGE~
Syntax: purge
        purge <name>

Purge is used to clean up the world.  Purge with no arguments removes all the
NPC's and objects in the current room, except those with a NOPURGE flag (the
pit, the fountain, shopkeepers).

Purge with a name looks nearest first: somebody in this room, then an object
here (in the room or carried), and only then somebody elsewhere in the world.
It says what it removed, its vnum and, for a mobile elsewhere, its room.  A
thing named directly is removed even if it is flagged NOPURGE.  A player is
never destroyed: purging one severs their link and leaves them link-dead.

See also: SLAY, RESTORE, LOAD
~


66 RESTORE~
Syntax: restore <character>
        restore room
        restore all

Restore restores full hit points, mana points, and movement points to the
target character.  It also heals poison, plague, and blindness.

Restore room (or restore with no argument) performs a restore on every player
in the room, restore all does the same for all connected players. Restore all
is only usable by creators and implementors.  Restore should be used sparingly
or not at all.

See also: PURGE, SLAY
~

61 HERBIE~
Syntax: herbie <player>

Summons Herbie the Angel directly to an online player's location.  Herbie
arrives in person, kneels beside the player, channels a golden healing
light, then departs.  The player and everyone in the room sees the full
sequence of acts.

Herbie cures: poison, plague, blindness, sleep, and curse.
Herbie restores: hit points, mana, movement, hunger, and thirst.

Cannot be used on players of equal or higher level.  Cannot target mobs.

See also: EMPOWER, SPELLUP
~

66 HERMIE SPELLUP SPELLPURGE~
Syntax: hermie [minutes]
Syntax: spellpurge

HERMIE places Hermie the Angel -- Herbie's girlfriend -- in your current
room (SPELLUP is the old name and still works).  With no number she stays
there, taking requests, until somebody removes her.  With a number of
minutes (1 to 1440) she leaves on her own when they run out; HERMIE
<minutes> where she already stands sets how much longer she stays.

After a restart that put mortals off -- a deploy, a reboot, a crash with
players on -- she comes by herself, as an apology: for four hours from the
boot she stands in the Oak Tree Square whenever a mortal is playing, and
steps away while none is.  A restart with only staff, or nobody, on brings
no Hermie.  SPELLPURGE ends the four hours early.  Every arrival and
departure is in the log and on WIZINFO.

Players talk to her with SAY.  Saying her name, or 'menu', 'list', 'hello'
or 'help', makes her read out her list; after that they say either the
number or the name of what they want and she casts it on them.  Saying
'all' gets them every entry in one go.

She ignores anything else said in the room, so she can be parked somewhere
busy without burying every conversation in her menu.

She offers armor, bless, shield, stone skin, sanctuary, haste, fly, pass
door, protection evil, fire shield, frost shield, divine protection, giant
strength, frenzy, major globe, infravision, detect invis, detect hidden,
detect magic, detect good, detect evil, refresh, heal and restore mana
individually, plus empower as a bundle.

Every spell she hands out lasts 30 ticks, whoever asks for it, and every
one is written to the game log.

She is a permanent fixture, not a visitor.  Remove the one in front of you
with PURGE, or remove every copy of her in the world with SPELLPURGE.

See also: HERBIE, EMPOWER, TITANIC, PURGE
~

66 SLAY~
Syntax: slay <character>

Slay kills a character in cold blood, no saving throw.  Best not to use this
command on players if you enjoy being a god.

See also: PURGE, RESTORE, KILL
~

63 TRANSFER TELEPORT~
Syntax: transfer <character>
        transfer all
        transfer <character> <location>
        transfer all         <location>

Transfer transfers the target character, or ALL player characters, to your
current location (default) or to a specified location.  Trans all should
almost never be used, as 100 players popping into one room can have horrible
effects on slow links.  Teleport is a synonym for transfer.  For the
TELEPORT spell, HELP TELEPORTSPELL.

See also: GOTO, AT, FORCE
~

63 AT~
Syntax: at <location> <command>

At executes the given command (which may have arguments) at the given
location.  The location may be specified as a vnum, as the name of
a mobile, or as the name of an object.

At works by temporarily moving you to that location, executing the
command, and then moving you back (if the command didn't change your
location).

See also: GOTO, TRANSFER, FORCE
~

64 ECHO~
Syntax: echo <message>

ECHO sends a raw message to all characters in your current room.  No name
or attribution is shown - the text appears verbatim on every nearby screen.
Useful for producing custom room flavor text or staging events.  Immortals
of higher trust will see a faint staff tag prepended to the message.

See also: GECHO, PECHO
~

64 GECHO~
Syntax: gecho <message>

GECHO broadcasts a message to every connected player on the entire MUD,
regardless of location.  No attribution is appended.  Use sparingly -
unnecessary global echoes are disruptive and are logged.

See also: ECHO, PECHO, ANNOUNCE
~

64 PECHO~
Syntax: pecho <player> <message>

PECHO sends a raw private message directly to a single player's screen.
No name or source is shown.  The player sees only the text you type.
Useful for delivering event narration or hints without breaking immersion.

See also: ECHO, GECHO, TELL
~

60 GOTO~
Syntax: goto <room vnum>
        goto <character or object>

GOTO takes you to a room: by its vnum, or to wherever the named character or
object is.  Use the room's Vnum (STAT ROOM prints both Number and Vnum; only
Vnum is a room you can go to).  A fight you are in ends when you leave.

Staff walk into private and solitary rooms however full they are, except a
room flagged IMP_ONLY, which needs trust 69.  The room sees your POOFOUT
leave and POOFIN arrive, or "raises into the sky" if you set none -- unless
your WIZINVIS level hides you from them.

Careful with a number: GOTO to a vnum where no room exists creates an empty
room there ("You form order from nothingness.").

See also: WIZINVIS, POOFIN, POOFOUT, TRANSFER, RWHERE
~

63 EXPLODE~
Syntax: explode container <container name>
        explode room

This command will scatter mobs in rooms, or items in containers in your
inventory all over the MUD. Its main use is to simplify placing quest
oriented stuff into the game. Item 26 is a generic item with a no_locate
flag on it that can be used as a quest item.

See also: PURGE, LOAD, VNUM
~

58 HOLYLIGHT~
Syntax: holylight   (staff)

Holy sight sees virtually everything regardless of visibility: invisible
characters, mobiles and objects; dark rooms; hidden, stealthed and
shadowmelded players.  It also sees through wizinvis and cloak at or
below its level.

Staff toggle it with HOLYLIGHT; when it is off, they see what mortal
players see.  Wearing the Triforce in the light slot gives the same sight
at level 59 for as long as it is worn -- no command needed.

AFFECT lists holy sight while you have it, with the level it sees at.
It does not change whether anybody else can see you.

See also: AFFECT, TRIFORCE, WIZINVIS, 'DETECT INVIS', 'DETECT HIDDEN'
~

60 WIZINVIS~
Syntax: wizinvis
        wizinvis <level>
        wizinvis 0

WIZINVIS hides you from everybody whose trust is below your wizinvis level.
They do not see you in the room or on WHO, and what you do in front of them
produces no message at all (see WIZINVIS LEAVES NO TRACE in the staff
documentation).  Those at or above your level see you marked (WIZI).

  wizinvis           toggles: on at your own trust, or off if already on
  wizinvis <level>   on at that level, from 2 up to your own trust
  wizinvis 0         off ("You are not wizinvis." if you were not)

The level is compared with trust, not with the level in WHO: a builder
trusted to 63 sees a wizinvis 62.  Trust 71 sees anyone at 71 or below; the
creator's 72 hides them from everyone, and WIZINVIS 71 lets trust 71 see
them.  Staff notices that name you (logins, deaths, restores) go out only to
staff who could see you.

See also: HOLYLIGHT, CLOAK, POOFIN, POOFOUT, TRUST
~

65 MEMORY~
Syntax: memory

Memory reports the size of the mud.

The 'Mobs' and 'Objs' lines report the number of types of mobiles and objects,
not the number of instances currently in the game.

The 'Strings' line shows memory used by area-file strings.  These strings have
a maximum limit, which is also shown.

The 'Perms' line shows permanently allocated memory blocks.  There is no limit
on the number and size of these blocks.

See also: SOCKETS, STAT, MEMCHECK
~

60 MEMCHECK~
Syntax: memcheck
        memcheck selftest [reuse]

Memcheck reports the game's memory safeguards.  A character, object or
connection that leaves the game is kept, whole and marked dead, for a
minute before its memory is given back (the quarantine).  Once a second the
game checks every stored pointer -- who is fighting, following, hunting or
replying to whom, what carries or holds what, which connection plays which
character -- and any that still points at something dead is written to the
log as a MEMORY line, announced on WIZINFO, and cleared so the game goes
on.

Whether something is gone is looked up by its address, never read from it.
A pointer to something still in quarantine is "dead", and the line names
what it was.  One whose memory has already been given back is "freed": it
is caught all the same, but nothing is known of what it was.  Everything
made gets a serial of its own, and the long-lived pointers (fighting,
following, leading, pets, hunting, reply, quest giver, a character's
connection, a bottle and its captive, which connection plays or snoops
whom) keep the serial of what they named: one whose memory was given to
something new of the same kind is "reused", and caught too.  Lists of what
is carried or in a room are checked by address alone.

  Quarantine   blocks held, the oldest one's age, the window and the cap
  Live         characters, players, objects and connections seen last check
  Tracked      how many live and quarantined things are known by address
  Stand-ins    temporary copies a command registers while it runs; any
               still registered when the check runs is a bug
  Sweep        how many checks, and what the last, slowest and average cost
  Stale        pointers caught since boot, by field; how many were freed
               or reused
  Last events  the newest MEMORY lines

Any MEMORY line is a bug to report: it names the field, who held it, and
what it pointed at.  MEMCHECK SELFTEST (and SELFTEST REUSE) exists only in
a test world.

See also: MEMORY, WIZINFO
~

65 VNUM~
Syntax: vnum <name>
        vnum <obj or mob> <name>
        vnum skill <name>

Vnum finds all objects and mobiles with a particular name, and prints out
the results.  Use vnum obj or vnum mob to limit the search.  This vnum is
very useful for loading. Vnum skill returns the skill number (for set) and
the slot number (for making new zones) of a skill name.

See also: LOAD, STAT, AREAVNUMS
~

63 STAT MSTAT OSTAT RSTAT~
Syntax: stat <name>
        stat mob  <name>
        stat obj  <name>
        stat room <number>
        mstat <name>       (the same as stat mob)
        ostat <name>       (the same as stat obj)
        rstat [number]     (the same as stat room)

The stat command gives you detailed information on an object, mobile, or room
within the game.  Without a class argument, the command will return an object
(if found), a mobile (if no object exists, but a mobile does), or a room
(if a number is  given).  Stat room with no argument stats the room you are
standing in.  Stat can be used to find room vnums for goto.

See also: GOTO, TRANSFER, VNUM
~

62 CLOAK~
Syntax: cloak <level>

Cloak will make you invisible to anyone not in the same room with you.
Anyone is higher level than the level you set your cloak at can see you
anywhere though.

See also: WIZHELP, IMMORTAL
~

69 BACKUP~
Syntax: backup
        backup now

This command will perform a backup on all of the pfiles.  Typing backup by
itself will show when the next scheduled backup will take place.

See also: MEMORY, SOCKETS

~

69 PSTAT~
Syntax: pstat

Pstat shows you various statistics of all the players connected to the mud.

See also: SOCKETS, MEMORY
~

62 MWHERE~
Syntax: mwhere <keyword>

MWHERE searches for all loaded instances of any mobile whose name field
matches the given keyword and displays their room name and vnum.
The search is case-insensitive and matches any part of the mob name.

Example:
  mwhere guard
  City Guard          [  2345]
  North Gate Guard    [ 12001]

See also: OWHERE, RWHERE, GWHERE, MSTAT
~

62 OWHERE~
Syntax: owhere <keyword>

OWHERE locates all instances of an object whose name field matches the
given keyword anywhere in the game world - carried, worn, in containers,
or on mob corpses.  Displays object name, holder/location, and room vnum.

See also: MWHERE, RWHERE, GWHERE, VNUM
~

62 RWHERE~
Syntax: rwhere <string>

RWHERE searches the room name of every loaded room for a partial match
and returns the room name and vnum.  Case-insensitive.  Useful for
locating a room when you only know part of its name.

Example:
  rwhere hermit
  Hermit's Hut          [  331]
  Hermit's Lair         [ 1589]
  The Hermit's Corner   [ 6373]

See also: MWHERE, OWHERE, GWHERE, GOTO
~

62 GWHERE~
Syntax: gwhere [keyword]

GWHERE with no argument lists every player currently connected, their
room name, and their room vnum.  With a keyword argument it filters to
mobiles matching that name, similar to MWHERE.

See also: MWHERE, OWHERE, RWHERE, WIZCHECK, PWHERE
~

62 PWHERE~
Syntax: pwhere

PWHERE lists every player in the game you can see: their level, room
vnum, room name and area, and whether they are link-dead, fighting or
idle.  Unlike GWHERE it counts link-dead characters, who are still in the
world without a connection.

See also: GWHERE, MWHERE, WHERE, PSTAT
~

62 QFLAG~
Syntax: qflag <player>

This command sets a (Quest) flag on players that will keep them from losing
experience or equipment during quests.  Remember to remove the quest flag
from the player after the quest is over.  The flag will automatically be
removed when the player quits otherwise.

See also: WIZHELP, IMMORTAL

~

64 PEACE~
Syntax: peace

Peace causes all characters in a room to stop fighting. It also strips the
AGGRESSIVE bit from mobiles.

See also: SLAY, FORCE
~

62 POOFIN~
Syntax: poofin
        poofin <message>

Sets what the room sees when you arrive by GOTO.  POOFIN alone shows the
current one.  Staff only (trust 62).

  - The message must contain your name exactly as it is spelled,
    capitals included ("You must include your name.").  It is shown as
    you typed it: $n and the like are not replaced.
  - Without one the room sees "<name> appears before you."  Once set it
    cannot be cleared, only replaced.
  - Only those whose trust reaches your WIZINVIS level see it.

Example: poofin Galdor rides in on a bolt of lightning.

See also: POOFOUT, GOTO, WIZINVIS
~

62 POOFOUT~
Syntax: poofout
        poofout <message>

Sets what the room sees when you leave by GOTO; POOFOUT alone shows the
current one.  The rules are POOFIN's: your name exactly as spelled, shown
as typed, only to those whose trust reaches your WIZINVIS level.  Without
one the room sees "<name> raises into the sky, and is gone."

Example: poofout Galdor vanishes with a thunderclap.

See also: POOFIN, GOTO, WIZINVIS
~

62 LOG~
Syntax: log <character>
        log all

LOG <name> toggles full recording of one player.  Creator only (trust
72).  The flag is saved, so it lasts until LOG <name> again; WARN sets
it too, and lifting a warning clears it.

  - Every player is already watched by default (LOG ALL starts on): each
    command is written to log/toc.log with its room, but the words of
    tells, replies, group tells, notes and anything that reads them back
    are withheld.
  - A player flagged with LOG <name> is recorded in full, private words
    included.
  - Passwords never are: PASSWORD, RESETPWD, DELETE, REMORT and ALIAS are
    written by name only, for everyone.

LOG ALL switches the default watch off or on until the next boot.  The
game's logs are copied to the public repository, so use LOG <name> only
with a reason.

See also: SNOOP, WARN, PSTAT
~

67 SNOOP~
Syntax: snoop <character>
        snoop <your own name>

Shows you, live, the commands a player types (after "% ") and what their
screen shows.  Creator only (trust 72).  Snooping yourself ends all your
snoops.

  - One snooper per player ("Busy already"); never anyone who outranks
    you.  A snoop ends by itself once you lose SNOOP's rank.
  - It is not every keystroke: a password, RESETPWD, DELETE or REMORT
    line, and every movement command, shows as an empty "% " line; a
    social or a word that is no command is not echoed; and a password
    prompt puts nothing on the screen.  Tells and alias bodies are shown.
  - The player is not told.  Use it only with a legitimate reason, and
    ask first when the purpose is debugging.

See also: LOG, SWITCH, PSTAT
~

64 SWITCH~
Syntax: switch <mobile>

SWITCH transfers your consciousness into the target mobile, giving you
direct control of it.  You type commands as if you were that mob - its
stats, skills, and position all apply.  Your immortal body is left standing
in place while you are switched.

Restrictions:
  - You can only switch into mobs, not player characters.
  - You cannot switch into a mob that already has a descriptor (i.e. one
    another immortal is already controlling).
  - You cannot switch into link-dead PCs.

Use RETURN to exit the mob and return to your immortal body; QUIT
also brings you back first.  SAVE saves your own character.  If the mob
is killed, or your connection drops, you are put back in your body.
Nobody can CHARM or ORDER a mob while you are in it.

See also: RETURN, FORCE, AT
~

64 RETURN~
Syntax: return

RETURN exits a mobile body and returns your immortal character to normal
control.  Use this after SWITCH to come back to yourself.  If you are
not currently switched into a mobile, RETURN has no effect.  QUIT, typed
while switched, returns you too.

See also: SWITCH
~

60 IMMTALK :~
Syntax: immtalk <message>
        immtalk
        : <message>

Sends a message to every immortal (trust 60 and up) with the channel on,
marked with your trust: "Zog [60] immtalks 'hello'".  IMMTALK alone
turns the channel off or on for you.  The colon is a shortcut for it.

Lines are kept for HISTORY at the rank that may hear them, and in the
channel log.

See also: GODTALK, HERO, WIZINFO, BEEP, CHANNELS
~

60 WIZHELP~
Syntax: wizhelp

Wizhelp provides a list of all the immortal commands available to you.

See also: COMMANDS, SUMMARY
~

69 COMPONENT~
Syntax: component

Component command forces the scattering of random components throughout
the world, rather than waiting for the MUD to do it randomly over time.
This was added to restore components after reboots or crashes that wipe
the current components from the world.

(do 'owhere component' to see the current components in the world.)

See also: LOAD, VNUM
~

62 SHOWHUNT~
Syntax: showhunt
        showhunt all

SHOWHUNT counts the mobiles on the hunter list against its maximum.
SHOWHUNT ALL also lists each one: its room, whether it is hunting or
annoying, its quarry and the quarry's room, and its wait.  Staff only
(trust 62).  A hunter whose quarry is gone is counted but not listed.

See also: HPARDON, MWHERE
~

62 HPARDON~
Syntax: hpardon <character>
        hpardon all

HPARDON <character> makes every mobile hunting that character give up.
Staff only (trust 62).  HPARDON ALL ends every hunt in the game and needs
trust 70; below that, ALL is read as a name.

Use it for a hunt stuck after a crash or a bug, or to clean up a test.

See also: SHOWHUNT
~

67 SWEDISH~
Syntax: swedish <character>

Makes the victim talk like a swede.  Remember to remove it from the player
before you or they leave.

See also: WIZHELP
~

60 WIZINFO~
Syntax: wizinfo

WIZINFO turns on or off whether you receive the game's automatic staff
notices: logins and logouts, lost links, deaths, levels, restores and the
other lines staff are told about.  It only toggles what you receive; it
does not send anything.  Typing a message after it says "Just type
'wizinfo' to toggle the channel on or off."

A notice naming a wizinvis character goes only to staff whose trust lets
them see that character.  To talk to staff use IMMTALK; to tell everyone,
ANNOUNCE.

See also: IMMTALK, GODTALK, ANNOUNCE, WIZINVIS
~

67 NONOTE~
Syntax: nonote <character>

This command completely removes target character's noting privileges.
This is to be used only as a last resort for players that have
consistently abused their note privilege.

See also: NOTE, WIZHELP
~

0 SAY~
Syntax: say <message>
        '<message>

Speaks to everyone in your room; only they hear it.  The apostrophe is a
shortcut: 'Hello is the same as SAY Hello.  Use TELL to speak to one
person anywhere, GTELL for your group, and the channels for everyone.

See also: TELL, EMOTE, CHANNELS, HISTORY
~

0 ; GTELL~
Syntax: ;<message>
Syntax: gtell <message>

The semicolon (;) is a shortcut for the GTELL command.  Typing:
   ;Careful, there's a trap ahead.
is identical to:
   gtell Careful, there's a trap ahead.

GTELL sends a message privately to all members of your current group,
regardless of where they are in the world.  Use GROUP to see who is in your
party.  Note that you must have no space between the semicolon and your
message.

See also: TELL, GOSSIP, GROUP
~

64 IPORTAL~
Syntax: iportal <target> [timer] [description]

IPORTAL creates an immortal portal in your current room leading to the
specified destination.  The target can be either a room vnum or the name
of a mob/player currently online.

Options:
  timer        - Number of ticks before the portal disappears.  Use 0 for
                 permanent.  Default is 0.
  description  - Custom description for the portal.  If omitted, the portal
                 appears as "(Shimmering) <default portal text>".

Examples:
  iportal 3001               - Permanent portal to room 3001
  iportal 3001 10            - Portal to room 3001 lasting 10 ticks
  iportal Brak 0 A swirling vortex of energy crackles here.

The portal carries 10000 charges and is not locked to you, so anyone who
finds it can use it.  Treat it as a door you are leaving open in whatever
room you drop it in.

To remove one: SMASH clears every object in the room and leaves the mobs
alone, or PURGE <portal> removes just that one.

See also: SMASH, PURGE, GATE, GOTO, TRANSFER, RECALL
~

69 @ GODTALK~
Syntax: godtalk <message>
        godtalk
        @<message>

The channel for trust 69 and above, marked with your trust like IMMTALK.
GODTALK alone turns it off or on for you; @ is a shortcut.

  @Anyone around for a reboot?

See also: IMMTALK, HERO, WIZINFO
~

70 DUMP_EXITS~
Syntax: dump_exits

DUMP_EXITS writes a complete dump of all room exits in the game to the
file "exits.dmp" in the area directory.  This is an IMP-only diagnostic
command used for debugging area connectivity or verifying exit data.

The dump includes source room vnum, destination vnum, and exit direction
for every exit defined in the loaded areas.

See also: AREAVNUMS, STAT, VNUM
~

64 ANNOUNCE~
Syntax: announce <message>

ANNOUNCE broadcasts a highlighted global message to every player online.
The message is framed with a colored banner so it stands out from regular
game text.  Use it for server-wide news, events, or important notices.

See also: WIZINFO, GECHO
~

63 REPOP~
Syntax: repop

REPOP forces an immediate reset of the area you are currently standing in.
It is equivalent to the area aging out naturally, respawning all mobs and
objects according to the area's reset data.  The area age is randomized
after the reset so the normal timer continues from a low value.

Useful for testing area resets or quickly restocking a cleared zone.

See also: LOAD, VNUM
~

64 STASIS~
Syntax: stasis <player>

STASIS is a toggle that locks a player in their current room.  While in
stasis the player can still communicate (say, tell, chat, etc.) but cannot
move, flee, or recall.  Toggling stasis again releases them.  The flag is
saved across logins.

See also: FREEZE, JAIL
~

65 SMASH~
Syntax: smash

SMASH removes all purgeable objects from the current room, leaving mobs
intact.  Objects with the NOPURGE flag (pits, fountains, etc.) are
unaffected.  A count of removed items is echoed back to the immortal.

See also: PURGE
~

62 RENAME~
Syntax: rename <player> <new_name>

RENAME changes an online player's character name and renames their save
file on disk accordingly.  The new name must be 2-12 alphabetic characters.
The player is notified of the change.  Immortals of equal or higher trust
cannot be renamed.

Use with caution - the old player file is deleted and replaced with a new
one under the new name immediately.

See also: RESETPWD, PRESTORE
~

63 MUTE~
Syntax: mute <player>

MUTE silences a player completely - they cannot use say, tell, shout,
channels, or emote until the immortal toggles the mute off.  All
communication flags are set simultaneously.  Toggling mute again on a
muted player restores all speech privileges.  The flag is saved across
logins.

See also: NOCHANNELS, NOTELL, NOSHOUT, NOEMOTE
~

64 DRAG~
Syntax: drag <player>

DRAG instantly pulls an online player from wherever they are to your
current room, as if hauled by an unseen force.  Any active combat the
victim is engaged in is stopped first.  The dragged player sees a
notification and an automatic room description.

See also: TRANSFER
~

64 DUEL~
Syntax: duel <player1> <player2>

DUEL forces two online players into combat against each other.  Player2
is transported to player1's room; both are set PK-enabled and their
fighting is started automatically.  A global notification is sent to both
participants and to you.

Use responsibly - this will force real combat between the two players.

See also: SLAY, PEACE
~

63 IWEATHER~
Syntax: iweather
        iweather sunny | cloudy | rain | storm

Sets the weather at once.  Staff only (trust 63).  IWEATHER alone shows
the current sky.  Each takes a start of its name, or of the word beside
it:

  sunny  (clear)      clear sky, pressure 1010
  cloudy (overcast)   clouds, pressure 990
  rain   (raining)    rain, pressure 970
  storm  (lightning, thunder)   lightning, pressure 950

Everyone playing, indoors or out, is told the change ("The sky erupts
with lightning and thunder!").  The weather then drifts from there as it
always does.  Players read it with WEATHER.

See also: WEATHER, LIGHTS
~

63 LIGHTS~
Syntax: lights

LIGHTS toggles the ROOM_DARK flag on your current room by reaching over
and flipping the proverbial light switch.  *CLICK*  When dark, mobs and
players without infravision or a light source cannot see clearly.
Flip it again to restore the room's lighting.  Let there be light!

See also: SEAL
~

63 SEAL~
Syntax: seal <direction>

Toggles a wizard lock on that exit of your room.  Staff only (trust 63).
A character below level 68 cannot pass it, even with pass door; level
68 and above step through.  Only this side of the
exit is sealed: seal the way back from the other room too.

Directions: north, east, south, west, up, down, northeast, northwest,
southeast, southwest, a start of one (n, sou, northe), or the short
forms NE, NW, SE and SW, as you would walk them.

See also: LIGHTS, GOTO
~

63 FINGER~
Syntax: finger <player>

FINGER retrieves information about a player.  If the player is online, their
live stats (level, race, class, title, HP/mana/move, room, playing time) are
shown.  If offline, the player file is scanned for level, race, class, last
login, and total playing time.

See also: ACTIVITY, PSTAT, WHOIS
~

63 ACTIVITY~
Syntax: activity <player>

ACTIVITY shows session statistics for a player.

If the player is online, live data for the current session is displayed:
  - Session login time and duration
  - Current level and experience (with session gains)
  - Mob kills, PK kills, deaths, and quests completed this session

If the player is offline, the last recorded session snapshot is shown with
the same fields from the moment they logged out.

Session data is tracked automatically from login to logout and written to
the player file on quit.

See also: FINGER, TRAIL, PSTAT
~

65 PRESTORE~
Syntax: prestore <player>                    the newest 30 snapshots
        prestore <player> all [page]         every snapshot, 50 a page
        prestore <player> deaths [page]      one kind: deaths, remorts,
                                             levels, quests, restores,
                                             milestones
        prestore <player> <stamp> show       what it holds, nothing changed
        prestore <player> <stamp>            the whole character
        prestore <player> <stamp> gear [force]   only their gear, back
        prestore <player> <stamp> stash      only their stash, added back
        prestore <player> corpses            their corpses, kept or ended
        prestore <player> corpse <id> show   what one held
        prestore <player> corpse <id> gear [force]   that, back to them

Every save of a player is a snapshot (at most one a minute): kept every
one for a day, the newest of each hour for a week and of each day for a
year.  A copy is also taken before every death, remort, deletion, staff
change and restore, and after every level gain and quest; those are kept
ninety days, and the newest hundred of each kind for good.  A death killed
by a mobile has two: "dying, before the death's experience penalty" and
"before death", which already carries the penalty.  Both are taken as the
blow lands, so a character restored to one stands up at one hit point in
the Temple rather than where they fell.

The list shows how long ago each was taken, then the time, the level, the
kind, and the stamp.  Restore by the stamp, not the number: every save adds
a snapshot at the top and moves the numbers down.  A unique prefix of the
stamp is enough; add its kind (20261023_023244.death) when an ordinary save
was taken the same second.  A second copy of one kind in the same second
has _02 after its stamp.

SHOW lists what the snapshot wore, carried and stashed.  The whole form puts
the character back as they were (an online player is saved and
disconnected first), keeping their password, rank and any jail sentence
as they stand now.  GEAR puts back only what they wore and carried, into
their inventory; STASH only the stash.  Both work online or offline and
change nothing else.  GEAR waits while the player's own corpse still holds
things -- they can fetch it -- unless you add FORCE.

A player's corpse lasts a week, is written down as it is after every
change, and comes back exactly after a restart.  When it ends -- rotted,
picked up, purged -- what it held is kept.  CORPSE <id> GEAR gives that
back once; a second time, or while it is still in the world, needs FORCE.

Every restore takes a copy of the character first, so it can be undone,
and changes nothing if that copy cannot be taken; it is written in a
ledger no restore rolls back, so a restore asked for through git never
runs twice.  A full restore keeps their current password, trust, rank,
login fixes and one-time claims.  See wiki/player-backups.md.
~

63 TRAIL~
Syntax: trail <player>

TRAIL shows the last few rooms visited by the specified player, taken from
their session ring-buffer.  The buffer holds the TRAIL_LEN most recent
rooms and is updated every time the player moves.  Entries are shown
oldest-first with room vnum and name.  The buffer is cleared on login.

See also: FINGER, PSTAT
~

63 RESETPWD~
Syntax: resetpwd <player> <new password>
        resetpwd <player> '<new password with spaces>'

Sets a new password for a player who is not logged in, for one who has
forgotten theirs.  Staff only (trust 63).

  - The player must be offline and not at the login prompts; one who is
    playing changes it with PASSWORD.
  - At least five characters, and no tilde.  Case counts.
  - Never on anyone who outranks you; below trust 70, not on your own
    trust either.  Implementors may reset each other.
  - Only the password line of the save file is rewritten.  It is stored
    as a SHA-512 crypt hash, counting every character.  An old password
    moved from DES keeps DES's eight-character limit until it is changed.
  - The password is never logged and a snooper sees an empty line; the
    reset itself is logged and announced on WIZINFO.

The game connection is plain Telnet: a password crosses it unencrypted,
and the save file holds a hash, not an encrypted copy.

See also: PASSWORD, FINGER
~

60 NEWBIE~
Syntax: newbie
        newbie <player>

NEWBIE builds a starter kit and hands it over.  With no argument the pack
appears in your own hands; with a player name it is given to them, with a
message to the recipient and to everyone in the room.

The pack is a renamed container holding a mud school diploma, red potions,
food and berries, potions of sanctuary and extra healing, a water jug, and
an assortment of starter jewellery.  Its contents are defined in the server
rather than in an area file, so they are the same every time regardless of
what is loaded in the world.

If an item has been renumbered out of the world the command still hands over
the pack and warns you how many pieces were missing, rather than failing.

The gift is logged and announced on WIZINFO.

See also: LOAD, TRANSFER
~

63 PETRIFY~
Syntax: petrify <player> [duration]

PETRIFY applies a stone affect to a player, completely blocking all commands
for the duration (in ticks).  Default duration is 50 ticks.  Petrified
players cannot act at all.  Toggling petrify on an already-petrified player
removes the effect immediately.  The effect is saved across logins.

See also: FREEZE, STASIS
~

66 EMPOWER~
Syntax: empower <player>
        empower <player> <ticks>
        empower <player> perm
        empower <player> toggle

EMPOWER grants a player a full suite of divine buffs: sanctuary, haste,
fly, pass door, protection, regeneration, divine shielding, and large
boosts to all five stats plus hitroll, damroll, and armor class, and
50% more experience for as long as it lasts.

  empower bob           -- permanent (duration -1); default when no arg given
  empower bob 200       -- applies for 200 ticks; expires naturally
  empower bob perm      -- explicit permanent
  empower bob toggle    -- permanent; if target is already empowered,
                           removes all empower effects immediately instead

If the target is already empowered, a second empower call (any argument)
strips the effects and acts as a toggle-off regardless of duration given.

All effects use the 'empower' skill type and are removed atomically.

For what a crafted EMPOWER does, HELP EMPOWER SPELL.

See also: TITANIC, RESTORE
~

66 TITANIC~
Syntax: titanic <player>
        titanic <player> <ticks>
        titanic <player> perm
        titanic <player> toggle

TITANIC makes a player's maximum HP, mana, and move five times what they
were (capped at 1,000,000,000 each), then heals them to full immediately.
Also grants +50% bonus experience for the duration.

  titanic bob          -- permanent (duration -1); default when no arg given
  titanic bob 300      -- 300-tick duration
  titanic bob perm     -- explicit permanent
  titanic bob toggle   -- permanent; if already titanic, strips and
                          removes the boost instead

If the target is already under titanic, calling again strips it
regardless of arguments.  Health, mana, and move are clamped back to
new (reduced) max on removal.

Boosts are applied as affects under the 'titanic' skill type.

For what a crafted TITANIC does, HELP TITANIC SPELL.

See also: EMPOWER, RESTORE
~

65 SUMMONEVENT~
Syntax: summonevent
        summonevent status

SUMMONEVENT manually spawns (or despawns) the current seasonal event boss
in Dresden's Oak Tree / Christmas Tree Square (room 2401).

  summonevent          -- if no boss is alive, spawn it; if alive, despawn it
  summonevent status   -- show active season and boss state without acting

The boss spawns automatically once every 48 hours during active seasonal
events (Hallows End: Oct 18-Nov 1; Winter Veil: Dec 16-Jan 6).  It
broadcasts a global shout on arrival, periodically yells taunts, and
auto-despawns after two real hours if not killed.

Event mobs:
  the Headless Horseman (vnum 29910) -- Hallows End boss, undead, level 52
  Father Winter          (vnum 29911) -- Winter Veil boss, human, level 50

Rare drops on kill:
  the Headless Horseman's Reaper (29920) -- two-handed vorpal sword
  the Horseman's cursed helm (29921)     -- powerful head armor
  Frostbite (29922)                      -- frost warhammer
  the Winter Veil mantle (29923)         -- powerful cloak armor
  a pumpkin-lantern charm (29924)        -- neck trinket (mana/move)
  a snowflake pendant (29925)            -- neck trinket (mana/hp)
  a piece of shadowed horseman's cake (29926) -- VERY RARE: +1 train on eat
  a slice of Winter Veil yule log (29927)     -- VERY RARE: +1 train on eat

This command requires Level 5 immortal or higher.

See also: TITANIC, EMPOWER, PETRIFY
~

65 FLING~
Syntax: fling <player>
        fling all

FLING hurls the target player (or all players) into the sky and drops
them in a completely random room somewhere in the world.  The target sees
an appropriately dramatic message and lands with a room description.

See also: TRANSFER, DRAG
~

69 GKICK~
Syntax: gkick <player> <direction>

Kicks a player standing in your room out through an exit: they RUN that
way, as far as RUN would carry them.  Trust 69.

  - The target must be a player in your room, and not you.  Nobody
    whose trust is at least yours (implementors may kick each other),
    and never a prisoner out of the cell: release them with JAIL first.
  - The direction is any start of one (n, sou, ne) and your room must
    have an exit there.
  - The room sees "Zog kicks Bob full on his butt!" and Bob go screaming
    out trailing fire.

See also: RUN, DRAG, TRANSFER
~

62 INVIS~
Syntax: invis [level]

INVIS is WIZINVIS under another name: see HELP WIZINVIS.  VISIBLE strips
spells and hiding, not this.  For the INVIS spell, HELP INVISSPELL.

See also: WIZINVIS, HOLYLIGHT
~

68 ITRANS~
Syntax: itrans <player> [room]
        itrans all [room]

ITRANS performs a silent transfer - the target is moved without seeing
the usual transfer arrival or departure messages.  Room may be a vnum or
a player name for their current location.  Omit room to transfer to your
own position.

See also: TRANSFER, DRAG
~

62 JAIL~
Syntax: jail <player> status
        jail <player> <duration>    30m, 2h, 7d, or a bare number of days
        jail <player> indefinite
        jail <player> release       (or: jail <player> 0)

JAIL puts a player in the jail cell (room 3) and keeps them there.  It
works on players online, link-dead or offline, and saves at once: if the
save fails, nothing changes and you are told.

A timed sentence runs from now, at most 30 days, and replaces any term
already being served.  When it runs out, or staff release them, they
go to the Temple: at once if online, and at their next login if not.
A pet standing with them in the cell goes with them, and a mount they
are riding stays under them.  INDEFINITE lasts until staff release them.
STATUS shows the release time, what is left, who set it and what it
imposed.

Nothing else ends a sentence: death, recall, portals, spells, items, the
Oracle, remort, DELETE, quitting, link loss and reboots all leave them in
the cell, and TRANSFER refuses a prisoner.  A sentence turns off channels
and titles; release gives back only what the sentence itself took.

See also: TRANSFER, STASIS
~

64 KSOCK~
Syntax: ksock <descriptor>

KSOCK forcibly closes a network connection by descriptor number.
Use SOCKETS to view current descriptor numbers.

See also: SOCKETS, DUMP
~

63 NOCHANNELS NOCHANNEL~
Syntax: nochannels <player>

Toggles a player's channel ban.  While it is on they cannot talk on
gossip, question, music, leveling, hero, castle chat, CGOS or the staff
channels; they can still SAY, EMOTE, TELL, SHOUT and YELL.  They are told
either way.  MUTE is the heavier answer.

See also: MUTE, NOSHOUT, NOEMOTE, NOTELL
~

64 NOEMOTE~
Syntax: noemote <player>

NOEMOTE prevents a player from using the emote command.
Toggle again to restore.

See also: MUTE, NOCHANNELS
~

64 NOSHOUT~
Syntax: noshout <player>

NOSHOUT prevents a player from using the shout command.
Toggle again to restore.

See also: MUTE, NOCHANNELS
~

65 NOTELL~
Syntax: notell <player>

NOTELL stops one player sending tells, and typing it again lets them send
again.  Use it when somebody is pestering one person; MUTE takes away every
channel at once and is the heavier answer.

The player is told either way, and the change is announced on WIZINFO.

See also: MUTE, TELL
~

65 NOTITLE~
Syntax: notitle <player>

NOTITLE prevents a player from changing their own title.
Toggle again to allow title changes.

See also: TITLE, MUTE
~

66 SLA~
Syntax: sla

A guard against a typo: SLA does nothing but say "If you want to SLAY,
spell it out."  Type SLAY in full.

See also: SLAY
~

69 DIAGNOSTICS~
Syntax: diagnostics

Prints the server's state at a glance: when it booted; how many areas,
rooms, mobile and object prototypes it loaded; how many connections,
characters, objects and active mobiles exist now; and when the next player
file backup and the next daily backup are due.  It changes nothing.

See also: MEMORY, SOCKETS
~

62 WARN~
Syntax: warn <player>

Toggles the WARNED flag on a player.  Staff only (trust 62).  Players
only: a mobile is refused.  Anything after the name is ignored: there is
no message to send.

  - Setting it tells the player "You have been warned about breaking the
    rules." and everyone on INFO "<name> has been warned for breaking the
    rules!"
  - It also sets LOG on them, if it was off: every command is recorded in
    full, tells included (HELP LOG).  Lifting the warning -- WARN again,
    or PARDON <player> WARNED -- lifts the LOG the warning turned on, and
    only that: a LOG set with LOG <name>, before or during the warning,
    stays.
  - Never on anyone who outranks you.

See also: LOG, PARDON, FREEZE
~

67 WHINER WHINE~
Syntax: whiner <player>

Toggles the WHINER flag.  A flagged player shows (WHINER) in WHO and
WHINER on their SCORE, and city guards taunt them.  It changes nothing
else: their channels and notes work as before.  They are told either
way.

See also: NOCHANNELS, NONOTE
~

62 WIZCHECK~
Syntax: wizcheck
        wizcheck <player>

WIZCHECK with no argument lists all immortals currently online with their
invisibility level and room vnum.  With a player name, shows extended
status info for that character.

See also: WIZLIST
~

0 TRAITOR~
A character marked (TRAITOR) has been flagged by the staff for betraying
the game or its players.  Guildmasters and trainers refuse to teach a
traitor, and city guards treat one as they treat the WANTED.  The flag is
set and lifted by the staff alone; if you believe yours is a mistake,
send a NOTE to immortal.

See also: WANTED, NOTE
~

0 ALIGNMENT ALIGN GOOD EVIL NEUTRAL DEVOTE PARAGON VIRTUOUS RUTHLESS VILE FIEND~
Syntax: alignment
        devote light | dark | balance        at the Temple or its altar
        devote preview [light | dark | balance]

Alignment runs from -1000 to 1000 in seven tiers:

  Fiend  Vile  Ruthless | Balanced | Kind  Virtuous  Paragon
  -900   -550    -300      -299..299   300    550       900

Kind and brighter are good (the light), Ruthless and darker evil (the
dark).  ALIGNMENT shows your tier, how far the next is, what yours gives
you and when you may next DEVOTE; SCORE names your tier.

What moves it -- you are what you slay:
  - Slaying the evil moves you toward the light, the good toward the
    dark: a point per hundred of the victim's alignment, half for a foe
    eight levels below you, nothing fifteen below.  The neutral move
    nothing.
  - A quest target moves you 25 further; the questmaster says which way.
  - DEVOTE moves you 100 toward the light, the dark or the middle, for an
    offering worth twenty kills of your level, once every thirty minutes.
    DEVOTE PREVIEW, free and from anywhere, shows where it would leave
    you, the offering, the wait, and any worn piece that would zap off.

You are told when you change tier; DAMAGENUMBERS shows each small move.

See also: 'ALIGNMENT PERKS', SCORE, 'KNOW ALIGNMENT'
~

0 'ALIGNMENT PERKS'~
What your alignment gives, more for each tier further from the middle:

  - Light: hit point regeneration +5/10/15%, holy damage +5/10/15%.
  - Dark: weapon blows drain 2/4/6% of their damage as hit points,
    negative damage +5/10/15%.
  - Balanced: holy and negative damage against you -15%.
  - Experience: +10% a tier for slaying the other side; balanced, +10%
    for slaying either.  No kill pays less for its alignment.
  - Shops: a keeper of your side charges up to 10% less, never below
    worth; one of the other side charges 10% more and pays 10% less.
    Neutral keepers, balanced customers and the black market keep to the
    list price.
  - Gear marked anti-good, anti-evil or anti-neutral will not be worn by
    that side, and zaps off if you cross over; IDENTIFY says who may
    wear it.
  - Spells: KNOW ALIGNMENT names a tier; DETECT GOOD and DETECT EVIL mark
    the sides; DISPEL GOOD, DISPEL EVIL and PROTECTION EVIL bite by side.

See also: ALIGNMENT, 'DETECT EVIL', 'DETECT GOOD'
~

0 CLASS CLASSES MULTICLASS~
Six classes are played.  Each has a help entry of its own:

  WARRIOR      strength, heavy weapons and armour; the front line
  THIEF        dexterity, daggers, stealth and backstab
  MAGE         intelligence, offensive and utility magic
  CLERIC       wisdom, healing and protective magic
  MONK         hand to hand, nerve strikes and discipline
  NECROMANCER  the dead raised to fight for you

You choose a class when you make a character, and change it only at a
REMORT: each life must be a class you have not lived, until the fifth
remort, which may choose any ('REMORT CLASSES').  A guild is a separate
choice (HELP GUILDS).

There is no multiclassing.  A class in another class's guild is the
nearest thing: a warrior in the thieves' guild (W/T) learns from both,
while a full warrior (W/W) gets what only the full class gets, such as
third attack.

See also: GUILDS, REMORT, RACES, ATTRIBUTES
~

0 WANTED KILLER~
A player marked (WANTED) on WHO has attacked another player, or been
caught stealing from one.  KILLER is the flag's old name.

You become WANTED by:
  - attacking a player who is not WANTED, outside an arena (an attack by
    your charmed follower counts as yours);
  - being caught trying to STEAL from a player;
  - reading a Scroll of Farslay.

While you are WANTED:
  - city guards and executioners who see you attack you;
  - the level range no longer shields you: a pkiller may attack you
    with KILL, though both of you must still be pkillers;
  - your corpse has no owner, so anyone may loot it.

It never wears off.  It is lifted when a player who is not WANTED kills
you, at a REMORT, or by the staff with PARDON.

See also: TRAITOR, PKILL, STEAL, 'SCROLL OF FARSLAY', DYING
~

0 TRAPS TRAP~
A trap sits on a locked door or a locked container and goes off as you
work the lock.  HELP TRAP THE SOUL is the spell.

  - Some areas lay traps when they reset, and now and then a locked door,
    or a locked chest nobody is near, grows one.  A trapped lock can be
    picked even if it was pickproof.
  - PICK on a trapped lock sets it off.  UNLOCK with the key sets it off
    four times in five; otherwise the trap is spent and the lock opens.
  - SEARCH <door or container> looks for a trap on a locked lock and, when
    it succeeds, disarms it.  It needs the SEARCH skill, takes a moment,
    is refused while a mobile more than five levels above you watches, and
    a failed search can set the trap off.

What goes off: fire (half your hit points, or everyone's in the room),
gas that spreads, a drunken mist, sleep, teleport (a random room, with
your hit points, mana and moves at 10), poison darts, a summoned
guardian, a lightning stun, plague, or a blinding flash.  The room sees
it, and INFO tells everyone.

Rooms can be hazards too -- a stinking cloud, a burning floor -- and some
are death traps.  SEARCH does not clear those.

See also: SEARCH, PICK, UNLOCK, DEATHTRAP
~

0 PET PETS~
Syntax: list                 (in a pet shop)
        buy <pet> [name]
        order <pet | all> <command>

A pet is a companion bought in a pet shop.  It follows you, groups with
you and fights beside you.

  - It costs ten gold times its level squared, less what HAGGLE takes
    off, and you must be at least its level.
  - One companion at a time: a pet or a mount (RIDE), not both.  If it
    dies, you may buy another.
  - A word after the pet's name is a name it also answers to: BUY DOG REX.
  - ORDER tells it what to do (ORDER ALL tells every charmed follower in
    the room).  It will not attack a player you could not attack.
  - It is saved with you if it is in your room, carrying nothing (a mount
    may wear its saddle), when you save or quit; a REMORT takes it to the
    Temple with you.

See also: ORDER, RIDE, GROUP, BUY
~

0 IMMUNE IMMUNITY RESISTANT RESISTANCE VULNERABLE VULNERABILITY~
Some characters shrug off a kind of damage, and some suffer from it:

  immune       none of it lands ("is unaffected by")
  resistant    a third less
  vulnerable   half as much again

The kinds are the weapon blows (bash, pierce, slash) and the elements and
powers (fire, cold, lightning, acid, poison, negative, holy, energy,
mental, disease, drowning, light, wind), with two broad ones over them:
WEAPON for the three blows and MAGIC for the rest.  A specific kind wins
over a broad one: resistant to magic but vulnerable to fire takes extra
fire damage.

They come from race (HELP RACES), from monsters' own make-up, and from
affects: IRON SKIN and some monsters' wards grant an immunity, and AFFECTS
names what one turns.  Immunity to summon or charm stops those spells.

See also: RACES, AFFECTS, 'IRON SKIN', DAMAGE
~

0 $~

#$
